Use Windows.pkg
Use cFTModalPanel.pkg         // FT: the dark modal, same base as SelectCopyScopeFT.dg
Use cFTDeferTimer.pkg            // oMLDeferTimer - the dialog's own deferred work, off the WebView2 pump
Use RefactorConstants.h.pkg
Use cFTGridHost.pkg              // FT: the dialog's one host - a grid of 8 px columns, the page over a row of buttons (RDCFlexTron)
Use FTAllWebControlClasses.pkg   // FT: cFTWebButton - the button row
Use cFTHtmlPage.pkg              // FT: the list's page - its title (psLabel) and the glyph beside it
Use cLibraryInventory.pkg        // the reader AND the writer for a .sws [Libraries] section
Use FTMarkupBuilders.pkg         // the HTML vocabulary - OpenHtml, IconLabel, the builders
Use FTThemeColors.pkg            // FTWebBg / FTCJTheme - the FT palette
Use RefactorFileIO.pkg           // rfOpenOutput / rfCloseOutput - channel file I/O
Use FTMessageBox.pkg             // FTStopBox / FTYesNoBox / FTHtmlMessage (RDCFlexTron)
Use FTRefactorMessageBox.pkg     // ShowPicker / ghoBrowsePackages - declared early
Use cShadowedPackageFiles.pkg    // RetirableLibraryFolder - which folder an OK'd removal orphans
Use cDfCliPackages.pkg           // df-cli package queries
Use cEncodingHelpers.pkg         // rfHtmlEscape and the encoding helpers
/* ghoLibraryReport - the library DATA this screen renders: the compiler-message tally, the
   dependency scan and its cell renderer, the version-list text, the reveal links.

   DECLARED HERE, not merely relied upon. cFTLibraryReport in turn needs ghoModAnalyzerDock
   (OutputDockFT.dg) and DashIcon (FTDashIcons.pkg), so this file has to sit AFTER those in the
   compile stream - and stating the dependency means moving this .dg too early fails loudly at
   compile instead of quietly losing a column. Include-once, so the .src ordering still governs. */
Use cFTLibraryReport.pkg
Use RDCRunProgram.pkg             // RDCOpenUrl / RDCRevealInExplorer - the row links
Use cRDCAsyncProcess.pkg            // RDCRunProgramBounded - df-cli convert, which can PROMPT
Use AddPackageFT.dg              // PromptForPackage - the one field that takes all three forms
Use File_dlg.pkg

/*  LIBRARY MAINTENANCE - the [Libraries] section of one workspace .sws, as a list you can edit.

    WHAT IT SHOWS IS EXACTLY WHAT THE FILE SAYS (Nils 2026-08-11). One row per [Libraries] entry, in
    file order, with the path written out as the .sws actually holds it. Nothing is added, nothing is
    hidden, and no row carries a verdict - this is the same starting point the Studio's own Maintain
    Libraries gives, and it is deliberately the whole of the first version. Bringing a library
    forward for a new DataFlex version is then the user's decision, made with the two facts that
    decide it in front of them: which build each entry points at, and which version that build is.

    WHY IT SITS IN THE MIGRATION FLOW. A copied workspace has just had its [Libraries] entries
    retargeted by NewSwsLines and, where a library ships no build for the target, a header
    synthesised for it. That is the moment the list is both most likely to need a hand and least
    likely to be looked at - so the copy shows it, once, before it offers to open the copy. Adding a
    newer library and dropping one the old version needed is precisely the hand-work the copy cannot
    do for the user, and it is far cheaper here than after the first failed compile.

    ROW EVIDENCE, not row judgement. Two facts are read off disk per row because they are properties
    of the entry rather than opinions about it: whether the referenced .sws is THERE at all, and what
    version it declares for itself ([Properties] Version=, never the filename - four incompatible
    naming schemes were found across four libraries in the 2026-07-30 survey). A missing file is the
    one state the row cannot be silent about: an entry pointing at nothing is the most likely damage
    after a copy, and WorkspaceLibraryInventory drops such entries entirely, so this dialog is the
    only place it can be seen.

    NOTHING IS WRITTEN UNTIL OK. Add and Remove edit an in-memory list; Cancel discards it. That is
    what lets both act immediately without a confirmation of their own - the commit point is a single
    button, and it is the only one that touches the file. OK rewrites just the [Libraries] section and
    takes NO backup - see SaveEntries for why a copy in DFRefactor_Backup was a hazard, not a net.

    AN HTML BODY, not a CodeJock grid. The list is short, needs no sorting and no checkboxes, and the
    one thing it must do - highlight the row Remove will act on - is a CSS class. It also keeps the
    dialog clear of the COM lifetime rules a cCJGrid brings (error 4399 when touched before the
    control exists, LoadData-timing, and a positional delete that removes the wrong row once the user
    has sorted). Same shape as ModProgressHistoryFT.dg.

    A PAGE OVER A ROW OF BUTTONS (2026-09-30), built as ModProgressHistoryFT.dg and the library's message
    box are: one grid host, which cFTModalPanel fits to the panel (phoClientHost); the list in a cFTHtmlPage,
    whose title is the old heading; and the footer's eight buttons as framework buttons (cFTWebButton) under
    it. A button's work runs in its OnButtonClick, after the web click has returned, so the timer below
    serves the page's own clickables only - the cards' chips, the banner's Convert, a row - and the steps
    that follow a conversion. RefreshButtons sets the buttons' states on every repaint, where BodyHtml drew
    them.
*/

// Deferred-action codes for oMLDeferTimer - the dialog's own work on a native tick: the repaint a Popup arms,
// the conversion offer after the close, and the convert-then-resume chain. 1, 3, 4, 7 and 18 - Add, OK,
// Cancel, Find usages, Update Check - were the button bar's, framework buttons since 2026-09-30 that run in
// their OnButtonClick; 2, 6, 9, 11-14, 17 and 19 were the page's clicks, in oMLPage's OnHtmlClick since
// 2026-10-07. The numbers are not reused.
Define CI_MLDeferRefresh for 5
Define CI_MLDeferAddPkg  for 8

/* THE CARD BADGE, in three named pieces. The two styles are markup rather than CSS on purpose -
   the reason lives in _BadgeHtml. Keep the 32px in step with .ml-cg in libraries.css. */
Define CS_LibBadgeBoxStyle for "background:transparent;width:32px;height:32px;flex:none;"
Define CS_LibBadgeImgStyle for "width:100%;height:100%;object-fit:contain;border-radius:4px;"
Define CS_LibBadgeOnError  for "var p=this.parentNode;p.removeChild(this);p.style.background=&quot;&quot;;p.textContent=p.dataset.i;"
Define CI_MLDeferSearchPkg for 10
Define CI_MLDeferConvertResume   for 15   // convert an INI workspace, then carry on with piResumeAfterConvert
Define CI_MLDeferUnresolvedOffer for 16   // the conversion failed: offer to drop the entries df-cli could not resolve
/*  UpdChk and PkgUpd were 15 and 16 - the SAME numbers as ConvertResume and UnresolvedOffer
    above (found 2026-09-10). OnTimer dispatches on a chain of independent If statements, not a
    Case, so BOTH branches ran: Update Check also called DoConvertThenResume, and a row Update
    also called DoOfferUnresolvedFix.

    It looked harmless only because every workspace it was used on was already JSON, where
    ConvertSwsToJson returns True at once. On an INI workspace, pressing a button labelled
    "check" would have CONVERTED the .sws without asking, and on a failed conversion gone on to
    offer deleting library entries. Nothing about that is a check.

    Hand-numbered Defines are what allowed it. The trail's equivalents are an Enum_List for
    exactly this reason; these stay Defines only because two of them are referenced from inside
    a #IF, but a NEW code goes at the end of this list and nowhere else. */
Define CI_MLDeferAutoUpdChk for 20   // the dialog opened on a workspace already known to be behind
Define CI_MLDeferConvertOffer for 21 // the dialog has CLOSED on an INI workspace: offer the conversion (once)
Define CI_MLDeferRetryInstall for 22 // a leftover lock entry was dropped: install the package again (sParam)

/* How far down the library chain the dialog walks. Two levels is what real workspaces show
   (E:\WinServ: workspace -> WebAppSrc -> seven leaves, none of which declares any); the cap is a
   backstop against a pathological configuration, not a limit anyone should meet. LibraryEntriesTree
   also refuses to revisit a .sws, so a cycle terminates regardless of this. */
Define CI_MLMaxChainDepth for 4

Object oMaintainLibraries_dg is a cFTModalPanel
    Set Label to "Libraries"
    // Six columns now that the verdict and the version lists live here too - the report's content
    // and the editor's controls are one screen (2026-08-11), so it needs the report's width.
    Set Size to 334 790
    Set Location to 2 6
    // 660 wide at least: the button row's fixed columns (976 px) must fit the host (2026-09-30).
    Set piMinSize to 176 660
    Set Icon to "DFRefactor.ico"

    // The .sws being maintained. NOT assumed to be the loaded workspace: the migration flow points
    // this at the freshly written copy, which is not open yet.
    Property String psSwsFile ""
    // The working list - raw [Libraries] values, exactly as they are written in the file. Edited by
    // Add / Remove, written back by OK, thrown away by Cancel.
    Property String[] pasEntries
    // The row Remove acts on, 0-based. -1 = nothing chosen, which is what greys Remove out.
    Property Integer piSelected -1
    // Has the list actually changed? A clean OK closes without touching the file - no backup, no
    // rewrite, no modified timestamp on a workspace file nobody edited.
    /* THE UPDATE CHECK'S RESULT, and the reason the row chips start disabled.

       pbUpdateChecked is False until Update Check runs, and every row's Update chip is dead while
       it is - a chip that offers an upgrade before anything has been checked would be claiming
       knowledge it does not have. pasUpdKey / pasUpdLatest are parallel: the key a row is found by,
       and what is available for it. An entry ABSENT from pasUpdKey after a check means "checked,
       nothing newer"; that is different from "not checked yet" and the two states must not share a
       chip. Cleared by ReloadEntriesFromFile - the file changed, so the answer is stale. */
    Property Boolean  pbUpdateChecked False
    // ONE automatic check per showing, whatever else repaints the body. Reset by Popup, so the
    // next opening asks again - the pins may have moved in between.
    Property Boolean  pbAutoUpdChkDone False
    Property String[] pasUpdKey
    Property String[] pasUpdLatest

    Property Boolean pbDirty False
    /* CONVERT, THEN CARRY ON (Nils 2026-09-03: "what I question is if Add Package ever should be
       disabled, as long as the target DataFlex version is 26"). A package needs the JSON workspace
       format, which is one df-cli step away - not a property of the workspace the user cannot
       change. So Add Package... and Browse Packages... stay live on an INI workspace: the click
       converts first and then continues with the action named here (a CI_MLDefer* value, 0 = the
       banner's plain conversion, which continues with nothing). */
    Property Integer piResumeAfterConvert 0
    // One retry after the entries df-cli could not resolve are removed - never a loop.
    Property Boolean pbConvertRetried False
    // The .sws.lock entry DoRetryInstallWithoutLockPin removes - set just before it is armed.
    Property String psRetryLockKey ""
    Property Boolean pbConfirmed False
    // The .sws the conversion was offered for on closing - once per workspace per session, see
    // DoOfferConvertAfterClose.
    Property String psConvertOfferedWS ""
    /* What the FILE declared when this showing opened - read in Popup, and never refreshed by the
       reloads a conversion or a package action causes. It is the baseline OfferRetireAfterClose
       compares the file with at close: a removal saved mid-session, by Add Package or by the
       conversion to the JSON format, is still a removal made in this showing. */
    Property String[] pasOpenEntries

    Object oOpenLibrarySwsDialog is a OpenDialog
        Set Dialog_Caption to "Select the library workspace file to add"
        Set Filter_String to "DataFlex workspace|*.sws|Any file|*.*"
    End_Object

    /* ABOVE oWebHost, because oWebHost CALLS it (2026-08-14).

       BodyHtml asks the panel for this while the panel is still being constructed - the
       design-time "Set psHtml to (BodyHtml(Self))" runs then - and a function defined further
       down the object is not there yet. The result is err 98 "Invalid message GET__FOLDEROF" at
       STARTUP, before the dialog has ever been opened. Same rule that put HelpHtml above its
       control in AddPackageFT: in DataFlex, define a function above whatever references it.

       The folder holding sFile, WITH its separator. Local because this dialog has no inventory
       object of its own at header-render time, and creating one to ask for a string split would
       be theatre. */
    Function _FolderOf String sFile Returns String
        Integer iPos
        Move (RightPos("\", sFile)) to iPos
        If (iPos > 0) ;
            Function_Return (Left(sFile, iPos))
        Function_Return ""
    End_Function

    /* Is this showing editing the LOADED workspace? "Is that library folder still declared?" is
       answered against the loaded workspace's home - RetirableLibraryFolder is given psHomeTarget,
       GetWorkspaceLibrarySourceFolders resolves from it, and the tile's count is the loaded
       workspace's - so a retire is offered only then. The copy flow opens this dialog on a copy that
       may not be loaded yet, and there "Libraries\X" would resolve against the ORIGINAL's home: the
       one way a retire could rename the wrong workspace's folder.

       ABOVE oWebHost, beside _FolderOf and for the same reason (see there): BodyHtml asks for it
       while oWebHost is still being constructed. Placed further down with the retire code it
       stopped DFRefactorFT at startup - err 98, GET__ISLOADEDWORKSPACE (2026-09-11). */
    Function _IsLoadedWorkspace Returns Boolean
        String sSws sLoaded

        Get psSwsFile to sSws
        Get psSWSFile of ghoApplication to sLoaded
        Move (Lowercase(Trim(sSws))) to sSws
        Move (Lowercase(Trim(sLoaded))) to sLoaded
        Function_Return (sSws <> "" and sSws = sLoaded)
    End_Function

    Object oWebHost is a cFTGridHost
        Set Location to 4 4
        Set Size to 310 782
        Set peAnchors to anNone         // placed by cFTModalPanel (phoClientHost)
        /* Row 0, the page, takes the extra height, and row 1 is 8 px of air above the buttons in row 2. The
           library buttons sit at the left, OK and Cancel at the right, and column 99 between them takes the
           extra width; the page spans every column. */
        Set piColumnCount to 123
        Set psColumnWidths to "99/1fr"
        Set psRowHeights to "0/1fr 1/8px"

        /* A path as HTML TEXT. & and < are escaped because a path is data here, not markup - "R&D"
           is an ordinary folder name and an unescaped & silently truncates what follows it, which is
           the sort of defect that works on every machine except the one that has it. */
        Function Esc String sText Returns String
            Function_Return (rfHtmlEscape(sText))
        End_Function

        /* The whole modal body: header, the entry table, and the two button groups.

           Reads each row's evidence through ONE cLibraryInventory, created and destroyed here. A
           workspace declares a handful of libraries and each row costs one existence test plus a
           short read, so there is nothing to cache - and a cache would have to be invalidated on
           every Add, which is more machinery than the work it saves. */
        /* The name df-cli calls a git dependency - <owner>/<sws stem>, which is how it named
           https://github.com/NilsSve/Library-DUF.git + DUFLibrary26.0.sws as NilsSve/DUFLibrary26.0.
           Derived, and only used as the uninstall key; df-cli config is where the authoritative
           names live if a repository layout ever defeats this. */
        Function _PackageNameOf tSwsDependency Dep Returns String
            String sOwner sStem sSrc
            Integer iPos

            Move (Trim(Dep.sSource)) to sSrc
            Move (Replaces("/", sSrc, "\")) to sSrc
            If (Right(Lowercase(sSrc), 4) = ".git") ;
                Move (Left(sSrc, Length(sSrc) - 4)) to sSrc
            // the repository name is the last segment, its OWNER the one before
            Move (RightPos("\", sSrc)) to iPos
            If (iPos > 0) ;
                Move (Left(sSrc, iPos - 1)) to sSrc
            Move (RightPos("\", sSrc)) to iPos
            If (iPos > 0) ;
                Move (Right(sSrc, (Length(sSrc) - iPos))) to sOwner
            Move (Trim(Dep.sSws)) to sStem
            Move (Replaces("/", sStem, "\")) to sStem
            Move (RightPos("\", sStem)) to iPos
            If (iPos > 0) ;
                Move (Right(sStem, (Length(sStem) - iPos))) to sStem
            If (Right(Lowercase(sStem), 4) = ".sws") ;
                Move (Left(sStem, Length(sStem) - 4)) to sStem
            If (sStem = "") ;
                Function_Return sOwner
            If (sOwner = "") ;
                Function_Return sStem
            Function_Return (sOwner + "/" + sStem)
        End_Function

        /* The key "df-cli package uninstall" matches on - the .sws STEM, nothing else.

           MEASURED, not guessed (2026-08-14), after uninstall failed with "Dependency:
           NilsSve/DUFLibrary26.0 is not part of workspace". Four candidates tried against a
           throwaway copy of a workspace that had the package:

               DUFLibrary26.0                                    removed
               github.com/NilsSve/Library-DUF.git/DUFLibrary26.0 removed
               https://...Library-DUF.git/DUFLibrary26.0.sws     removed
               NilsSve/DUFLibrary26.0                            NOT removed

           The last is what df-cli config REPORTS as the resolved name and what _PackageNameOf
           derives - so the name it shows you is not the name it takes. The stem works, needs no
           url parsing, and is the least there is to get wrong. */
        // The body moved to SwsDependencyKey in cDfCliPackages.pkg (2026-09-10) so the shared update
        // scan can produce keys these rows will match. Kept as a method because both call sites here
        // read better for it, and because a row key and a scan key drifting apart would show up as an
        // Update chip that never lights.
        Function _PackageKeyOf tSwsDependency Dep Returns String
            Function_Return (SwsDependencyKey(Dep))
        End_Function

        /* ONE row's Update chip, in the three states it can be in - and they are THREE, not two.

           not checked yet  -> dead chip, "Run Update Check first". Offering an upgrade before
                               anything has been asked would claim knowledge we do not have.
           checked, current -> a dead chip that SAYS it is current, so a user who checked and found
                               nothing gets an answer instead of an unchanged-looking dialog.
           update available -> a live chip naming what it would move to.

           A GIT row's "available" depends on its pin. A COMMIT pin: the branch head has MOVED PAST
           it - not that a release exists, and the tooltip says exactly that rather than implying a
           numbered release (Nils 2026-08-21). A version TAG pin (2026-09-25, when the RDC libraries
           went to tags): a later release in the same major version has been published.

           An UNKNOWN answer - GitRemoteHead returned "" because git is missing, the network is down,
           or the remote asked for credentials - renders as the "up to date" dead chip and NEVER as an
           update. Guessing "there might be one" would send the user into an upgrade for no reason. */
        Function _UpdateChipHtml String sKey String sPinned Boolean bIsGit Returns String
            String[] asKey asLatest
            String sLatest sTip
            Boolean bChecked
            Integer i iMax

            Get pbUpdateChecked of (oMaintainLibraries_dg(Self)) to bChecked
            If (bChecked = False) ;
                Function_Return (((DivOpenTip("ml-cx ml-cx-off", "Run Update Check first - nothing has been asked yet.")) + 'Update') + CS_DivEnd)

            Get pasUpdKey of (oMaintainLibraries_dg(Self)) to asKey
            Get pasUpdLatest of (oMaintainLibraries_dg(Self)) to asLatest
            Move "" to sLatest
            Move (SizeOfArray(asKey) - 1) to iMax
            For i from 0 to iMax
                If (Uppercase(Trim(asKey[i])) = Uppercase(Trim(sKey))) ;
                    Move (Trim(asLatest[i])) to sLatest
            Loop

            If (sLatest = "") ;
                Function_Return (((DivOpenTip("ml-cx ml-cx-off", "Checked - this is the newest version this workspace can take.")) + 'Up to date') + CS_DivEnd)

            If ((bIsGit = True) and (IsVersionTagPin(sPinned) = True)) ;
                Move ("Version " + sLatest + " is published. Update moves the pin from " + (Trim(sPinned)) + " to " + sLatest + " and installs it; this list is saved first.") to sTip
            Else If (bIsGit = True) ;
                Move ("The branch head has moved past the pinned commit, to " + (Left(sLatest, 12)) + ". These repositories carry commits, not releases, so this means newer commits exist - not that a new release was published.") to sTip
            Else ;
                Move ("Upgrade to " + sLatest + ". df-cli rewrites the dependency and installs it; this list is saved first.") to sTip
            Function_Return ((ClickableHtml("div", "ml-cx ml-cx-u", ('mlpkgupd|' + (Esc(Self, sKey))), "", "Update", ((Esc(Self, sTip))))))
        End_Function

        /* THE OVERRIDE THAT APPLIES TO A CARD, as a ready-made line - or "" when there is none.

           MATCHED ON SEVERAL CANDIDATES, deliberately. The override map is keyed by "dependency
           identifier", and which spelling that is depends on how the dependency was declared - the
           package name, the .sws stem and the raw entry are all plausible, and df-cli accepts more
           than one spelling elsewhere (uninstall takes three). Matching any of them is right when
           the alternative is showing nothing; a false match would need a key that equals an
           unrelated entry, which cannot happen inside one workspace.

           SAID IN AMBER because it contradicts the line above it: the card states what the
           dependency declares, and this states what the build will actually use. */
        Function _OverrideNote tSwsOverride[] aOvr String sName String sStem String sEntry Returns String
            String sKey sTxt
            Integer i iMax

            Move (SizeOfArray(aOvr) - 1) to iMax
            For i from 0 to iMax
                Move (Lowercase(Trim(aOvr[i].sKey))) to sKey
                If (sKey <> "") Begin
                    If (sKey = Lowercase(Trim(sName)) or sKey = Lowercase(Trim(sStem)) or sKey = Lowercase(Trim(sEntry))) Begin
                        Move "" to sTxt
                        If (Trim(aOvr[i].sVersion) <> "") ;
                            Move ("version " + (Esc(Self, Trim(aOvr[i].sVersion)))) to sTxt
                        If (Trim(aOvr[i].sSws) <> "") Begin
                            If (sTxt <> "") ;
                                Move (sTxt + ", ") to sTxt
                            Move (sTxt + "sws " + (Esc(Self, Trim(aOvr[i].sSws)))) to sTxt
                        End
                        If (sTxt = "") ;
                            Move "declared, with nothing changed" to sTxt
                        Function_Return (((DivOpen("ml-ovr")) + 'overridden by this workspace ') + CS_EmDash + ' ' + sTxt + CS_DivEnd)
                    End
                End
            Loop
            Function_Return ""
        End_Function

        /* ONE CARD. Everything the six columns said, on three lines.

           sState arrives wrapped in a <td> because StatusCellHtml builds cells - the wrapper is
           stripped HERE rather than at every call site, so the verdict logic above stays exactly
           as it was when it drove a table. Left(-5) drops the closing tag; the opening one goes
           by finding its ">". */
        /* sVia is the ONLY thing allowed after the version on the meta line, and it exists because
           a transitive row has to say whose it is (Nils 2026-08-21). The line was trimmed to
           "publisher, version, and nothing else" on 2026-08-19 for good reason - what followed was
           redundant - so this is a deliberate re-opening for one fact that is neither redundant nor
           available anywhere else on the card. Direct rows pass "" and are unchanged. */
        /* THREE FACTS A MANIFEST HOLDS AND THE CARD NEVER SHOWED (Nils 2026-08-21, after updating
           vWin32fh on WinServ and having nowhere to see what he had got).

           The card read three keys - description, icon, version - out of the dozen a manifest
           carries. These are the ones worth the space:

             licence        now that all five repos declare one; blank before today, and blank here
                            when a package still does not say.
             DataFlex range df-min / df-max - the fact that decides whether a package suits a
                            workspace at all.
             requires       what THIS package pulls in. The card already says "required by" for a
                            transitive row - the UPWARD view - and never had the downward one, which
                            is precisely what he went looking for.

           DELIBERATELY NOT the Studio's five tabs. Two of them (Description, Version) are already on
           this card, and the Versions tab lists repository TAGS - a network call per package, and
           four of the five repos have none. Re-implementing a dialog the Studio does better, one
           click away, is not where this dialog earns its keep; usages, compiler messages and the
           update check are.

           Returns "" for a package whose manifest is missing or says none of this, so a card with
           nothing to add is not given an empty meta segment. */
        Function _PkgMetaExtras Handle hoInv String sManifest String sWsDf Returns String
            tSwsDependency[] aReq
            String sOut sLic sMin sMax sRange sReq sOne
            Integer i iMax

            If (Trim(sManifest) = "") ;
                Function_Return ""

            Move "" to sOut
            /* AN UNDECLARED LICENCE IS ITSELF WORTH SAYING (Nils 2026-08-21). Silence reads as "we
               did not look"; the Studio meanwhile shows an SPDX NoAssertion link, which is where the
               mystery started this morning. Naming it turns a blank into something actionable for a
               package you own - add "license" to its manifest - and into an honest statement for one
               you do not. */
            Get SwsManifestValue of hoInv sManifest "license" to sLic
            If (Trim(sLic) <> "") ;
                Move (sOut + (((SpanOpenTip("", "Licence declared by the package manifest.")) + (SpanOpen("ml-gl")) + CS_LicenceGlyph) + CS_SpanEnd) + (Esc(Self, Trim(sLic))) + CS_SpanEnd) to sOut
            Else ;
                Move (sOut + (SpanOpenTipHtml("ml-none", ("This package declares no license in its manifest," ;
                    + " so the Studio shows it as SPDX NoAssertionLicense. A package you own is fixed by adding" ;
                    + " a &quot;license&quot; key (an SPDX id such as MIT) to its .sws."))) ;
                    + (SpanOpen("ml-gl")) + CS_LicenceGlyph + CS_SpanEnd ;
                    + 'no licence declared' + CS_SpanEnd) to sOut

            /* THE RANGE ONLY WHEN IT CONSTRAINS SOMETHING (Nils 2026-08-21: "Not much of a help,
               is it?" - and he was right). A package installed in a DF26 workspace obviously supports
               DF26, so "DataFlex 26.0+" on every row is a tautology dressed as information. It earns
               its place in two cases only:

                 - the package declares a CEILING (df-max) - a real fact about a future workspace
                   upgrade, and nothing else on screen says it;
                 - its floor is ABOVE this workspace's DataFlex version, which should not happen and
                   is worth seeing if it does.

               Everything else is suppressed. */
            Get SwsManifestValue of hoInv sManifest "df-min" to sMin
            Get SwsManifestValue of hoInv sManifest "df-max" to sMax
            /* NO String -> Number CONVERSION HERE, deliberately. DataFlex converts through the OS
               LOCALE, and a JSON manifest always writes "26.0" with a dot: on a Swedish machine that
               parses as 26 or 0, not 26.0. The first version of this compared numerically and died
               at RUNTIME with error 52, "Bad format of expression (operator)", the moment the dialog
               opened - compiling perfectly, like every other fault in this family today.

               A STRING comparison answers the only question being asked. The range is suppressed
               when the package's floor is the same version the workspace is, which is the tautology
               worth losing; anything else - a ceiling, or a floor that differs - is shown. No
               ordering is needed, so no number is needed. */
            Move "" to sRange
            If (Trim(sMax) <> "") Begin
                If ((Trim(sMin) <> "") and (Trim(sMin) <> Trim(sMax))) ;
                    Move ("DataFlex " + Trim(sMin) + " - " + Trim(sMax)) to sRange
                Else ;
                    Move ("DataFlex up to " + Trim(sMax)) to sRange
            End
            Else If ((Trim(sMin) <> "") and (Trim(sMin) <> Trim(sWsDf))) ;
                Move ("DataFlex " + Trim(sMin) + "+") to sRange
            If (sRange <> "") ;
                Move (sOut + (((SpanOpenTip("", "The DataFlex versions this package declares support for")) + (SpanOpen("ml-gl")) + CS_GearGlyph) + CS_SpanEnd) + (Esc(Self, sRange)) + CS_SpanEnd) to sOut

            // What this package pulls in - the direction the card never had.
            Get SwsJsonObjectDependencies of hoInv sManifest to aReq
            Move "" to sReq
            Move (SizeOfArray(aReq) - 1) to iMax
            For i from 0 to iMax
                Get GitRepoFromSource of hoInv (Trim(aReq[i].sSource)) to sOne
                If (Trim(sOne) = "") ;
                    Move (Trim(aReq[i].sSws)) to sOne
                If (Trim(sOne) <> "") Begin
                    If (sReq <> "") ;
                        Move (sReq + ", ") to sReq
                    Move (sReq + sOne) to sReq
                End
            Loop
            If (sReq <> "") ;
                Move (sOut + (((SpanOpenTip("", "Packages this one brings with it")) + (SpanOpen("ml-gl")) + CS_RequiresGlyph) + CS_SpanEnd + 'requires ') + (Esc(Self, sReq)) + CS_SpanEnd) to sOut

            Function_Return sOut
        End_Function

            /* THE LADDER: the library's OWN icon, else the DataFlex mark, else its initial (Nils
               2026-08-19: "The PoweredByDataFlex.png did not work well to use as a glyph. Is it
               possible to instead use an image from the DFRefactor/Bitmaps folder: Default.ico?").
               He was right about the cause - that PNG is 90x27, a 3.3:1 banner, and nothing makes a
               wide banner look like a square badge. Bitmaps\Default.ico is the DataFlex mark itself,
               square and multi-resolution (16 up to 256), so it is the honest stand-in for any
               DataFlex library that ships no icon of its own.

               LINKED, not inlined - the opposite of the package-cache case directly above, and for
               the opposite reason. That file lives outside AppHtml and could only travel as bytes;
               this one is OURS, so it is copied to AppHtml\Images and referenced relatively, exactly
               as the style sheets of CS_PaletteCssLink are. It costs the page nothing and the WebView
               caches it once, where inlining a 43 KB .ico would have put a ~58 KB data URI on every
               card that fell back.

               The initial survives as the last rung: if the file is ever missing from a deployment,
               onerror restores exactly what the card showed before. The parent is captured BEFORE
               the <img> is removed - after removeChild, this.parentNode is null. */
            /* THE BADGE BOX IS SIZED INLINE, NOT ONLY BY .ml-cg (Nils 2026-08-24: "mega-large
               characters ... about two library bands" flashing when the dialog opened).

               The <img> inside asks for width/height 100%. That resolves against the PARENT, and
               the parent gets its 32x32 from libraries.css - so in the frame before that sheet
               applies, .ml-cg is an ordinary auto-height div, 100% height is indeterminate, and
               the image falls back to its INTRINSIC size. Default.ico is multi-resolution up to
               256, and a package ships whatever it likes, so every badge painted at full icon
               size for one frame - clipped by the card, and legible enough to read: vWin32fh's
               package icon carries "VDF" lettering, which is what was seen. Data-dependent, which
               is why removing that one package made it stop and adding another did not bring it
               back. Inline wins because it applies at FIRST paint, before any stylesheet - the
               same reason cFTThemeHost_mixin.pkg injects the page background inline. Keep the 32px in
               step with .ml-cg in libraries.css. */
        Function _BadgeHtml String sKind String sIconUri String sShortName Returns String
            String sCls

            Move ("ml-cg ml-cg-" + sKind) to sCls
            If (sIconUri <> "") ;
                Function_Return ((DivOpenStyle(sCls, CS_LibBadgeBoxStyle)) ;
                    + (ImgHtml(sIconUri, "", CS_LibBadgeImgStyle, "")) + CS_DivEnd)

            // No icon of its own: the shipped Default.ico, with the package initial parked in
            // data-i for the onerror to read back. data-i is the one attribute still written out
            // here - it is the only data-* attribute the app emits, so it has earned no builder.
            Function_Return ('<div class="' + sCls + '" data-i="' + (Esc(Self, Uppercase(Left(sShortName, 1)))) ;
                + '" style="' + CS_LibBadgeBoxStyle + '">' ;
                + (ImgHtml("Images/Default.ico", "ml-cgd", CS_LibBadgeImgStyle, CS_LibBadgeOnError)) ;
                + CS_DivEnd)
        End_Function

        Function _CardHtml String sName String sKind String sCls String sPath String sVer ;
                            String sVerCls String sAvail String sState String sUse String sChain ;
                            String sLink String sAct String sEntry String sOvr ;
                            String sDesc String sIcon String sPub String sVia Returns String
            String sHtml sVerdict sShortName sPublisher sIconUri
            Integer iPos iSlash

            /* THE TITLE IS THE PACKAGE, NOT THE PATH TO IT (Nils 2026-08-19). A DataFlex system package
               arrives as "DataFlexCode/VDFQuery20" - publisher and package in one string - and putting
               the whole thing in the title row makes every card lead with the publisher instead of the
               thing itself. Split at the LAST "/": the title takes the package name, and the publisher
               moves to the meta line under it, behind a person glyph. A name with no "/" (a plain
               library) is unchanged - sPublisher stays empty and no glyph appears. */
            Move (Trim(sName)) to sShortName
            Move "" to sPublisher
            Move (RightPos("/", sShortName)) to iSlash
            If (iSlash > 1) Begin
                Move (Left(sShortName, (iSlash - 1))) to sPublisher
                Move (Right(sShortName, (Length(sShortName) - iSlash))) to sShortName
            End
            /* A CALLER THAT KNOWS THE PUBLISHER SAYS SO. A git dependency's owner comes off its URL
               (NilsSve), which no amount of splitting the title can recover now that the title is the
               repo name rather than "publisher/package". Passed in wins; the split below still serves
               a DataFlex system package, whose name genuinely carries both halves. (2026-08-19) */
            If (Trim(sPub) <> "") ;
                Move (Trim(sPub)) to sPublisher
            /* THE PUBLISHER SLOT ALWAYS SAYS SOMETHING (Nils 2026-08-19), which is also what retires
               the LOCAL tag beside the title:
                 - a DataFlex system package carries no publisher in its name, and the DataFlex system
                   packages live under DataFlex-dev on GitHub - so that is who published it;
                 - a library on disk has no publisher at all, and "LOCAL" belongs HERE rather than as a
                   chip after the title ("instead of adding the word LOCAL after the library title, use
                   LOCAL as the user name").
               A git url keeps whatever it parsed above (DataFlexCode, ...). */
            If (sPublisher = "" and Lowercase(Trim(sKind)) = "cloud") ;
                Move "DataFlex-dev" to sPublisher
            If (sPublisher = "" and Lowercase(Trim(sKind)) = "local") ;
                Move "Local" to sPublisher

            Move sState to sVerdict
            If (Right(sVerdict, 5) = "</td>") ;
                Move (Left(sVerdict, Length(sVerdict) - 5)) to sVerdict
            Move (Pos(">", sVerdict)) to iPos
            If (iPos > 0) ;
                Move (Mid(sVerdict, Length(sVerdict), iPos + 1)) to sVerdict

            Move (DivOpen(("ml-card" + sCls))) to sHtml
            /* THE PACKAGE'S OWN ICON where it ships one, else the initial. The <img> is given the
               badge's own size and an onerror that falls back to the letter, because the file sits
               outside AppHtml (in the package cache) and a WebView may refuse a file:// URL from a
               page served elsewhere - if it does, the card looks exactly as it did before rather than
               showing a broken image. The initial follows the TITLE, so a package badges its own
               letter, not its publisher's (DataFlexCode/VDFQuery20 badges "V", not "D"). */
            /* INLINED, not linked (2026-08-19). The first cut pointed an <img> at file:///... and the
               icon never appeared - the web host serves this page from its own origin and a WebView
               will not fetch a file:// URL from it, so the onerror fallback quietly put the letter
               back and the card looked untouched. rfFileAsDataUri embeds the bytes instead, which no
               origin rule can refuse. sIcon arrives as a PATH; "" back means unreadable or too big,
               and then the initial stands as before. */
            Move "" to sIconUri
            If (Trim(sIcon) <> "") ;
                Move (rfFileAsDataUri(Trim(sIcon))) to sIconUri
            Move (sHtml + (_BadgeHtml(Self, sKind, sIconUri, sShortName))) to sHtml
            Move (sHtml + (DivOpen("ml-ct"))) to sHtml
            // The kind chip stays for everything EXCEPT a local library, whose "LOCAL" now sits in the
            // publisher slot below - showing both would say the same word twice on one card.
            Move (sHtml + (DivOpen("ml-cn")) + (Esc(Self, sShortName))) to sHtml
            /* The chip says GIT for a DataFlex system package, not CLOUD (Nils 2026-08-19): the DataFlex
               system packages live on GitHub too, so it is the same kind of thing the Add Package
               dialog calls git - one word for one concept. The CSS class keeps the internal name, so
               the badge colour is unaffected. */
            If (Lowercase(Trim(sKind)) <> "local") Begin
                If (Lowercase(Trim(sKind)) = "cloud") ;
                    Move (sHtml + (((SpanOpen("ml-tag")) + 'git') + CS_SpanEnd)) to sHtml
                Else ;
                    Move (sHtml + (SpanOpen("ml-tag")) + sKind + CS_SpanEnd) to sHtml
            End
            Move (sHtml + CS_DivEnd) to sHtml
            /* THE PACKAGE'S DESCRIPTION, above the path (2026-08-19). What the Studio shows under a
               package name, and what our own browse list already receives in df-cli's Description
               column - it was simply never carried through to the card. A library that declares none
               is unchanged: no key, no line. */
            If (Trim(sDesc) <> "") ;
                Move (sHtml + (DivOpen("ml-cd")) + (Esc(Self, sDesc)) + CS_DivEnd) to sHtml
            Move (sHtml + (DivOpen("ml-cs")) + sPath + CS_DivEnd) to sHtml
            Move (sHtml + (DivOpen("ml-cm"))) to sHtml
            /* PUBLISHER then VERSION, both as glyphs rather than words (Nils 2026-08-19: "a
               person/personal glyph followed by DataFlexCode ... then skip the word Version and
               replace with a glyph, followed by the version number, on the same line"). Numeric HTML
               entities, not literal characters: the string travels DataFlex -> web host, and an entity
               cannot be mangled by an encoding step on the way. The title attributes keep the words,
               so a glyph nobody recognises is one hover from being explained. */
            If (sPublisher <> "") ;
                Move (sHtml + (((SpanOpenTip("", "Publisher / repository")) + (SpanOpen("ml-gl")) + CS_PublisherGlyph) + CS_SpanEnd) + (Esc(Self, sPublisher)) + CS_SpanEnd) to sHtml
            If (Trim(sVer) <> "") Begin
                    /* A COMMIT IS NOT A VERSION, AND MUST NOT WEAR THE VERSION GLYPH (Nils 2026-08-19:
                       "The version number makes no sense: 'a1f8ff9', it says absolutely nothing"). It
                       was right: a bare SHA sat behind the layers mark, reading as this library's
                       version. Shortening it to 7 characters made it tidier, not more meaningful. So a
                       pinned commit now says the word "commit" IN the row - the explanation cannot live
                       in a title attribute, because the complaint was about what the card SHOWS, and
                       nobody hovers a value they have already read as a version. The commit node glyph
                       (U+25C9, a filled ring - a commit on a graph) replaces the layers mark, and the
                       SHA is dimmed to ml-cm's own grey: it identifies the build, but it is not the
                       fact the eye should land on. A repo that declares a "version" in its .sws keeps
                       the layers mark and the bright blue, so the two are never confusable. */
                    If (sVerCls contains "commit") Begin
                        Move (sHtml + (SpanOpenTipHtml("", ("The pinned COMMIT this package is built from -" ;
                            + " this repository declares no version of its own. Add a &quot;version&quot; to its" ;
                            + " .sws and that is shown here instead."))) ;
                            + (SpanOpen("ml-gl")) + CS_CommitGlyph + CS_SpanEnd ;
                            + 'commit ' + '<code class="' + sVerCls + '">' + sVer + '</code>' ;
                            + CS_SpanEnd) to sHtml
                    End
                    Else Begin
                        /* THE LAYERS MARK, U+29C9 (Nils 2026-08-19: "the V was a dull one. The Studio is
                           using the layer glyph"). Two joined squares - stacked sheets, which is what the
                           Studio's version icon shows. It is a TEXT glyph because an <img> cannot take
                           the CSS colour. (Since 2026-09-29 an SVG file in AppHtml\FTGlyphs could, but
                           the character does the job.) It is monochrome, so it inherits ml-gl's size and
                           blue like the person glyph. */
                        Move (sHtml + (((SpanOpenTip("", "Version")) + (SpanOpen("ml-gl")) + CS_VersionGlyph) + CS_SpanEnd + '<code class="') + sVerCls + '">' + sVer + ('</code>' + CS_SpanEnd)) to sHtml
                    End
                End
            /* THE LINE ENDS AT THE VERSION (Nils 2026-08-19, rephrasing: "skip all text after the
               version number, the text is either redundant or not needed"). What used to follow it -
               "available ...", the path, the install verdict and the entry string - was either already
               on the card (the path is the ml-cs line directly above) or noise on a row whose job is
               to say WHOSE this is and WHICH version. Publisher, version, and nothing else.

               sVerdict / sState and sEntry are still PARAMETERS of this function: the callers build
               them and other cards' markup uses sState, so they are left computed rather than ripped
               out - the decision here is only about what this line shows. */
            // sVia rides INSIDE the meta div, so it lands after the version on the same line.
            Move (sHtml + sVia + CS_DivEnd + sOvr + sUse + sChain + CS_DivEnd + sAct + CS_DivEnd) to sHtml
            Function_Return sHtml
        End_Function

        Function BodyHtml Returns String
            String[] asEntries asMsgLibs asSeen
            tLibraryInfo[] aInfo
            tSwsDependency[] aObjDeps
            String sPkgName sPkgSws sPkgVer sPkgSrc sPkgKey sCard sSortKey sKind sVerCls sAct sOvrNote
            String sPkgTitle sPkgDesc sPkgIcon sPkgPub sExtras sWsDf sRegSws
            Boolean bPkgIsCommit
            tSwsOverride[] aOvr
            String[] asCardHtml asCardKey
            Handle hoInv2
            Integer[] aiMsgCnt
            String sHtml sRows sSws sHome sTarget sEntry sResolved sName sVer sAvail
            String sVerCell sStateCell sRowCls sStem sLink sUrl sPathLink sReveal sUse sNote
            String sChain sIndent
            Integer i iMax iOwnMax iSel iPos iMsgAt iAttn iOwn iDirect iObjDeps iSelKind iSwsFormat iObjMax iCardMax iSortAt
            tSwsDependency[] aWork aNested
            String[] asVia asCarded
            Integer[] aiDepth
            Integer[] aiParentOf aiRootOf aiGitCardAt aiStack aiOrder   // the git rows' tree: who brought each, and its root's card
            Integer iRootCard iDep iNode iKid       // a nested card goes INSIDE its root card (2026-09-29)
            String sVia sNestKey sNestName sNestSws sNestVer sNestDesc sNestIcon sNestPub sNestPath sNestAct
            Integer j k iWorkAt iNestMax iGuard
            Boolean bBehind bHaveMsgs bHaveUse bKeepGoing
            Handle hoInv

            // Resolved through (Self) rather than by bare name - the form every other FT modal here
            // uses, and the one that still resolves when this runs during construction (a design-time
            // Set psHtml below) as well as from the live panel.
            Get psSwsFile  of (oMaintainLibraries_dg(Self)) to sSws
            Get pasEntries of (oMaintainLibraries_dg(Self)) to asEntries
            Get piSelected of (oMaintainLibraries_dg(Self)) to iSel
            Get psTargetDfVersion of ghoApplication to sTarget

            // ---- inline dark stylesheet (the dashboard palette, as every FT modal uses) ----
            // The stylesheet is AppHtml/CssStyle/libraries.css. Linked after palette/buttons/bars,
            // so a rule here still wins a tie against a shared one exactly as the inline block did.
            Move (CS_PaletteCssLink + CS_LibrariesCssLink) to sHtml

            // ---- the page's body, in one wrapper. The heading is the page's own title now (oMLPage's psLabel):
            // both kinds live in this list, so it names both (Nils 2026-08-19). ----
            Move (sHtml + (DivOpen("ml-wrap"))) to sHtml
            Move (RightPos("\", sSws)) to iPos
            Move sSws to sStem
            If (iPos > 0) ;
                Move (Right(sSws, (Length(sSws) - iPos))) to sStem
            /* The legend follows what the rows can ACTUALLY say. With no target chosen nothing can
               be behind anything, so promising an amber that will never appear would send the
               reader hunting for a mark that is not there. */
            Move (sHtml + (DivOpen("ml-sub")) + (BoldHtml(Esc(Self, sStem)))) to sHtml
            If (sTarget <> "") Begin
                Move (sHtml + ' ' + CS_MidDot + ' migrating to DataFlex ' + (Esc(Self, sTarget))) to sHtml
                Move (sHtml + ' ' + CS_MidDot + ((' the Version column is what each library declares for ITSELF; ' + (SpanOpen("behind")) + 'amber') + CS_SpanEnd + ' is older than the target - a candidate to bring forward.')) to sHtml
            End
            Else Begin
                Move (sHtml + ' ' + CS_MidDot + ' the Version column is what each library declares for ITSELF. Choose a target DataFlex version to see which of them are behind it.') to sHtml
            End
            Move (sHtml + CS_DivEnd) to sHtml

            /* SAY IT HERE, not three clicks later (Nils 2026-08-14: "there is nothing that - so
               far - indicates that the conversion to Json failed. Not until the Add package button
               is clicked"). A failed conversion was reported only in the copy's completion report,
               which appears AFTER this dialog has been and gone - so the one screen that is all
               about this file said nothing about the state the file was in.

               Shown whenever the file is INI, not only after a copy: the format is a fact about the
               workspace, and it is equally worth knowing on one opened cold. */
            Get Create (RefClass(cLibraryInventoryApp)) to hoInv
            Get SwsFormatOf of hoInv sSws to iSwsFormat
            Send Destroy of hoInv
            /* A DF 25 BUILD MUST NOT OFFER THIS (Nils 2026-08-19: the banner appeared when running
               DFRefactorFT under DF 25). The JSON workspace format and the df-cli that writes it are
               DataFlex 26 features; a build made with the DF 25 toolchain has no business telling its
               user that their INI workspace is behind, and no df-cli of its own to convert it with.
               Compile-time, not a runtime test, because it is a property of THIS BUILD - the whole
               banner simply does not exist in the DF 25 one. (Its "mlconvert" handler is guarded the
               same way, or it would be dead code referencing a chip nothing can emit.) */
            If (iSwsFormat = eSwsIni) Begin
                /* WORDED AS THE NORMAL STEP (Nils 2026-10-07): it read as a packages-only matter - "the
                   libraries below work as they are, but a package cannot be recorded" - so on a move to
                   DataFlex 26 it was easy to read past. */
                Move (sHtml + ((DivOpen("ml-ini")) + ('This workspace file is still in the older ' + (BoldText("INI")) + ' format. DataFlex 26 uses the ' + (BoldText("JSON")) + ' format, which is also the only format that can hold packages. Converting is the normal step when moving to DataFlex 26 ' + CS_EmDash + ' the libraries below keep working.'))) to sHtml
                Move (sHtml + (ClickableHtml("span", "ml-inibtn", "mlconvert", "", "Convert to JSON now", "Convert this workspace file to the DataFlex 26 JSON format, using df-cli.")) + CS_DivEnd) to sHtml
            End

            /* TWO BOUNDS, and they are NOT the same number - which is what broke this on its first
               real workspace (Nils 2026-08-12: "still only #1 WebAppSrc shown").

                 iOwnMax - the WORKING LIST. What Add and Remove edit, what OK writes, and what a
                          selection index means.
                 iMax    - the RENDERED TREE, which is longer the moment any library declares
                          libraries of its own.

               The row loop was bounded by the working list while iterating the tree, so on
               E:\WinServ - one declared library, seven behind it - it rendered row 0 and stopped.
               Every unit test passed throughout: they cover the WALK, and the walk was right. This
               is the renderer, which needs a window and so has no test to fail. */
            Move (SizeOfArray(asEntries) - 1) to iOwnMax
            /* NO EARLY RETURN WHEN THERE ARE NO PATH ENTRIES (Nils 2026-08-14: "the whole list
               goes empty").

               This used to render "This workspace declares no libraries", its own footer, and
               RETURN - which was true when a library could only be a path. It is not now: a git or
               cloud package is a dependency with no path at all, so a workspace can have nothing
               to say here and still depend on three things. Removing the last path entry made the
               packages vanish with it.

               The note is emitted and the code falls through instead. The entry loop is bounded by
               the tree, which is empty, so it simply does not run - and the package cards below
               still do. */
            /* Names only the buttons this BUILD actually shows - Add Package... is DF 26+. A Begin/End
               block, NOT the "If (...) ;" continuation that was here: a compiler directive between the
               continuation and its statement splits the two, and the If then swallows whatever line
               followed the #ENDIF. */
            /* NO EMPTY-STATE PARAGRAPH (Nils 2026-08-21: "Remove that text!").

               It named the two buttons sitting in plain sight a few centimetres below it, and on a
               workspace with packages but no path libraries - Order Entry, once DFAbout and vWin32fh
               were added - it took nearly HALF the dialog height to say nothing the footer does not
               already say. A workspace with no path entries simply shows its package cards. */

            /* ---- the entries, in file order --------------------------------------------------

               THE ROWS COME FROM THE WORKING LIST, described by LibraryEntriesInfo - NOT from a
               fresh read of the .sws. Add and Remove change the list before OK writes it, so a
               file-based description would show the list as it was rather than as it is, and a
               just-added row would render as though it were not there.

               Every entry gets a row, including one whose .sws is missing. That is the whole reason
               this is LibraryEntriesInfo and not WorkspaceLibraryInventory: the inventory keeps only
               what it can resolve, which silently shortened the old report's list and its counts. */
            Get _FolderOf of (oMaintainLibraries_dg(Self)) sSws to sHome
            Get Create (RefClass(cLibraryInventoryApp)) to hoInv
            /* THE CHAIN, not just the workspace's own entries (Nils 2026-08-12). A library declares
               libraries of its own and its Version= describes only itself, so a workspace can show
               one row reading "25.0" while seven libraries behind it sit on 19.1 and get compiled
               into a DF26 build. Depth 0 rows are the workspace's, and the only editable ones;
               everything below is shown because it is evidence, and left alone because editing it
               would mean writing into a library's own .sws.

               Built from the WORKING list, so a library just added brings its chain with it. */
            Get LibraryEntriesTree of hoInv (&asEntries) sHome sTarget 0 CI_MLMaxChainDepth (&asSeen) to aInfo
            // The row loop runs over the TREE from here on, so its bound comes from the tree.
            Move (SizeOfArray(aInfo) - 1) to iMax
            // The compiler's own evidence, per library - asked once for the whole table rather than
            // per row. False means the analyzer has no data at all, which must not read as zero.
            Get LibraryMessageCounts of ghoLibraryReport (&asMsgLibs) (&aiMsgCnt) to bHaveMsgs
            /* NO SILENT SHORTENING. A git/cloud dependency has no path and no readable version, so
               it gets no row - but a list that is quietly shorter than the file is the same defect
               as the old report's dropped entries. The note line below says it exists and says who
               owns it.

               ASKED HERE, with every other hoInv read, because hoInv is DESTROYED once the row loop
               ends - and the note is built after that. Sending to a destroyed object is error 98,
               "Invalid message", which is what this did on its first run. */
            Get SwsJsonObjectDependencyCount of hoInv sSws to iObjDeps
            // Read here with every other hoInv call - hoInv is destroyed once the cards are built.
            Get SwsJsonOverrides of hoInv sSws to aOvr
            // What KIND the selected row is, read here while hoInv is alive - the button row below
            // needs it to say whether it will remove a list entry or uninstall a package.
            Move eDepPath to iSelKind
            If (iSel >= 0 and iSel <= iOwnMax) ;
                Get DependencyKind of hoInv asEntries[iSel] to iSelKind
            Get pbHaveUsages of ghoLibraryReport to bHaveUse

            /* ---- ONE FLAT LIST OF CARDS, sorted by name (Nils 2026-08-14) ------------------

               The table became a card list because the job changed. Nils migrated DFRefactor
               itself by adding each local library AGAIN as a GitHub package and then deleting the
               local twin - and that only works if the twins are VISIBLE TOGETHER. A table grouped
               parent-then-children put them pages apart; sorted by name they land adjacent, which
               is the whole point of flattening.

               TWO SOURCES, one list. aInfo holds the path and cloud entries; the git/file OBJECT
               dependencies are not in it at all (SwsJsonLibraryEntries skips them - no path to
               resolve). Cards are built from both into asCardHtml, keyed by name in asCardKey, and
               sorted together. A local X and a GitHub X are then neighbours whatever the file
               order was.

               EVERY CARD CARRIES ITS OWN ACTION. The old table drove Remove through piSelected, an
               index into the working list that only depth-0 rows had - the "two bounds" trap that
               rendered row 0 and stopped. A button on the card needs no index and cannot act on
               the wrong entry. Selection survives only as highlighting.

               Nothing the columns said is dropped: version, available versions, the verdict, the
               chain roll-up, compiler messages, usages and the resolved path all move onto the
               card. */
            Move "" to sRows
            Move -1 to iRootCard
            For i from 0 to iMax
                Move aInfo[i].sRawEntry to sEntry
                Move aInfo[i].sRefSwsFile to sResolved
                Move aInfo[i].sName to sName
                Move aInfo[i].sRefVersion to sVer
                /* A LIBRARY CAN DESCRIBE ITSELF TOO (2026-08-19). Its own .sws carries the keys once it
                   is JSON - the same "description" / "workspaceName" a package keeps in the cache - so
                   a library referenced by path is no longer the poor relation of a DataFlex system package.
                   "" for every INI .sws, which is still the overwhelming majority, and the card then
                   looks exactly as it did. The package branch below overwrites these from the cache. */
                Get SwsManifestValue of hoInv aInfo[i].sRefSwsFile "description"   to sPkgDesc
                Get SwsManifestValue of hoInv aInfo[i].sRefSwsFile "workspaceName" to sPkgTitle
                Move "" to sPkgIcon
                If (Trim(sPkgTitle) <> "") ;
                    Move (Trim(sPkgTitle)) to sName
                Get _VersionListText of ghoLibraryReport (&aInfo[i].asAvailVersions) to sAvail
                Move "" to sVerCls
                Move "" to sKind

                If (aInfo[i].eKind = eDepPackage) Begin
                    /* A CLOUD PACKAGE. Its version is PINNED BY THIS WORKSPACE rather than
                       declared by a file, so it is never amber - "older than the target" is a
                       statement about a .sws and means nothing here. Available comes from the
                       package's own df-min/df-max, blank when it does not say. */
                    Move "cloud" to sKind
                    Move "pkg" to sVerCls
                    /* WHAT THE PACKAGE SAYS ABOUT ITSELF (Nils 2026-08-19, comparing with the Studio,
                       which shows a name and a description per package). Both come from the package's
                       own df.sws in the cache - the same text df-cli's search Description column
                       returns - so no process is spawned per row. Absent keys give "", and the card
                       then looks exactly as it did before. */
                    Get PackageManifestValue of hoInv aInfo[i].sRawEntry "workspaceName" to sPkgTitle
                    Get PackageManifestValue of hoInv aInfo[i].sRawEntry "description"   to sPkgDesc
                    Get PackageIconPath      of hoInv aInfo[i].sRawEntry to sPkgIcon
                    If (Trim(sPkgTitle) <> "") ;
                        Move (Trim(sPkgTitle)) to sName
                    Get PackageRangeText aInfo[i].sDfMin aInfo[i].sDfMax to sAvail
                    Get StatusCellHtml aInfo[i].eStatus sTarget to sStateCell
                    If (aInfo[i].eStatus = eLibPackageMismatch and sAvail <> "") ;
                        Move (Left(sStateCell, Length(sStateCell) - 5) + (' ' + (SpanOpen("msgs"))) + CS_MidDot + ' supports ' + (Esc(Self, sAvail)) + (CS_SpanEnd + '</td>')) to sStateCell
                    If (aInfo[i].bFound = False) ;
                        Move (Left(sStateCell, Length(sStateCell) - 5) + (' ' + (SpanOpen("gone"))) + CS_MidDot + (' not in the package cache' + CS_SpanEnd + '</td>')) to sStateCell
                End
                Else If (aInfo[i].bFound = False) Begin
                    // NOT FOUND, and said so on the card. An entry pointing at nothing is the most
                    // likely damage after a copy and the most actionable state there is.
                    Move "local" to sKind
                    Move "gone" to sVerCls
                    Move CS_EmDash to sVer
                    Move "" to sAvail
                    Move ((CellHtml("st-gone", 'not found ' + CS_EmDash + ' nothing is at that path'))) to sStateCell
                End
                Else Begin
                    Move "local" to sKind
                    If (sVer = "") ;
                        Move CS_EmDash to sVer
                    /* NO VERSION VERDICT ON A DF26 WORKSPACE (Nils 2026-08-13: "Should we complain
                       at all about what version the .sws file is at? The dialog is for DF 26 and
                       up, and the version should/could be anything." - parked behind the Remove
                       trace then, delivered 2026-08-15).

                       Under DF26 each entry names whatever .sws it wants and the package handler
                       resolves it - there is no one version the chain must sit on, so "no 26.0
                       build - needs a newer copy" judged rows against a rule the format does not
                       have. The declared version stays on the card as a FACT; the amber class and
                       the verdict are what go. Not-found keeps its verdict in the branch above - a
                       path pointing at nothing is broken in any format - and a PACKAGE keeps its
                       own df-min/df-max verdict, which is the package's claim, not a .sws rule. */
                    If (iSwsFormat = eSwsJson) ;
                        Move (CellHtml("st-ok", 'found')) to sStateCell
                    Else Begin
                        // No Version= to read -> an em dash, and NEVER amber: "we could not tell"
                        // and "it is old" are different answers and only one is actionable.
                        Get IsVersionBelow of hoInv aInfo[i].sRefVersion sTarget to bBehind
                        If (bBehind = True) ;
                            Move "behind" to sVerCls
                        Get StatusCellHtml aInfo[i].eStatus sTarget to sStateCell
                    End
                End

                // The compiler messages coming out of THIS library, folded into its verdict.
                Move (SearchArray(sName, asMsgLibs)) to iMsgAt
                If (iMsgAt <> -1) ;
                    Move (Left(sStateCell, Length(sStateCell) - 5) + (' ' + (SpanOpen("msgs"))) + CS_MidDot + ' ' + String(aiMsgCnt[iMsgAt]) + (' compiler messages' + CS_SpanEnd + '</td>')) to sStateCell

                // WHERE TO GET IT - only for a library that needs something. DFRefactor fetches
                // nothing; this opens the project page.
                Move "" to sLink
                If (aInfo[i].bFound = False or (iSwsFormat <> eSwsJson and aInfo[i].eStatus <> eLibMatchesTarget)) Begin
                    Get LibraryHomeUrl of hoInv sName to sUrl
                    If (sUrl <> "") ;
                        Move (' ' + (ClickableHtml("span", "proj", ('cfmurl:' + sUrl), "", "project page &rsaquo;", "Open this library&apos;s project page in your browser. DFRefactor downloads nothing - it is yours to choose and install."))) to sLink
                End

                // WHICH COPY: the resolved .sws, revealed in Explorer on click. A machine
                // routinely holds several copies of one library.
                If (aInfo[i].bFound = True) ;
                    Get PathLinkHtml of ghoLibraryReport sResolved sResolved "ml-plink-cool" to sPathLink
                Else Begin
                    Get NearestExistingFolder of ghoLibraryReport sResolved to sReveal
                    If (sReveal <> "") ;
                        Get PathLinkHtml of ghoLibraryReport sReveal ("expected at " + sResolved) "ml-plink-cool" to sPathLink
                    Else ;
                        Move (((SpanOpen("ml-dim")) + 'expected at ') + (Esc(Self, sResolved)) + CS_SpanEnd) to sPathLink
                End
                Move "" to sUse
                If (bHaveUse = True) ;
                    Get UsageCellHtml of ghoLibraryReport sName to sUse

                // THE ROLL-UP - what makes a parent's own Version= honest.
                Move "" to sChain
                If (aInfo[i].iChainBehind > 0 and iSwsFormat <> eSwsJson) Begin
                    If (aInfo[i].iChainBehind = aInfo[i].iChainCount) ;
                        Move (((DivOpen("chain")) + 'declares ') + (Esc(Self, aInfo[i].sRefVersion)) + ', but all ' + String(aInfo[i].iChainCount) + ' of its own libraries are older') to sChain
                    Else ;
                        Move (((DivOpen("chain")) + 'declares ') + (Esc(Self, aInfo[i].sRefVersion)) + ', but ' + String(aInfo[i].iChainBehind) + ' of its ' + String(aInfo[i].iChainCount) + ' own libraries are older') to sChain
                    If (aInfo[i].sChainOldest <> "") ;
                        Move (sChain + ' ' + CS_EmDash + ' oldest ' + (Esc(Self, aInfo[i].sChainOldest))) to sChain
                    Move (sChain + CS_DivEnd) to sChain
                End
                Else If (aInfo[i].iChainCount > 0) Begin
                    Move (((DivOpen("chainok")) + 'declares ') + String(aInfo[i].iChainCount) + ' librar' + (If(aInfo[i].iChainCount = 1, "y", "ies")) + (' of its own' + CS_DivEnd)) to sChain
                End

                /* DEPTH 0 IS THE WORKSPACE'S OWN, and still the only kind that can be removed:
                   anything deeper belongs to a library's .sws, and editing that would fork the
                   library. A deeper card is tagged "nested", and gets no action button at all, rather
                   than one that refuses.

                   It is also INDENTED, under its own parent (2026-09-29, Nils: "the nested packages are
                   no longer indented" - the DataFlex Reports chain in Order Entry). As a card of its
                   own it was sorted by its own name and landed wherever the alphabet put it, which is
                   why it had only been tagged. Now it goes INSIDE its root's card - see iRootCard below
                   - in the report's own order: a parent, then its children (LibraryEntriesTree). */
                Move "" to sRowCls
                Move "" to sAct
                If (aInfo[i].iDepth = 0) Begin
                    If (iOwn = iSel) ;
                        Move " sel" to sRowCls
                    /* The Uninstall chip is df-cli's, so it exists only in a DF 26+ build. A DF 25 build
                       can still SHOW a package row - it happens when a DF 26 workspace is opened with
                       it - but it must not offer an action it cannot carry out; the row then simply
                       carries no chip. (See the Add Package button for the whole-feature reasoning.) */
                    If (aInfo[i].eKind = eDepPackage) ;
                        Move ((ClickableHtml("div", "ml-cx ml-cx-q", ('mldetails|S*' + (Esc(Self, sEntry))), "", "Details", "Licence, supported DataFlex versions, what it requires, and the full description.")) + (_UpdateChipHtml(Self, sEntry, (PackageVersion(hoInv, sEntry)), False)) + (ClickableHtml("div", "ml-cx", ('mlpkgdel|' + (Esc(Self, sEntry))), "", "Uninstall", "Uninstall this package from the workspace. It happens at once - Cancel will not put it back."))) to sAct
                    If (aInfo[i].eKind <> eDepPackage) Begin
                        /* CHANGE, beside Remove (Nils 2026-08-13: "each and every one of the local
                           libraries should be able to be deleted or updated for another .sws
                           file"). The everyday case is a version move - the entry names the 19.1
                           file and the 26.0 sits beside it. Remove + Add gets there too, but drops
                           the row and makes the user re-find the folder; Change starts the browse
                           in the folder the current file is in. List-only until OK, like Remove. */
                        Move ((ClickableHtml("div", "ml-cx", ('mlchange|' + String(iOwn)), "", "Change...", "Swap this entry for another .sws file - for instance the newer build sitting in the same folder. The list changes now; nothing is written until OK."))) to sAct
                        Move (sAct + (ClickableHtml("div", "ml-cx ml-cx-q", ('mlrowdel|' + String(iOwn)), "", "Remove", "Remove this library, and any child libraries it declares, from the workspace library list. Nothing is written until OK."))) to sAct
                    End
                    Increment iOwn
                End
                Else Begin
                    /* A NESTED ENTRY IS REMOVABLE TOO (Nils 2026-08-14), because "library" here
                       usually means the user's OWN shared code, not a vendor drop - WebAppSrc is
                       his. The Studio removes such an entry from whatever .sws lists it, and for
                       code you own that is simply right. The guard is not refusal but NAMING the
                       file, since it is shared: see DoRemoveNested.

                       The button carries the declaring .sws and the raw entry, so nothing has to
                       be resolved from a name afterwards. */
                    If (Trim(aInfo[i].sDeclaredBySws) <> "") ;
                        Move ((ClickableHtml("div", "ml-cx ml-cx-q", ('mlnestdel|' + (Esc(Self, Trim(aInfo[i].sDeclaredBySws))) + '|' + (Esc(Self, sEntry))), "", "Remove", "Remove this entry from the library that declares it. Written immediately, and it changes that library for every workspace using it."))) to sAct
                    /* Begin/End, NOT "Else ;" (2026-08-14). The continuation binds Else to the NEXT
                       LINE, and the next line here was a comment - so the Move ran unconditionally
                       and every card came out "nested". Nils saw it at once: all the glyphs turned
                       one colour. A comment between "Else ;" and its statement silently detaches
                       them; a block cannot.

                       "nested" is also ONE TOKEN because sKind becomes part of a CSS class name,
                       and the phrase that was here before ("declared by a library") produced no
                       class at all. */
                    Move "nested" to sKind
                    // Indented by depth - at most three steps, like a git package's card.
                    Move aInfo[i].iDepth to iDep
                    If (iDep > 3) ;
                        Move 3 to iDep
                    Move (" ml-c-dep ml-c-dep" + String(iDep)) to sRowCls
                End

                /* A SYSTEM PACKAGE GETS THE SAME EXTRAS AS A GIT ONE (Nils 2026-08-21: "I added
                   a system package and it didn't reveal any extra info either").

                   They never had nothing to say - DataFlex's own manifests carry license,
                   df-min, df-max AND dependencies. They were never asked: a system package is
                   a STRING dependency drawn by THIS loop, while the extras were added to the git
                   OBJECT loop further down. Two card paths, one of them missed.

                   PackageCacheFolder already knew where to look - it builds the FULL mangled
                   name, repo and package and version together, in DataFlex's global cache. My
                   first attempt reused the GIT lookup, which matches on the version suffix
                   alone: unique for a 40-character commit, but "DataFlex_dev_Web_UI-1.0.52" and
                   "DataFlex_dev_Web_UI_FlexTron-1.0.52" both end "-1.0.52", so Web UI came out
                   claiming to require Web UI. */
                Move "" to sExtras
                If (aInfo[i].eKind = eDepPackage) Begin
                    Get PackageCacheFolder of hoInv sEntry to sRegSws
                    If (sRegSws <> "") ;
                        Get _PkgMetaExtras hoInv (sRegSws + "df.sws") sWsDf to sExtras
                End
                Get _OverrideNote aOvr sName sName sEntry to sOvrNote
                Get _CardHtml sName sKind sRowCls sPathLink sVer sVerCls sAvail sStateCell sUse sChain sLink sAct sEntry sOvrNote sPkgDesc sPkgIcon "" sExtras to sCard
                /* A NESTED CARD GOES INSIDE ITS ROOT'S (2026-09-29): appended to the HTML of the depth-0
                   card above it, so the sort below, which orders the cards by name, carries it along.
                   Only the roots are sorted. A sort key of the parent's name + a separator cannot hold
                   a child under its parent: DataFlex compares strings by collation, and a child keyed
                   "DUF~X" sorted in under a top-level DUF-Extra ("~"), or DUF2 (Character(1)) - probe
                   CardOrder, 2026-09-29. */
                If ((aInfo[i].iDepth > 0) and (iRootCard >= 0)) Begin
                    Move (asCardHtml[iRootCard] + sCard) to asCardHtml[iRootCard]
                End
                Else Begin
                    Move sCard to asCardHtml[-1]
                    Move (Uppercase(sName)) to asCardKey[-1]
                    Move (SizeOfArray(asCardHtml) - 1) to iRootCard
                End
            Loop

            /* The GIT / FILE dependencies, into the SAME list. These are the ones the table could
               never hold: an object dependency has no path, no folder and no on-disk version. */
            // The workspace's own DataFlex version, so a package range that merely restates it can
            // be suppressed instead of printed on every row.
            Get SwsManifestValue of hoInv sSws "df" to sWsDf
            Get SwsJsonObjectDependencies of hoInv sSws to aObjDeps
            Move (SizeOfArray(aObjDeps) - 1) to iObjMax
            For i from 0 to iObjMax
                Get _PackageNameOf aObjDeps[i] to sPkgName
                Get _PackageKeyOf  aObjDeps[i] to sPkgKey
                /* WHAT THE CHECKED-OUT REPO SAYS ABOUT ITSELF (Nils 2026-08-19, after publishing a JSON
                   .sws to Library-DFAbout and seeing none of it): the title was the .sws FILE STEM
                   ("DFABOUT-LIBRARY-DF26"), there was no description, and the version slot held the
                   40-character commit. All three are answered by the manifest df-cli has already put
                   in the DfPkg checkout - workspaceName, description, icon - with the commit shortened
                   to the 7 characters git itself shows when the repo declares no version of its own. */
                Get ObjectDependencyManifest of hoInv sHome aObjDeps[i] to sPkgSws
                Move "" to sPkgDesc
                Move "" to sPkgIcon
                /* AND THE VERSION, which until 2026-08-19 was the one of the three NOT cleared here.
                   It is only assigned inside the "manifest was found" branch below, so a dependency
                   whose DfPkg checkout is missing kept whatever the PREVIOUS card had - and, being
                   non-blank, skipped the fall-back to the commit as well. The card would then show the
                   card-above's version, in the declared-version colour, with no hint it was borrowed.
                   Dormant while no git package declared a version at all; live the moment one does. */
                Move "" to sPkgVer
                /* THE REPO IS THE IDENTITY. Title = the GitHub repo (Library-DFAcout -> Library-DFAbout),
                   publisher = its owner (NilsSve) - both off the source URL, because that is what the
                   user went and fetched. workspaceName is deliberately NOT the title here: it is what
                   the workspace calls itself ("DFAbout"), which is a different thing from the repo. */
                Get GitRepoFromSource  of hoInv (Trim(aObjDeps[i].sSource)) to sPkgTitle
                Get GitOwnerFromSource of hoInv (Trim(aObjDeps[i].sSource)) to sPkgPub
                If (Trim(sPkgTitle) <> "") ;
                    Move (Trim(sPkgTitle)) to sPkgName
                If (sPkgSws <> "") Begin
                    Get SwsManifestValue of hoInv sPkgSws "description" to sPkgDesc
                    Get SwsManifestValue of hoInv sPkgSws "icon"        to sPkgIcon
                    // The icon is named RELATIVE to the manifest, so it resolves beside it.
                    If (Trim(sPkgIcon) <> "") Begin
                        Move (Left(sPkgSws, RightPos("\", sPkgSws)) + Trim(sPkgIcon)) to sPkgIcon
                        If (vFilePathExists(sPkgIcon) = 0) ;
                            Move "" to sPkgIcon
                    End
                    /* A VERSION THE REPO DECLARES BEATS THE COMMIT (Nils 2026-08-19: "a1f8ff9 says
                       absolutely nothing" - and he is right, a hash identifies but does not inform).
                       When the manifest names a version, that is shown; otherwise the pinned commit
                       is all there is, shortened and labelled as one by its tooltip rather than
                       sitting in the slot pretending to be a release number. */
                    Get SwsManifestValue of hoInv sPkgSws "version" to sPkgVer
                End
                Move False to bPkgIsCommit
                If (Trim(sPkgVer) = "") Begin
                    Get ShortDependencyVersion of hoInv (Trim(aObjDeps[i].sVersion)) to sPkgVer
                    Move True to bPkgIsCommit
                End
                Move "" to sStateCell
                Move (CellHtml("", ((SpanOpen("ml-ok")) + CS_Check + ' installed' + CS_SpanEnd))) to sStateCell
                Move ((SpanOpen("ml-dim")) + (Esc(Self, Trim(aObjDeps[i].sSource))) + CS_SpanEnd) to sPathLink
                Move "" to sAct
                Move ((ClickableHtml("div", "ml-cx ml-cx-q", ('mldetails|G*' + (Esc(Self, Trim(aObjDeps[i].sSource))) + '*' + (Esc(Self, Trim(aObjDeps[i].sVersion))) + '*' + (Esc(Self, sPkgSws))), "", "Details", "Licence, supported DataFlex versions, what it requires, and the full description.")) + (_UpdateChipHtml(Self, sPkgKey, (Trim(aObjDeps[i].sVersion)), True)) + (ClickableHtml("div", "ml-cx", ('mlpkgdel|' + (Esc(Self, sPkgKey))), "", "Uninstall", "Uninstall this package from the workspace. It happens at once - Cancel will not put it back."))) to sAct
                Get _PkgMetaExtras hoInv sPkgSws sWsDf to sExtras
                Get _OverrideNote aOvr sPkgName sPkgKey (Trim(aObjDeps[i].sSws)) to sOvrNote
                Get _CardHtml sPkgName "git" "" sPathLink sPkgVer (If(bPkgIsCommit, "pkg commit", "pkg")) "" sStateCell "" "" "" sAct (Trim(aObjDeps[i].sSws)) sOvrNote sPkgDesc sPkgIcon sPkgPub sExtras to sCard
                Move sCard to asCardHtml[-1]
                Move (Uppercase(sPkgName)) to asCardKey[-1]
                Move (SizeOfArray(asCardHtml) - 1) to aiGitCardAt[-1]    // where the rows it brought go
            Loop

            /* THE PACKAGES NOBODY ASKED FOR - what the ones above pulled in.

               A workspace file records only the dependencies the USER named; whatever those
               drag in is installed but never written there. Proven on E:\OrderEntry
               (2026-08-21): asking for DUF alone produced FOUR checkouts in DfPkg - DUF,
               RDCToolsLib, DFAbout and vWin32fh - and exactly one line in the .sws. Before this
               the dialog showed that single line, so DUF looked as though it had brought
               nothing, and a problem inside vWin32fh would have come from a package the user
               could not see was even present.

               A WORK LIST, not a nested loop, because the graph is deeper than one level:
               vWin32fh arrives under RDCToolsLib, which arrives under DUF. Each entry is
               expanded in turn and anything new is appended, so the walk reaches whatever depth
               the graph actually has. iGuard bounds it at 64 expansions - a malformed pair of
               manifests that name each other would otherwise spin here, and a dialog that hangs
               is worse than one that stops listing.

               asCarded starts as the DIRECT keys, so a library that is both named by the
               workspace and pulled in by another appears once, as the direct row it is - with
               its Uninstall and Update intact.

               Read-only rows (Nils 2026-08-21: "No Uninstall or Update on transitive rows").
               Neither action would be honest here: df-cli removed all four when DUF was
               uninstalled, so an Uninstall on vWin32fh would fight its owner, and moving a
               transitive pin means editing RDCToolsLib's manifest, not this workspace's. */
            /* A dependent goes UNDER the package that needs it (Nils 2026-08-21: "it would be better
               if the dependent packages where indented and listed under the main package"). Cards are
               sorted by name AFTER they are built, so a child appended as a card of its own was
               scattered among the parents. From 2026-08-21 a child was keyed "<parent key>~<own name>"
               so the sort would file it beneath its parent - but the path was made of owner/stem while
               the parent's card sorts by its repo title, so no child ever landed under its parent,
               and DataFlex's collation would have filed one under the wrong top-level name anyway
               (probe CardOrder, 2026-09-29). Now a child goes INSIDE its root's card, in tree order -
               aiParentOf, aiRootOf and aiOrder below - as the library and system package rows do. */
            Move aObjDeps to aWork
            Move (SizeOfArray(aObjDeps) - 1) to iObjMax
            For i from 0 to iObjMax
                Get _PackageKeyOf  aObjDeps[i] to sNestKey
                Move (Uppercase(Trim(sNestKey)))  to asCarded[-1]
                Move 0  to aiDepth[-1]
                Move "" to asVia[-1]
                Move -1 to aiParentOf[-1]
                Move i  to aiRootOf[-1]
            Loop

            Move 0 to iWorkAt
            Move 0 to iGuard
            While (iWorkAt < SizeOfArray(aWork) and iGuard < 64)
                Increment iGuard
                Get ObjectDependencyManifest of hoInv sHome aWork[iWorkAt] to sNestSws
                If (sNestSws <> "") Begin
                    Get _PackageNameOf aWork[iWorkAt] to sVia
                    Get SwsJsonObjectDependencies of hoInv sNestSws to aNested
                    Move (SizeOfArray(aNested) - 1) to iNestMax
                    For j from 0 to iNestMax
                        Get _PackageKeyOf aNested[j] to sNestKey
                        Move (SearchArray((Uppercase(Trim(sNestKey))), asCarded)) to k
                        If (k = -1) Begin
                            Move (Uppercase(Trim(sNestKey))) to asCarded[-1]
                            Move aNested[j] to aWork[-1]
                            Move sVia      to asVia[-1]
                            Move (aiDepth[iWorkAt] + 1) to aiDepth[-1]
                            Move iWorkAt to aiParentOf[-1]
                            Move aiRootOf[iWorkAt] to aiRootOf[-1]
                        End
                    Loop
                End
                Increment iWorkAt
            Loop

            /* THE TRANSITIVE ROWS IN TREE ORDER - a package, then what it brought, depth first - so
               each is drawn right under its own parent. The walk above is breadth first (a queue), so
               the order is made here from each row's parent: a stack per direct package, a row's
               children pushed last-first so they come off in the order they were found. */
            For iNode from 0 to iObjMax
                Move (ResizeArray(aiStack, 0)) to aiStack
                Move iNode to aiStack[0]
                While (SizeOfArray(aiStack) > 0)
                    Move aiStack[SizeOfArray(aiStack) - 1] to k
                    Move (ResizeArray(aiStack, (SizeOfArray(aiStack) - 1))) to aiStack
                    If (k <> iNode) ;
                        Move k to aiOrder[-1]
                    Move (SizeOfArray(aWork) - 1) to iKid
                    While (iKid >= 0)
                        If (aiParentOf[iKid] = k) ;
                            Move iKid to aiStack[-1]
                        Decrement iKid
                    Loop
                Loop
            Loop

            // Everything after the direct entries is transitive - card it, without actions, in tree order.
            Move (SizeOfArray(aWork) - 1) to iMax
            For iNode from 0 to (SizeOfArray(aiOrder) - 1)
                Move aiOrder[iNode] to i
                // Named the way the direct rows are: the REPO the user fetched, not the .sws stem.
                Get _PackageNameOf aWork[i] to sNestName
                Get GitRepoFromSource  of hoInv (Trim(aWork[i].sSource)) to sNestKey
                Get GitOwnerFromSource of hoInv (Trim(aWork[i].sSource)) to sNestPub
                If (Trim(sNestKey) <> "") ;
                    Move (Trim(sNestKey)) to sNestName
                Get ObjectDependencyManifest of hoInv sHome aWork[i] to sNestSws
                Move "" to sNestDesc
                Move "" to sNestIcon
                Move "" to sNestVer
                If (sNestSws <> "") Begin
                    Get SwsManifestValue of hoInv sNestSws "description" to sNestDesc
                    Get SwsManifestValue of hoInv sNestSws "version"     to sNestVer
                    Get SwsManifestValue of hoInv sNestSws "icon"        to sNestIcon
                    // The icon is named RELATIVE to the manifest, so it resolves beside it.
                    If (Trim(sNestIcon) <> "") Begin
                        Move (Left(sNestSws, RightPos("\", sNestSws)) + Trim(sNestIcon)) to sNestIcon
                        If (vFilePathExists(sNestIcon) = 0) ;
                            Move "" to sNestIcon
                    End
                End
                Move False to bPkgIsCommit
                If (Trim(sNestVer) = "") Begin
                    Get ShortDependencyVersion of hoInv (Trim(aWork[i].sVersion)) to sNestVer
                    Move True to bPkgIsCommit
                End
                Move (CellHtml("", ((SpanOpen("ml-ok")) + CS_Check + ' installed' + CS_SpanEnd))) to sStateCell
                Move ((SpanOpen("ml-dim")) + (Esc(Self, Trim(aWork[i].sSource))) + CS_SpanEnd) to sNestPath
                // The whole point of the row: WHO brought it. Without this it reads as something
                // the user forgot they added.
                /* Indent by DEPTH, so a dependency of a dependency sits one step further in:
                   vWin32fh arrives under RDCToolsLib, which arrives under DUF, and one flat indent
                   would say those two are siblings. Depth is the number of "~" in the sort path,
                   capped at 3 so a deep graph cannot walk the cards off the panel. */
                Move aiDepth[i] to k
                If (k > 3) ;
                    Move 3 to k
                Move (" ml-c-dep ml-c-dep" + String(k)) to sRowCls
                // Toned down, and carried on the VERSION line (the sVia slot), not below it.
                Get _PkgMetaExtras hoInv sNestSws sWsDf to sExtras
                Move (sExtras + ((SpanOpen("ml-via")) + 'required by ') + (Esc(Self, asVia[i])) + CS_SpanEnd) to sChain
                /* DETAILS ON A TRANSITIVE ROW TOO, which is where it matters most: this is a package
                   nobody chose and nobody read the terms of. Uninstall and Update stay off these rows
                   (Nils 2026-08-21: "No Uninstall or Update on transitive rows") because neither would
                   be honest - df-cli owns the checkout and the pin lives in the PARENT's manifest.
                   Details changes nothing, so that reasoning does not reach it. */
                Move "" to sNestAct
                Move ((ClickableHtml("div", "ml-cx ml-cx-q", ('mldetails|G*' + (Esc(Self, Trim(aWork[i].sSource))) + '*' + (Esc(Self, Trim(aWork[i].sVersion))) + '*' + (Esc(Self, sNestSws))), "", "Details", "Licence, supported DataFlex versions, what it requires, and the full description."))) to sNestAct
                Get _CardHtml sNestName "git" sRowCls sNestPath sNestVer (If(bPkgIsCommit, "pkg commit", "pkg")) "" sStateCell "" "" "" sNestAct (Trim(aWork[i].sSws)) "" sNestDesc sNestIcon sNestPub sChain to sCard
                // Inside its root's card, like a nested library's (see iRootCard above).
                Move (asCardHtml[aiGitCardAt[aiRootOf[i]]] + sCard) to asCardHtml[aiGitCardAt[aiRootOf[i]]]
            Loop
            Send Destroy of hoInv

            /* SORTED BY NAME, which is what puts a local X next to a GitHub X - the entire reason
               for flattening. An insertion sort over two parallel arrays: the lists are a handful
               of entries, and SortArray on a struct array needs a Desktop comparator this class has
               no business acquiring.

               THE GUARD IS A SEPARATE "If", NOT "iSortAt >= 0 and ..." (2026-08-14). DataFlex does
               NOT short-circuit: both sides of an "and" are evaluated, so the combined form reads
               asCardKey[-1] on the last pass of every item that sorts ahead of everything before
               it - which, with cards arriving in tree order, happens on the very first comparison
               of an ordinary list. Whether an out-of-range read comes back empty or faults is not
               a property this code should be resting on either way.

               bKeepGoing carries the decision, so the index is only ever used once it is known to
               be in range. Equal keys stop the shift, which keeps the sort STABLE - two entries for
               the same library must stay in file order, or "entry 2" in a message names a different
               row than the second card on screen. */
            Move (SizeOfArray(asCardKey) - 1) to iCardMax
            For i from 1 to iCardMax
                Move asCardKey[i]  to sSortKey
                Move asCardHtml[i] to sCard
                Move (i - 1) to iSortAt
                Move True to bKeepGoing
                While (bKeepGoing = True)
                    If (iSortAt < 0) ;
                        Move False to bKeepGoing
                    Else If (asCardKey[iSortAt] <= sSortKey) ;
                        Move False to bKeepGoing
                    Else Begin
                        Move asCardKey[iSortAt]  to asCardKey[iSortAt + 1]
                        Move asCardHtml[iSortAt] to asCardHtml[iSortAt + 1]
                        Decrement iSortAt
                    End
                Loop
                Move sSortKey to asCardKey[iSortAt + 1]
                Move sCard    to asCardHtml[iSortAt + 1]
            Loop

            Move (DivOpen("ml-scroll ml-list")) to sRows
            For i from 0 to iCardMax
                Move (sRows + asCardHtml[i]) to sRows
            Loop
            Move (sRows + CS_DivEnd) to sRows
            Move (sHtml + sRows) to sHtml

            /* The one line under the table that says what the whole list adds up to, and NAMES the
               absence of analyzer data rather than letting it read as a clean bill: "no compiler
               messages" and "not analyzed yet" look identical in a count and mean opposite things. */
            /* COUNTED OVER THE WORKSPACE'S OWN ROWS. The chain rows are evidence, not scope: a
               workspace declaring one library that drags in seven would otherwise read "8", which
               is true of the build and false of the file the user is editing. The chain is reported
               as its own clause instead, so neither number pretends to be the other. */
            Get AttentionCount (&aInfo) (iSwsFormat = eSwsJson) to iAttn
            Get DirectCount (&aInfo) to iDirect
            Move (DivOpen("ml-note")) to sNote
            If (sTarget = "") Begin
                Move (sNote + ((BoldText("No compiler is selected")) + ', so there is no target version to judge against - the list above is what this workspace uses.')) to sNote
            End
            Else If (iAttn > 0) Begin
                Move (sNote + (BoldHtml(String(iAttn) + ' of ' + String(iDirect))) + ' need' + (If(iAttn = 1, "s", "")) + ' attention. Retargeting changes this workspace&apos;s [Libraries] entries - it is not a refactoring. Library code is maintained upstream and is never edited in place.') to sNote
            End
            Else Begin
                Move (sNote + ((SpanOpen("ok")) + 'All ') + String(iDirect) + (' are on the target version.' + CS_SpanEnd)) to sNote
            End
            /* THE CHAIN CLAUSE. A library's own libraries are compiled into this workspace just the
               same, so their count belongs on screen - and the indented rows are not editable here,
               which has to be said once rather than discovered by clicking. */
            /* THE GIT PACKAGES ARE NOT IN THE COUNT ABOVE: AttentionCount and DirectCount run over aInfo,
               which holds the path libraries and the DataFlex packages only - so the note says so. Until
               2026-09-29 it said they were "not listed above" (Nils: the note contradicted the list), which
               stopped being true when they got cards of their own on 2026-08-14. With no target there is no
               count to leave them out of. iObjDeps was read BEFORE hoInv was destroyed - see where it is
               fetched. */
            If (sTarget <> "") Begin
                If (iObjDeps = 1) ;
                    Move (sNote + ' The git package is not checked against the target version.') to sNote
                If (iObjDeps > 1) ;
                    Move (sNote + ' The ' + (BoldHtml(String(iObjDeps))) + ' git packages are not checked against the target version.') to sNote
            End
            If ((iMax + 1) > iDirect) ;
                Move (sNote + ' The indented rows are libraries those libraries declare - ' + String((iMax + 1) - iDirect) + ' of them. They are compiled into this workspace too, but they are declared in the libraries&apos; own .sws files, so they are shown here and not edited here.') to sNote
            If (bHaveMsgs = False) ;
                Move (sNote + ' Run a Check to see how many compiler messages come from each library.') to sNote
            If (bHaveUse = False) ;
                Move (sNote + (' ' + (BoldText("Find usages")) + ' reads every source file in scope to see which of them Use each library - the answer to what a retarget would cost you.')) to sNote
            Else ;
                Move (sNote + ' Usages are counted by FILE, and DIRECT Use only - a file reaching a library through another library belongs to that library, not to you.') to sNote
            Move (sHtml + sNote + CS_DivEnd) to sHtml

            // The footer is the framework buttons under the page since 2026-09-30 (RefreshButtons sets their states);
            // only the wrapper is closed here.
            Move (sHtml + CS_DivEnd) to sHtml
            Function_Return sHtml
        End_Function

        /* The verdict cell for one row - the report's own wording, kept verbatim so a user who
           learned it there reads the same sentence here. Always emits a complete <td>...</td>, which
           is what lets the caller graft the message count and the project link onto its tail. */
        Function StatusCellHtml Integer eStatus String sTarget Returns String
            /* A PACKAGE IS NOT JUDGED AGAINST THE TARGET. Its DataFlex compatibility is the
               df-min/df-max range in its own package block, which nothing here reads yet - so the
               cell says what it IS and stops. Claiming "no 26.0 build here" about a package would
               be a verdict on something unread, which is the failure this dialog exists to avoid.
               Its actions belong to df-cli (package install / uninstall / upgrade), not to us. */
            /* A PACKAGE THAT HAS NOT SAID is not a package that has said no. With no readable
               df-min/df-max the row states what it IS and stops - which was the whole of the
               verdict before the range could be read, and is still the right answer when it
               cannot be. */
            If (eStatus = eLibPackage) ;
                Function_Return ((CellHtml("st-pkg", 'package ' + CS_EmDash + ' managed by df-cli')))
            // ...and when it HAS said, and said no. Its own claim, so this is a verdict rather
            // than an inference - which is why it reads as firmly as the library ones.
            If (eStatus = eLibPackageMismatch) ;
                Function_Return ((CellHtml("st-bad", 'does not support DataFlex ' + sTarget)))
            If (eStatus = eLibMatchesTarget) ;
                Function_Return ((CellHtml("st-ok", 'already on ' + sTarget)))
            If (eStatus = eLibRetargetLocal) ;
                Function_Return ((CellHtml("st-warn", 'a ' + sTarget + ' build is in the same folder ' + CS_EmDash + ' repoint')))
            If (eStatus = eLibNoLocalBuild) ;
                Function_Return ((CellHtml("st-bad", 'no ' + sTarget + ' build here ' + CS_EmDash + ' needs a newer copy')))
            // There, but not readable as a workspace file: an unfinished merge (2026-09-14).
            If (eStatus = eLibUnreadable) ;
                Function_Return ((CellHtml("st-bad", 'unreadable ' + CS_EmDash + ' git conflict markers in the file; resolve that merge')))
            If (eStatus = eLibNotFound) ;
                Function_Return ((CellHtml("st-gone", 'not found ' + CS_EmDash + ' nothing is at that path')))
            Function_Return (CellHtml("st-none", 'no target set'))
        End_Function

        /* The supported-release range as one readable phrase, or "" when there is none.

           Both ends are optional and each shape says something different: "26.0" is a package
           pinned to one release, "25.0 - 26.0" spans two, "26.0 and later" has no upper bound, and
           "up to 26.0" no lower one. Writing them all as a bare pair would make the open-ended
           cases read as closed. */
        Function PackageRangeText String sMin String sMax Returns String
            Move (Trim(sMin)) to sMin
            Move (Trim(sMax)) to sMax
            If (sMin = "" and sMax = "") ;
                Function_Return ""
            If (sMin <> "" and sMax = "") ;
                Function_Return (sMin + " and later")
            If (sMin = "" and sMax <> "") ;
                Function_Return ("up to " + sMax)
            If (sMin = sMax) ;
                Function_Return sMin
            Function_Return (sMin + " - " + sMax)
        End_Function

        /* Rows that are not simply fine, counted over THE WORKSPACE'S OWN entries only.

           A missing entry counts: it is the most actionable state there is, and leaving it out of
           the tally is how the old report reported four of six.

           A BEHIND CHAIN COUNTS TOO, even when the library itself is on the target. That is the
           WinServ case exactly - WebAppSrc declares 25.0 and would have passed every test this
           dialog applied, while the seven libraries it drags in are on 19.1. A row whose own
           dependencies undercut it is not fine, and the tally is where that has to show. */
        Function AttentionCount tLibraryInfo[] ByRef aInfo Boolean bWsJson Returns Integer
            Integer i iMax iAttn

            Move 0 to iAttn
            Move (SizeOfArray(aInfo) - 1) to iMax
            For i from 0 to iMax
                If (aInfo[i].iDepth = 0) Begin
                    /* ON A DF26 WORKSPACE only what is broken in ANY format counts: an entry whose
                       file is gone, and a package that itself says it does not support the target
                       (df-min/df-max is the package's own claim, not a .sws version rule). The
                       version verdicts - retarget, no-local-build, chain-behind - are not formed
                       there at all, so counting them would put a number on judgments no card shows.
                       See the card build for the reasoning. */
                    If (bWsJson = True) Begin
                        If (aInfo[i].eStatus = eLibNotFound or aInfo[i].eStatus = eLibUnreadable or aInfo[i].eStatus = eLibPackageMismatch) ;
                            Increment iAttn
                    End
                    Else Begin
                        // eLibPackageMismatch counts: the package itself says it does not support
                        // the target, which is as actionable as a library with no build for it.
                        If (aInfo[i].eStatus = eLibRetargetLocal or aInfo[i].eStatus = eLibNoLocalBuild or aInfo[i].eStatus = eLibNotFound or aInfo[i].eStatus = eLibUnreadable or aInfo[i].eStatus = eLibPackageMismatch) ;
                            Increment iAttn
                        Else If (aInfo[i].iChainBehind > 0) ;
                            Increment iAttn
                    End
                End
            Loop
            Function_Return iAttn
        End_Function

        // How many rows the WORKSPACE itself declares - the denominator the user is editing, as
        // distinct from the total number of libraries the build ends up with.
        Function DirectCount tLibraryInfo[] ByRef aInfo Returns Integer
            Integer i iMax iN
            Move 0 to iN
            Move (SizeOfArray(aInfo) - 1) to iMax
            For i from 0 to iMax
                If (aInfo[i].iDepth = 0) ;
                    Increment iN
            Loop
            Function_Return iN
        End_Function

        /* Row 0: THE PAGE - the workspace line, the INI banner, the cards in their own scroll box and the note
           under them. Its heading is the page's title, beside the Libraries tile's glyph (stack). */
        Object oMLPage is a cFTHtmlPage
            Set piRowIndex to 0
            Set piColumnSpan to 123
            Set pbFillHeight to True        // the body fills the page; the cards scroll in their own box
            Set psLabel to "Libraries and packages used by this workspace"
            Set psCSSClass to "ftpage ftg-stack"
            // Design-time render only. Popup pushes the real body via UpdateHtml, which is what a
            // WARM re-open needs - a bare Set psHtml is read on the FIRST (cold) mount only.
            // DESIGN-TIME PLACEHOLDER for the Studio's Windows Designer, which models the LAST literal
            // psHtml it can parse. A SAMPLE, not the runtime seed (Nils 2026-09-29): with no workspace the
            // list is empty, so this is the real body for DFRefactor's own workspace, cut to three cards -
            // one of them nested - with the badge the dialog falls back to (Images/Default.ico) where each
            // package has its own icon, and without the tooltips and click ids, which the Designer never
            // shows. Tools\RegenerateDesignPlaceholders.py skips this box (SKIP_BOX): edit it by hand.
            // The runtime expression sits in End_Construct_Object below.
            Set psHtml to """
            <link rel="stylesheet" href="RDCFlexTron/palette.css">
            <link rel="stylesheet" href="RDCFlexTron/buttons.css">
            <link rel="stylesheet" href="RDCFlexTron/bars.css">
            <link rel="stylesheet" href="CssStyle/libraries.css">
            <div class="ml-wrap">
                <div class="ml-sub"><b>DFRefactorFlexTron.sws</b> &middot; migrating to DataFlex 26.0 &middot; the Version column is what each library declares for ITSELF; <span
                    class="behind">amber</span> is older than the target - a candidate to bring forward.</div>
                <div class="ml-scroll ml-list">
                    <div class="ml-card">
                        <div class="ml-cg ml-cg-git" data-i="L" style="background:transparent;width:32px;height:32px;flex:none;"><img class="ml-cgd" src="Images/Default.ico" alt=""
                            style="width:100%;height:100%;object-fit:contain;border-radius:4px;" onerror="var p=this.parentNode;p.removeChild(this);p.style.background=&quot;&quot;;p.textContent=p.dataset.i;"></div>
                        <div class="ml-ct">
                            <div class="ml-cn">Library-DUF<span class="ml-tag">git</span></div>
                            <div class="ml-cd">Database Update Framework: versioned, repeatable database schema updates for DataFlex.</div>
                            <div class="ml-cs"><span class="ml-dim">https:/&#47;github.com/NilsSve/Library-DUF.git</span></div>
                            <div class="ml-cm"><span><span class="ml-gl">&#128100;&#65038;</span>NilsSve</span><span><span class="ml-gl">&#10697;</span><code class="pkg">1.0.0</code></span><span><span
                                class="ml-gl">&#9878;&#65038;</span>MIT</span><span><span class="ml-gl">&#8618;</span>requires Library-DFAbout, Library-RDCToolsLib, Library-vwin32fh</span></div></div>
                        <div class="ml-cx ml-cx-q">Details</div>
                        <div class="ml-cx ml-cx-off">Update</div>
                        <div class="ml-cx">Uninstall</div></div>
                    <div class="ml-card">
                        <div class="ml-cg ml-cg-cloud" data-i="W" style="background:transparent;width:32px;height:32px;flex:none;"><img class="ml-cgd" src="Images/Default.ico" alt=""
                            style="width:100%;height:100%;object-fit:contain;border-radius:4px;" onerror="var p=this.parentNode;p.removeChild(this);p.style.background=&quot;&quot;;p.textContent=p.dataset.i;"></div>
                        <div class="ml-ct">
                            <div class="ml-cn">Web UI FlexTron<span class="ml-tag">git</span></div>
                            <div class="ml-cd">Use Web UI controls within Windows Applications.</div>
                            <div class="ml-cs"><span class="ml-plink ml-plink-cool">C:\ProgramData\DataFlex\Packages\Cache\DataFlex_dev_Web_UI_FlexTron-1.0.54\df.sws</span></div>
                            <div class="ml-cm"><span><span class="ml-gl">&#128100;&#65038;</span>DataFlex-dev</span><span><span class="ml-gl">&#10697;</span><code
                                class="pkg">1.0.54</code></span><span><span class="ml-gl">&#9878;&#65038;</span>DocumentRef-DataFlex</span><span><span class="ml-gl">&#9881;&#65038;</span>DataFlex up to 26.0</span></div>
                            <div class="chainok">declares 1 library of its own</div></div>
                        <div class="ml-cx ml-cx-q">Details</div>
                        <div class="ml-cx ml-cx-off">Update</div>
                        <div class="ml-cx">Uninstall</div></div>
                    <div class="ml-card ml-c-dep ml-c-dep1">
                        <div class="ml-cg ml-cg-nested" data-i="W" style="background:transparent;width:32px;height:32px;flex:none;"><img class="ml-cgd" src="Images/Default.ico" alt=""
                            style="width:100%;height:100%;object-fit:contain;border-radius:4px;" onerror="var p=this.parentNode;p.removeChild(this);p.style.background=&quot;&quot;;p.textContent=p.dataset.i;"></div>
                        <div class="ml-ct">
                            <div class="ml-cn">Web UI<span class="ml-tag">nested</span></div>
                            <div class="ml-cd">Core of the Web UI Framework.</div>
                            <div class="ml-cs"><span class="ml-plink ml-plink-cool">C:\ProgramData\DataFlex\Packages\Cache\DataFlex_dev_Web_UI-1.0.54\df.sws</span></div>
                            <div class="ml-cm"><span><span class="ml-gl">&#10697;</span><code class="pkg">1.0.54</code></span><span><span
                                class="ml-gl">&#9878;&#65038;</span>DocumentRef-DataFlex</span><span><span class="ml-gl">&#9881;&#65038;</span>DataFlex up to 26.0</span></div></div>
                        <div class="ml-cx ml-cx-q">Remove</div></div></div>
                <div class="ml-note"><span class="ok">All 2 are on the target version.</span> The <b>6</b> git packages are not checked against the target version. The indented rows are libraries
                    those libraries declare - 4 of them. They are compiled into this workspace too, but they are declared in the libraries&apos; own .sws files, so they are shown here and not edited
                    here. Run a Check to see how many compiler messages come from each library. <b>Find usages</b> reads every source file in scope to see which of them Use each library - the answer to what a retarget would cost you.</div></div>
            """

            // RUNTIME content - here rather than in the object body, so the Designer keeps the literal above.
            Procedure End_Construct_Object
                Forward Send End_Construct_Object
                Set psHtml to (BodyHtml(Self))
            End_Procedure

            /* A click on the page does its work here, after the web click has returned (the library's click
               relay): every action either pops a modal (a warning box, the details) or repaints the body, and
               doing that from inside the WebView2 pump faults err 4410 - the wall every FT modal in this codebase
               documents. Row selection too: it repaints the body, which is no thing to do underneath the click
               that is still being handled. Only OUR ids act; a cWebHtmlBox click also reaches sibling handlers.
               The footer's buttons are framework buttons since 2026-09-30, with their work in OnButtonClick.

               The two BODY-LINK ids hand off to ShellExecute, which returns immediately; neither pops a modal
               nor ends the panel, so the dialog deliberately stays up: a report can point at several places
               in turn without losing the user's place, and without discarding their edits. "cfmdir:" /
               "cfmurl:" keep the names ConfirmDialogFT gave them, because PathLinkHtml and UsageCellHtml EMIT
               those ids and both are still borrowed from ghoLibraryReport. Each target is guarded at the far
               end: RDCRevealInExplorer refuses a path that is not on disk, and RDCOpenUrl refuses anything that
               is not http/https.

               THE PAYLOAD RIDES IN sId after a "|" - the framework does not split an id on "|" and hand the
               tail over as sParam (a chip that assumed it did looked live and did nothing, Nils 2026-08-21).
               The prefixes are COUNTED: "mlnestdel|" and "mldetails|" are ten characters, the rest nine - a
               nine-character prefix tested against ten once silently produced a dead button. */
            Procedure OnHtmlClick String sId String sParam
                Handle hoDlg

                Move (oMaintainLibraries_dg(Self)) to hoDlg
                If (Left(sId, 7) = "cfmdir:") ;
                    Send RDCRevealInExplorer (Mid(sId, (Length(sId) - 7), 8))
                Else If (Left(sId, 7) = "cfmurl:") ;
                    Send RDCOpenUrl (Mid(sId, (Length(sId) - 7), 8))
                Else If (Left(sId, 10) = "mlnestdel|") ;
                    Send DoRemoveNested of hoDlg (Mid(sId, (Length(sId) - 10), 11))
                Else If (Left(sId, 10) = "mldetails|") ;
                    Send DoShowPackageDetails of hoDlg (Mid(sId, (Length(sId) - 10), 11))
                Else If (Left(sId, 9) = "mlchange|") ;
                    Send DoChangeIndex of hoDlg (Mid(sId, (Length(sId) - 9), 10))
                Else If (Left(sId, 9) = "mlrowdel|") ;
                    Send DoRemoveIndex of hoDlg (Mid(sId, (Length(sId) - 9), 10))
                // Every package action goes through df-cli, which is DF 26+ - see the Add Package button.
                Else If (Left(sId, 9) = "mlpkgdel|") ;
                    Send DoUninstallNamedPackage of hoDlg (Mid(sId, (Length(sId) - 9), 10))
                Else If (Left(sId, 9) = "mlpkgupd|") ;
                    Send DoUpgradeNamedPackage of hoDlg (Mid(sId, (Length(sId) - 9), 10))
                // Guarded with the banner that emits it - see the note at the "ml-ini" block above.
                Else If (sId = "mlconvert") ;
                    Send DoConvertFromBanner of hoDlg
                Else If (sId = "mlrow") ;
                    Send SelectRow of hoDlg sParam
                Else If (sId = "mlremove") ;
                    Send DoRemove of hoDlg
            End_Procedure
        End_Object

        /* Row 2: THE BUTTONS - framework buttons (cFTWebButton, RDCFlexTron) on 8 px columns, the library actions
           at the left and OK and Cancel at the right. Each one's work runs in its OnButtonClick, after the web
           click has returned (the library's click relay), so a file picker, a message box or Close_Panel can
           run there. RefreshButtons sets their states on every repaint, and says why. Update Check and Retire
           folders show only when they apply, so they come LAST in the left group, where a hidden one leaves no
           gap; until 2026-09-30 the order was Update Check, Retire folders, Find usages. Spans fit the captions
           (Add Library... ~112 px, Add Package... ~119, Browse Packages... ~141, Find usages ~105, Update Check
           ~117, Retire folders ~113, OK ~59, Cancel ~78, measured 2026-09-30).

           NO REMOVE BUTTON HERE: every card carries its own action - Remove for a list entry, Uninstall for a
           package - so a footer button acting on "the selected row" would be a control with nothing to act on;
           the cards do not set a selection. piSelected and SelectRow survive as the highlight only. */

        /* ADD LIBRARY... is the .sws picker on both formats - a library on disk is a library on disk, whatever
           the workspace format. THREE DOORS ON DF26, ONE ON INI (Nils 2026-08-16, the decision that stands):
           Add Library..., Add Package... and Browse Packages... are separate - clearer, and what the tour
           shows. */
        Object oMLAddLibBtn is a cFTWebButton
            Set piRowIndex to 2
            Set piColumnIndex to 0
            Set piColumnSpan to 16
            Set psCaption to "Add Library..."
            Set psCSSClass to "ftbtn ftg-add"
            Set psToolTip to "Add a library by choosing its workspace (.sws) file. The entry is written relative to this workspace whenever the library sits inside it or beside it, so the reference survives being copied."

            Procedure OnButtonClick
                Send DoAdd of (oMaintainLibraries_dg(Self))
            End_Procedure
        End_Object

        // ADD PACKAGE... - the one-field dialog: a publisher/package#version, or a git url. Its tooltip is RefreshButtons'.
        Object oMLAddPkgBtn is a cFTWebButton
            Set piRowIndex to 2
            Set piColumnIndex to 16
            Set piColumnSpan to 17
            Set psCaption to "Add Package..."
            Set psCSSClass to "ftbtn ftg-add"

            Procedure OnButtonClick
                Send DoAddPackage of (oMaintainLibraries_dg(Self))
            End_Procedure
        End_Object

        /* BROWSE PACKAGES..., formerly "Search Registry..." (Nils 2026-08-15: "Search Registry makes one think of
           Windows Registry - which this is not"). It browses: no search-term prompt - the click goes straight to
           the full list of what the package manager offers this workspace, where a click installs
           (DoSearchPackages). Its tooltip is RefreshButtons'. */
        Object oMLBrowseBtn is a cFTWebButton
            Set piRowIndex to 2
            Set piColumnIndex to 33
            Set piColumnSpan to 19
            Set psCaption to "Browse Packages..."
            Set psCSSClass to "ftbtn ftg-scan"

            Procedure OnButtonClick
                Send DoSearchPackages of (oMaintainLibraries_dg(Self))
            End_Procedure
        End_Object

        // FIND USAGES - what a retarget of a [Libraries] entry would cost. Its tooltip is RefreshButtons'.
        Object oMLUsagesBtn is a cFTWebButton
            Set piRowIndex to 2
            Set piColumnIndex to 52
            Set piColumnSpan to 15
            Set psCaption to "Find usages"
            Set psCSSClass to "ftbtn ftg-scan"

            Procedure OnButtonClick
                Send DoFindUsages of (oMaintainLibraries_dg(Self))
            End_Procedure
        End_Object

        // UPDATE CHECK - shown on a DataFlex 26 workspace only (RefreshButtons).
        Object oMLUpdChkBtn is a cFTWebButton
            Set piRowIndex to 2
            Set piColumnIndex to 67
            Set piColumnSpan to 16
            Set psCaption to "Update Check"
            Set psCSSClass to "ftbtn ftg-refresh"
            Set pbRender to False
            Set psToolTip to "Ask what newer versions are available, without changing anything. Registry packages are checked against what this workspace's DataFlex version allows; git packages against their branch head."

            Procedure OnButtonClick
                Send DoUpdateCheck of (oMaintainLibraries_dg(Self))
            End_Procedure
        End_Object

        // RETIRE FOLDERS - shown when there is a folder to retire (RefreshButtons), and its tooltip names how many.
        Object oMLRetireBtn is a cFTWebButton
            Set piRowIndex to 2
            Set piColumnIndex to 83
            Set piColumnSpan to 16
            Set psCaption to "Retire folders"
            Set psCSSClass to "ftbtn ftg-eraser"
            Set pbRender to False

            Procedure OnButtonClick
                Send DoRetireStaleLibraryFolders of (oMaintainLibraries_dg(Self))
            End_Procedure
        End_Object

        // Column 99, empty, takes the extra width. OK writes the list back; Cancel closes without writing it.
        Object oMLOkBtn is a cFTWebButton
            Set piRowIndex to 2
            Set piColumnIndex to 100
            Set piColumnSpan to 11
            Set psCaption to "OK"
            Set psCSSClass to "ftbtn ftg-check"
            Set psToolTip to "Write this list back to the workspace file (.sws). No backup copy of it is made."

            Procedure OnButtonClick
                Send DoOk of (oMaintainLibraries_dg(Self))
            End_Procedure
        End_Object

        Object oMLCancelBtn is a cFTWebButton
            Set piRowIndex to 2
            Set piColumnIndex to 111
            Set piColumnSpan to 12
            Set psCaption to "Cancel"
            Set psCSSClass to "ftbtn ftg-close"
            Set psToolTip to "Close without writing the list changes that are waiting for OK. A package added, updated or uninstalled here, or a conversion to the DataFlex 26 format, was written when it happened and stays."

            Procedure OnButtonClick
                Send DoCancel of (oMaintainLibraries_dg(Self))
            End_Procedure
        End_Object
    End_Object

    // cFTModalPanel fits the host to the panel, when it is shown and on every resize.
    Set phoClientHost to (oWebHost(Self))


    /* REPAINT FROM THE WORKING LIST. UpdateHtml keeps the page for a mount still to come and pushes it to a
       page that is up; CheckUpdateWebUI makes the push immediate.

       Why the stored copy matters here (2026-08-16, Nils: OrderEntry's packages still showing after
       switching to WinServ and re-opening this dialog): this once pushed the LIVE channel alone and never
       stored psHtml, which is what a REMOUNTING page serves. Popup arms a deferred repaint; on a workspace
       switch the WebView2 host tears down and remounts for the new showing, the remount served the stored
       psHtml - the PREVIOUS render, OrderEntry's list - and the deferred UpdateHtml either landed before
       the mount (lost) or raced it. Every property on this dialog was correctly reset; only the picture was
       stale. Activating below re-pushes once the panel is genuinely up, the same belt to this suspender. */
    Procedure RefreshBody
        Send UpdateHtml of (oMLPage(Self)) (BodyHtml(oWebHost(Self)))
        Send RefreshButtons
        Send CheckUpdateWebUI of (oWebHost(Self))
    End_Procedure

    /* THE BUTTONS' STATES, from the workspace (2026-09-30). BodyHtml drew them into the page's footer until
       then; as framework buttons they are set here, from RefreshBody - every repaint, so every moment one can
       change: a conversion, a package action, a retire.

       THE FORMAT DECIDES THREE OF THEM, and a dead button is DIM with the reason in its tooltip, never
       live-looking and inert:
         Add Package... and Browse Packages... are live on an INI workspace too (Nils 2026-09-03): the format a
           package needs is one df-cli step away, so the click converts first and carries on (DoAddPackage ->
           _ConvertFirstFor). They are shut only when there is no workspace file at all, and the tooltip says
           so in the words of the ACTUAL reason - "an INI workspace has nowhere to record one" was a lie there,
           with no banner and nothing to convert.
         Find usages is shut on a DataFlex 26 workspace (Nils 2026-08-15): usage counts answer what a retarget
           of a [Libraries] entry would cost, and there each dependency pins its own version.

       TWO SHOW ONLY WHEN THEY APPLY:
         Update Check, on a DataFlex 26 workspace: it asks about installed packages, which an INI workspace has
           none of. ONE check for the whole dialog, never a poll (Nils 2026-08-21) - df-cli's search returns the
           whole compatible table in a single call; the row chips stay dead until it has run.
         Retire folders, when there is something to retire (Nils 2026-09-11, who nearly missed it among the
           others): no greyed stand-in on a tidy workspace. The count is the Libraries tile's memo (piLibStale on
           ghoLibraryReport), so the bar pays nothing for it, and it is the LOADED workspace's - the copy flow
           opens this dialog on a copy that may not be loaded, where a retire would rename the other workspace's
           folders. The question at close (OfferRetireAfterClose) is the main door; this is for a workspace that
           arrived already in this state, or a question that was declined. */
    Procedure RefreshButtons
        Handle hoHost hoInv hoAddPkg hoBrowse hoUsages
        String sSws sNoFile
        Integer iFormat iStale
        Boolean bLoaded

        Get psSwsFile to sSws
        Get Create (RefClass(cLibraryInventoryApp)) to hoInv
        Get SwsFormatOf of hoInv sSws to iFormat
        Send Destroy of hoInv
        Move 0 to iStale
        Get _IsLoadedWorkspace to bLoaded
        If (bLoaded = True) ;
            Get StaleLibraryFolderCount of ghoLibraryReport to iStale
        Move (oWebHost(Self)) to hoHost
        Move (oMLAddPkgBtn(hoHost)) to hoAddPkg
        Move (oMLBrowseBtn(hoHost)) to hoBrowse
        Move (oMLUsagesBtn(hoHost)) to hoUsages

        If (iFormat <> eSwsMissing) Begin
            Send UpdateEnabled of hoAddPkg True
            Send UpdateToolTip of hoAddPkg "Add a package by naming it: a publisher/package#version for a DataFlex Package Manager package, or a git url. Recorded as a dependency and fetched by df-cli where needed. On an INI workspace the file is converted to the DataFlex 26 JSON format first, then this continues."
            Send UpdateEnabled of hoBrowse True
            Send UpdateToolTip of hoBrowse "List the packages the DataFlex package manager offers this workspace - a click on one installs it. On an INI workspace the file is converted to the DataFlex 26 JSON format first."
        End
        Else Begin
            Move "No workspace file was found for this dialog, so there is nothing to add a package to. Open a workspace first." to sNoFile
            Send UpdateEnabled of hoAddPkg False
            Send UpdateToolTip of hoAddPkg sNoFile
            Send UpdateEnabled of hoBrowse False
            Send UpdateToolTip of hoBrowse sNoFile
        End
        If (iFormat = eSwsJson) Begin
            Send UpdateEnabled of hoUsages False
            Send UpdateToolTip of hoUsages "Usage counts answer what RETARGETING a [Libraries] entry would cost, and a DataFlex 26 workspace has no retargeting - each dependency pins its own version."
        End
        Else Begin
            Send UpdateEnabled of hoUsages True
            Send UpdateToolTip of hoUsages "Read every source file in scope and count which of them Use each library. Not done on open, because it reads the whole workspace."
        End
        Send UpdateRender  of (oMLUpdChkBtn(hoHost)) (iFormat = eSwsJson)
        Send UpdateRender  of (oMLRetireBtn(hoHost)) (iStale > 0)
        Send UpdateToolTip of (oMLRetireBtn(hoHost)) ("Rename the " + String(iStale) + " library folder(s) a package has replaced to <name>.OLD, so the compiler cannot find them. Nothing is deleted.")
    End_Procedure

    // Re-push once the panel is active - the one moment a flush is guaranteed to stick, and the
    // fix for a deferred repaint that fired before or during the remount.
    Procedure Activating
        Forward Send Activating
        Send RefreshBody
    End_Procedure

    Procedure SelectRow String sIndex
        Integer i
        Move (Integer(sIndex)) to i
        Set piSelected to i
        Send RefreshBody
    End_Procedure

    /* THE LIBRARY .sws PICKER DoAdd and DoChangeIndex share: the open dialog started in sStartIn, the full path
       chosen (File_Name, not File_Title: the entry has to be the full path - File_Title is the leaf only, which
       would resolve against the workspace home and point at a file that is not there) and the one rule - a
       library is referenced by its WORKSPACE file, so the file chosen has to be a .sws. "" when the dialog was
       cancelled or the choice refused (the refusal says why). */
    Function _BrowseForLibrarySws String sStartIn Returns String
        Handle hoDlg
        Boolean bOpened
        String sChosen

        Move (oOpenLibrarySwsDialog(Self)) to hoDlg
        Set Initial_Folder of hoDlg to sStartIn
        Get Show_Dialog of hoDlg to bOpened
        If (bOpened = False) ;
            Function_Return ""
        Get File_Name of hoDlg to sChosen
        If (Trim(sChosen) = "") ;
            Function_Return ""
        If (Lowercase(Right(Trim(sChosen), 4)) <> ".sws") Begin
            Send FTStopBox "A library is referenced by its WORKSPACE file, so the file chosen has to be a .sws. Pick the library's .sws - it usually sits in the library's own folder beside its source." "Not a workspace file"
            Function_Return ""
        End
        Function_Return sChosen
    End_Function

    /* A DEV / DEMO / SAMPLE .sws is the trap worth stopping on, and it is easy to walk into: libraries ship
       several .sws files side by side and the roles are only visible in the name. RDCToolsLibDev25.0.sws
       builds the library; RDCToolsLibLibrary25.0.sws is the one a consumer references. Referencing the Dev
       file drags the library's own build workspace into this one. Asked rather than refused - it is legal,
       and a library author doing exactly this on purpose should not be blocked. Defaults to No. True when
       the file is the consumable kind, or the user takes it anyway; sVerb is the question's verb ("Add" /
       "Use"). */
    Function _AcceptLibrarySwsRole Handle hoInv String sChosen String sVerb Returns Boolean
        String sLeaf
        Integer iPos iRetval

        Move (RightPos("\", sChosen)) to iPos
        Move sChosen to sLeaf
        If (iPos > 0) ;
            Move (Right(sChosen, (Length(sChosen) - iPos))) to sLeaf
        If (IsConsumableLibrarySws(hoInv, sLeaf) = True) ;
            Function_Return True
        Get FTYesNoBox (sLeaf + " looks like a library's DEVELOPMENT, demo or sample workspace rather than the one meant for consumers." + Character(10) + Character(10) + "Libraries normally ship both side by side - the consumable one usually carries 'Library' in its name. Referencing the wrong file pulls the library's own build workspace into this one." + Character(10) + Character(10) + sVerb + " it anyway?") "Check the workspace role" MB_DEFBUTTON2 to iRetval
        Function_Return (iRetval = MBR_Yes)
    End_Function

    /* ADD - choose a library .sws, then check the four things that make an entry wrong rather than
       merely unusual. Each refusal names what is wrong, because "nothing happened" is the one
       response a user cannot act on. */
    Procedure DoAdd
        String[] asEntries
        String sChosen sSws sHome sEntry sExisting sResolved
        Integer i iMax
        Boolean bAccepted
        Handle hoInv

        Get psSwsFile to sSws
        If (sSws = "") ;
            Procedure_Return
        Get _FolderOf sSws to sHome

        Get _BrowseForLibrarySws sHome to sChosen
        If (sChosen = "") ;
            Procedure_Return

        // ITS OWN .sws - a workspace listing itself as a library is a loop, and the Studio will not
        // thank anyone for it.
        Get Create (RefClass(cLibraryInventoryApp)) to hoInv
        Get NormalizePath of hoInv sChosen to sResolved
        Get NormalizePath of hoInv sSws to sExisting
        If (Lowercase(sResolved) = Lowercase(sExisting)) Begin
            Send Destroy of hoInv
            Send FTStopBox "That is this workspace's own .sws file. A workspace cannot be a library of itself." "Cannot add this workspace"
            Procedure_Return
        End

        // ALREADY LISTED - compared on the RESOLVED path, because the same library can be named two
        // ways ("Libraries\X\X.sws" and an absolute path) and a text comparison would let a
        // duplicate through.
        Get pasEntries to asEntries
        Move (SizeOfArray(asEntries) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            Get ResolveLibraryPath of hoInv asEntries[i] sHome to sExisting
            Get NormalizePath of hoInv sExisting to sExisting
            If (Lowercase(sExisting) = Lowercase(sResolved)) Begin
                Send Destroy of hoInv
                /* POINT AT THE CARD, do not just name it (Nils 2026-08-14).

                   Naming the entry was not enough, and the way it failed is worth keeping: the list
                   is sorted by name, so two entries for one library sit TOGETHER - and if that name
                   sorts late they are both at the bottom, out of sight. Nils read "already in the
                   list, as entry 2", could not see it, and reasonably concluded the list was broken.
                   Two rounds went into hunting a missing card that was on screen the whole time.

                   So the row is SELECTED before the message shows: it comes back highlighted, and
                   the text says where to look rather than leaving the reader to find it. piSelected
                   indexes the working list, which is what i counts here - depth-0 rows are emitted
                   in entry order, so the two agree.

                   Highlighting, not scrolling: this HTML lives in the web framework's DOM, where an
                   injected <script> does not reliably run, and a half-working scroll would be worse
                   than an honest "it is highlighted below". */
                Set piSelected to i
                Send RefreshBody
                Send FTStopBox ("That library is already in the list, as entry " + String(i + 1) + " of " + String(iMax + 1) + ":" + Character(10) + Character(10) + asEntries[i] + Character(10) + Character(10) + "It is highlighted in the list. The list is sorted by name, so it may be further down than you expect - two entries for the same library sit next to each other.") "Already listed"
                Procedure_Return
            End
        Loop

        // The dev / demo / sample trap - see _AcceptLibrarySwsRole.
        Get _AcceptLibrarySwsRole hoInv sChosen "Add" to bAccepted
        If (bAccepted = False) Begin
            Send Destroy of hoInv
            Procedure_Return
        End

        // Written RELATIVE where that resolves - the reference then survives this workspace being
        // copied, junctioned or cloned, which is the premise of the whole migration flow.
        Get RelativeLibraryEntry of hoInv sChosen sHome to sEntry
        Send Destroy of hoInv

        Move sEntry to asEntries[-1]
        Set pasEntries to asEntries
        Set piSelected to (SizeOfArray(asEntries) - 1)
        Set pbDirty to True
        Send RefreshBody
    End_Procedure

    /* REMOVE - the selected row, immediately and without a question of its own. Nothing has been
       written yet, Cancel throws the whole edit away, and OK is the one commit point - so a
       confirmation here would be asking about a change that does not exist. */
    Procedure DoRemove
        String[] asEntries asOut
        String sEntry
        Integer i iMax iSel iKind
        Handle hoInv

        Get piSelected to iSel
        Get pasEntries to asEntries
        Move (SizeOfArray(asEntries) - 1) to iMax
        If (iSel < 0 or iSel > iMax) ;
            Procedure_Return

        /* A PACKAGE IS NOT OURS TO DROP FROM THE LIST. Removing its string by hand looks like an
           uninstall and is not one: the cache entry stays, its own dependencies stay, and df-cli's
           check that nothing else still needs it never runs. Add already goes through df-cli; this
           is the half that did not, and a workspace could be left in a state this tool created. */
        Move asEntries[iSel] to sEntry
        Get Create (RefClass(cLibraryInventoryApp)) to hoInv
        Get DependencyKind of hoInv sEntry to iKind
        Send Destroy of hoInv
        If (iKind = eDepPackage) Begin
            Send DoUninstallPackage sEntry
            Procedure_Return
        End

        For i from 0 to iMax
            If (i <> iSel) ;
                Move asEntries[i] to asOut[-1]
        Loop
        Set pasEntries to asOut
        // Keep a row selected where there still is one, so a second Remove needs no second click.
        If (iSel > (SizeOfArray(asOut) - 1)) ;
            Move (SizeOfArray(asOut) - 1) to iSel
        Set piSelected to iSel
        Set pbDirty to True
        Send RefreshBody
    End_Procedure

    /* CONVERT this workspace's .sws from the INI format to DataFlex 26's JSON, through df-cli.

       Offered rather than merely described (Nils 2026-08-14). The old dead end printed the df-cli
       command and left the user to run it in a console - for a workspace DFRefactor had just copied,
       which is precisely the case where it should be able to finish the job.

       WHICH VERSION. Packages need 26 or later, so that is the floor; a higher target wins. Read
       from the application's target rather than hardcoded, so a DF27 workspace is not written as 26.

       SAVES FIRST, for the same reason DoAddPackage does: df-cli rewrites the file, so anything held
       in memory would be written back over the conversion at OK. Re-reads afterwards, because after
       the rewrite the [Libraries] section this dialog was editing does not exist any more - it is a
       "dependencies" array now.

       THE VERDICT COMES FROM THE FILE, never the exit code - the only question that matters is
       whether the .sws is JSON now, and the file is the one witness that cannot be wrong.

       BOUNDED. df-cli asks "Failed to load workspace. Would you like to attempt a repair? (y/n):"
       when the library chain does not resolve, and a plain RDCRunProgramWait hangs the application on
       it for ever ([[RDCRunProgramBounded]] and the note there). A prompt is a fast failure here, and
       the failure message names the entries that most likely caused it. */
    Function ConvertSwsToJson Returns Boolean
        String  sSws sTarget sDfCli
        Integer iFormat
        Boolean bOk
        Handle  hoInv

        Get psSwsFile to sSws
        If (sSws = "") ;
            Function_Return False

        Get Create (RefClass(cLibraryInventoryApp)) to hoInv
        Get SwsFormatOf of hoInv sSws to iFormat
        If (iFormat = eSwsJson) Begin
            Send Destroy of hoInv
            Function_Return True                    // already JSON - nothing to do, and not an error
        End
        Get psTargetDfVersion of ghoApplication to sTarget
        Move (Trim(sTarget)) to sTarget
        If (sTarget = "") ;
            Move "26.0" to sTarget
        If (IsVersionBelow(hoInv, sTarget, "26.0") = True) ;
            Move "26.0" to sTarget
        Send Destroy of hoInv

        Get DfCliPath to sDfCli
        If (sDfCli = "") ;
            Function_Return False                   // the caller explains; this only converts

        // COMMIT FIRST - df-cli owns the file from here.
        Get pbDirty to bOk
        If (bOk = True) Begin
            Get SaveEntries to bOk
            If (bOk = False) ;
                Function_Return False
        End

        /* HANDED TO THE CONVERSION DIALOG (Nils 2026-08-14: "the conversion to Json deserves its
           own dialog ... how about showing output from that process").

           Everything the status panel could not do, that dialog does: the run is polled from a
           timer instead of a blocking loop, so the UI stays live and its progress bar - pure CSS -
           actually moves; and df-cli's own output is tailed into it as it arrives, which explains
           a refusal far better than anything this program can say at second hand.

           The verdict still comes from the FILE, on both sides of the call. */
        Get ConvertWorkspaceToJson sSws sTarget to bOk

        Get Create (RefClass(cLibraryInventoryApp)) to hoInv
        Get SwsFormatOf of hoInv sSws to iFormat
        Send Destroy of hoInv
        Move (iFormat = eSwsJson) to bOk

        If (bOk = True) Begin
            // The file is a different shape now - everything held about it is a guess.
            Send ReloadEntriesFromFile
            Send RefreshBody
            Function_Return True
        End

        /* NO SECOND DIALOG WHEN IT FAILS. ConvertWorkspaceToJson has already shown df-cli's own
           output and a verdict under it - which says more than anything this could add - and a
           modal opened from the return path of the one just closed is exactly how the confirm
           dialog's web host got broken ("Could not find object oCfmBody_hb"). The caller simply
           learns it did not convert; the banner stays up, which is the standing reminder. */
        Function_Return False
    End_Function

    /* The banner's own action. No message of its own either way: the conversion dialog shows the
       run and states the outcome before it closes, and ConvertSwsToJson repaints this body - so a
       success is visible as the banner disappearing, and a failure as it staying put. */
    // DF 26+ ONLY - every line below goes through df-cli. Guarded with its buttons and dispatch (Nils 2026-08-19).
    Procedure DoConvertFromBanner
        Set piResumeAfterConvert to 0
        Set pbConvertRetried to False
        Send DoConvertThenResume
    End_Procedure

    /* THE CONVERSION, OFFERED ON THE WAY OUT (Nils 2026-10-07: "after adding all the needed libraries
       there were no offer made to convert to JSON when the Libraries dialog was closed"). The banner
       says it while the dialog is open, but the moment the list is right - the libraries added, the
       dialog closed - is the moment a conversion that failed during the copy can succeed, and nothing
       asked. So the close asks, once per workspace per session (psConvertOfferedWS, latched before the
       question, so "Not now" holds), and only when it can help: the file is still INI, the target is
       DataFlex 26 or later - the JSON format is a DataFlex 26 one - and df-cli is there to do it.
       Armed on oMLDeferTimer by MaintainWorkspaceLibraries, so it opens a tick after the dialog has
       gone, never off the back of its close. The conversion dialog states its own verdict; nothing is
       shown after it. */
    Procedure DoOfferConvertAfterClose String sSws
        String sTarget sHtml sSwsEsc
        Integer iFormat
        Boolean bBelow bOk
        Handle hoInv

        If (sSws = "" or sSws = psConvertOfferedWS(Self)) ;
            Procedure_Return
        Get psTargetDfVersion of ghoApplication to sTarget
        Move (Trim(sTarget)) to sTarget
        If (sTarget = "") ;
            Procedure_Return
        If (DfCliPath() = "") ;
            Procedure_Return
        Get Create (RefClass(cLibraryInventoryApp)) to hoInv
        Get SwsFormatOf of hoInv sSws to iFormat
        Get IsVersionBelow of hoInv sTarget "26.0" to bBelow
        Send Destroy of hoInv
        If (iFormat <> eSwsIni or bBelow = True) ;
            Procedure_Return
        Set psConvertOfferedWS to sSws
        // The file on its own line, as _ConvertFirstFor shows it - "&" is legal in a folder name.
        Move (Replaces("&", sSws, "&amp;")) to sSwsEsc
        Move (HelpParagraph("The workspace file:")) to sHtml
        Move (sHtml + '<p style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;font-size:12px;color:var(--dfr-ink, #eceae1);overflow-wrap:anywhere;margin:2px 0 10px 0;">' + sSwsEsc + CS_ParaEnd) to sHtml
        Move (sHtml + (HelpParagraph("is still in the older " + (BoldText("INI")) + " format. DataFlex 26 uses the " + (BoldText("JSON")) ;
            + " format, which is also the only format that can hold packages."))) to sHtml
        Move (sHtml + (HelpParagraph("df-cli converts it, and the libraries keep working. Earlier DataFlex Studios cannot open the JSON form."))) to sHtml
        Get FTHtmlMessage "Convert the workspace file?" sHtml "Convert" "Not now" to bOk
        If (bOk = False) ;
            Procedure_Return
        Get ConvertWorkspaceToJson sSws sTarget to bOk
        // The Start Center's workspace file row and the boards read the format - repaint them.
        If (bOk = True) ;
            Broadcast Send RequestTileRefresh of (Client_Id(phoMainPanel(ghoApplication)))
    End_Procedure

    /* CONVERT, THEN CARRY ON WITH WHAT WAS ASKED FOR (Nils 2026-09-03). Add Package... and Browse
       Packages... on an INI workspace land here through _ConvertFirstFor, the banner through
       DoConvertFromBanner. On success the action recorded in piResumeAfterConvert is re-armed on
       the timer, so the Add Package dialog opens from a clean stack against a workspace that is
       JSON now - the user asked to add a package; the conversion was a means, not the outcome.

       On failure the explanation is DEFERRED too: a modal opened from the return path of the
       conversion dialog is exactly how the confirm host broke ("Could not find object
       oCfmBody_hb"), so the offer to fix the cause runs on the next timer tick. */
    Procedure DoConvertThenResume
        Boolean bOk
        Integer iResume

        Get ConvertSwsToJson to bOk
        If (bOk = True) Begin
            Get piResumeAfterConvert to iResume
            Set piResumeAfterConvert to 0
            Set pbConvertRetried to False
            If (iResume <> 0) Begin
                Send ArmAction of (oMLDeferTimer(Self)) iResume
            End
        End
        Else Begin
            Send ArmAction of (oMLDeferTimer(Self)) CI_MLDeferUnresolvedOffer
        End
    End_Procedure

    /* WHY df-cli SAID NO, AND THE FIX (Nils 2026-09-03, LegacyApp). df-cli reads the whole library
       chain before it converts and refuses when an entry does not resolve - the copy step had
       carried two entries for libraries that were never copied. The conversion dialog shows
       df-cli's output, which does not say this; the dialog knows, because the cards already mark
       each entry it cannot find. So: name them, offer to drop them and convert again, ONCE. The
       removal is committed through SaveEntries, as every conversion path requires, and the
       libraries come back through Add Library... or Add Package... afterwards. Package entries
       are never "unresolved" here - bFound means "installed in the cache" for those, and an INI
       workspace has none anyway. */
    Procedure DoOfferUnresolvedFix
        String[] asEntries asKeep
        tLibraryInfo[] aInfo
        String sSws sHome sTarget sList sBadList sAsk
        Integer i iMax iRetval iDead iBad
        Boolean bRetried bOk
        Handle hoInv

        Get pbConvertRetried to bRetried
        Get psSwsFile to sSws
        If (sSws = "" or bRetried = True) Begin
            Set piResumeAfterConvert to 0
            Procedure_Return
        End
        Get pasEntries to asEntries
        Get _FolderOf sSws to sHome
        Get psTargetDfVersion of ghoApplication to sTarget
        Get Create (RefClass(cLibraryInventoryApp)) to hoInv
        Get LibraryEntriesInfo of hoInv (&asEntries) sHome sTarget to aInfo
        Send Destroy of hoInv
        Move "" to sList
        Move "" to sBadList
        Move 0 to iDead
        Move 0 to iBad
        Move (SizeOfArray(aInfo) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            If (aInfo[i].bFound = False and aInfo[i].eKind <> eDepPackage) Begin
                Move (sList + Character(10) + "   " + asEntries[i]) to sList
                Increment iDead
            End
            Else If (aInfo[i].eStatus = eLibUnreadable) Begin
                Move (sBadList + Character(10) + "   " + asEntries[i]) to sBadList
                Increment iBad
            End
        Loop
        /* A FILE THAT IS THERE BUT CARRIES GIT CONFLICT MARKERS (2026-09-14) is not an entry to
           remove - the library is wanted; its repository holds an unfinished merge. Named on its
           own and never offered for removal: the fix is in that repository, not in this list. On a
           real workspace df-cli's account of it was a dump of the file's text and "Failed to
           load/install library X" - true, and no help at all. */
        If (iDead = 0) Begin
            If (iBad > 0) ;
                Send FTStopBox ("The workspace was not converted. df-cli reads the whole library chain first, and " + String(iBad) + " entry(ies) point at a .sws that carries git conflict markers - an unfinished merge, '<<<<<<< ' at the start of a line:" + Character(10) + sBadList + Character(10) + Character(10) + "Resolve that merge in the library's repository - delete the marker lines and the duplicate half - then convert again.") "A library file that cannot be read"
            // Otherwise not this cause. The conversion dialog has already shown df-cli's own account.
            Set piResumeAfterConvert to 0
            Procedure_Return
        End
        Move ("The workspace was not converted. df-cli reads the whole library chain first, and " + String(iDead) + " entry(ies) in this workspace point at a .sws that is not there:" + Character(10) + sList) to sAsk
        If (iBad > 0) ;
            Move (sAsk + Character(10) + Character(10) + "And " + String(iBad) + " entry(ies) point at a .sws that carries git conflict markers, which removing the entries above will not fix - resolve that merge in the library's repository:" + Character(10) + sBadList) to sAsk
        Get FTYesNoBox (sAsk + Character(10) + Character(10) + "Remove the missing ones from the list and convert again? They can be added back afterwards with Add Library... (a folder on disk) or Add Package... (a DataFlex 26 package).") "Entries that do not resolve" MB_DEFBUTTON2 to iRetval
        If (iRetval <> MBR_Yes) Begin
            Set piResumeAfterConvert to 0
            Procedure_Return
        End
        // Kept by INDEX: LibraryEntriesInfo describes asEntries one for one, in order.
        For i from 0 to iMax
            If (aInfo[i].bFound = True or aInfo[i].eKind = eDepPackage) ;
                Move asEntries[i] to asKeep[-1]
        Loop
        Set pasEntries to asKeep
        Set piSelected to -1
        Set pbDirty to True
        Get SaveEntries to bOk
        If (bOk = False) Begin
            Set piResumeAfterConvert to 0
            Procedure_Return
        End
        Send RefreshBody
        Set pbConvertRetried to True
        Send ArmAction of (oMLDeferTimer(Self)) CI_MLDeferConvertResume
    End_Procedure

    /* THE CONVERT-FIRST DOOR for a package action on an INI workspace: names the file, says what
       the conversion does, and on Convert arms the conversion with iResume as the action to carry
       on with. Lifted out of DoAddPackage (2026-09-03) so Browse Packages... takes the same door. */
    Procedure _ConvertFirstFor Integer iResume
        String sSws sHtml sSwsEsc
        Boolean bOk

        Get psSwsFile to sSws
        If (DfCliPath() = "") Begin
            Send FTStopBox ("This workspace file is in the older INI format, which has no place to record a package - packages live in the JSON workspace format DataFlex 26 uses." + Character(10) + Character(10) + "Converting needs df-cli.exe, and none was found beside the selected compiler. Choose a DataFlex 26 compiler in Target version..., or convert it by hand:" + Character(10) + "   df-cli convert --df 26.0 " + '"' + sSws + '"') "Packages need the DataFlex 26 workspace format"
            Procedure_Return
        End
        /* NAMES THE FILE FIRST, on its own line (Nils 2026-08-14). The first draft opened with
           "This workspace file is..." and left the path buried mid-sentence in the paragraph
           below - so the one thing the reader needs to identify, WHICH file is about to be
           rewritten, was the hardest thing on the dialog to find.

           "&" is legal in a Windows folder name and would otherwise start an entity. There is no
           HtmlEscaped reachable from here - it is a method on DashboardFT.vw - and a path cannot
           contain < or >, so the one substitution that matters is done inline. */
        Move (Replaces("&", sSws, "&amp;")) to sSwsEsc
        Move ((HelpParagraph("The workspace file:"))) to sHtml
        /* NO QUOTED FONT NAME HERE, unlike the .pth class above - and it is squeezed from both
           sides. This CSS sits in a style="..." ATTRIBUTE, so a double quote would close the
           attribute early and break the markup; and the DataFlex string is single-quoted, where
           '' is not an escape (error 52). .pth can write "Courier New" because it lives in a
           STYLESHEET (CssStyle/libraries.css), not an attribute. Consolas,monospace it is. */
        Move (sHtml + '<p style="font-family:Consolas,monospace;font-size:12px;color:var(--dfr-ink, #eceae1);overflow-wrap:anywhere;margin:2px 0 10px 0;">' + sSwsEsc + CS_ParaEnd) to sHtml
        Move (sHtml + (HelpParagraph("is still in the older " + (BoldText("INI")) ;
            + " format, and must be converted to the " + (BoldText("JSON")) ;
            + " format DataFlex 26 uses before a package can be added " + CS_EmDash + " the INI format has nowhere to record one."))) to sHtml
        Move (sHtml + (HelpParagraph("Converting rewrites the file in place. df-cli does it; the " ;
            + (BoldText("[Libraries]")) + " section becomes a " + (BoldText("dependencies")) ;
            + " array, and the libraries listed here keep working. Earlier DataFlex Studios cannot open the JSON form. When the conversion is done, this continues with what you asked for."))) to sHtml
        Get pbDirty to bOk
        If (bOk = True) ;
            Move (sHtml + '<p style="color:var(--dfr-gold, #e0b34d);">Your unsaved changes to the list will be saved first ' + CS_EmDash + (' df-cli writes the file itself, so they cannot be held back.' + CS_ParaEnd)) to sHtml
        Get FTHtmlMessage "Packages need the DataFlex 26 workspace format" sHtml "Convert" "Cancel" to bOk
        If (bOk = False) ;
            Procedure_Return
        /* DEFERRED, not called inline (Nils 2026-08-14, "Could not find object oCfmBody_hb").
           FTStopBox and FTInfoBox show in the SAME message box (FTMessageBox.dg) this
           branch just popped for the Convert button (FTHtmlMessage). Converting from inside that
           popup's return path re-pops it before its web host has remounted, and the client
           fails to find oCfmBody_hb. The 1ms timer is this dialog's existing answer to exactly
           that problem; the conversion then runs from a clean stack, and its own dialogs open
           against a dialog that is properly closed. */
        Set piResumeAfterConvert to iResume
        Set pbConvertRetried to False
        Send ArmAction of (oMLDeferTimer(Self)) CI_MLDeferConvertResume
    End_Procedure

    // DF 26+ ONLY - every line below goes through df-cli. Guarded with its buttons and dispatch (Nils 2026-08-19).
    // (The directive must sit OUTSIDE the /* */ block: inside one it is text, not a directive, and the
    //  matching #ENDIF is then orphaned - which is what the balance check caught.)
    /* ADD PACKAGE - the DataFlex 26 package catalogue, through df-cli.

       WHY THIS IS NOT "Add Library..." WITH A DIFFERENT PICKER. A library is a path this dialog
       writes into the .sws itself. A package is resolved, downloaded, unpacked into the global cache
       and only THEN written - by df-cli, which is the only thing allowed to touch that cache. So
       this hands the file over rather than editing it, and the asymmetry is real: Add Library and
       Remove are deferred to OK, an install COMMITS.

       WHICH IS WHY IT SAVES FIRST. df-cli rewrites the dependency list on disk; our in-memory
       working list would then be stale, and OK would write it back over df-cli's change. Committing
       the user's own edits before handing over, and re-reading afterwards, is the only order in
       which neither side loses. The confirmation says so rather than doing it silently.

       GATED ON THE FILE, NOT ON THE VERSION. An INI .sws has [Libraries] and nowhere to put a
       package, whatever DataFlex version it declares - so the answer is the format, and the message
       names the conversion that fixes it. */
    Procedure DoAddPackage
        tPackageHit[] aHits
        String[] asEntries asOutput
        String sSws sTerm sHtml sAction sPick sMsg sSwsEsc
        Integer i iMax iFormat
        Boolean bOk
        Handle hoInv

        Get psSwsFile to sSws
        If (sSws = "") ;
            Procedure_Return

        Get Create (RefClass(cLibraryInventoryApp)) to hoInv
        Get SwsFormatOf of hoInv sSws to iFormat
        Send Destroy of hoInv
        /* AN INI .sws IS NOT A DEAD END ANY MORE (Nils 2026-08-14). It used to print the df-cli
           command and stop, leaving the user to run it in a console - for a workspace this program
           had usually just created. It now offers to do it, and only falls back to the printed
           command when df-cli is not there to do it with. */
        /* CONVERT FIRST, THEN COME BACK HERE (Nils 2026-09-03). The button is live on an INI
           workspace now; _ConvertFirstFor asks, converts, and re-arms CI_MLDeferAddPkg - so this
           procedure runs a second time against a JSON file and goes straight through below. It
           used to stop after converting and leave the user to press the button again. */
        If (iFormat <> eSwsJson) Begin
            Send _ConvertFirstFor CI_MLDeferAddPkg
            Procedure_Return
        End
        If (DfCliPath() = "") Begin
            Send FTStopBox ("No df-cli.exe was found beside the selected compiler, and it is what installs a package - DFRefactor does not download anything itself." + Character(10) + Character(10) + "df-cli ships with DataFlex 26. Choose a DataFlex 26 compiler in Target version..., or install the package from the Studio.") "df-cli not available"
            Procedure_Return
        End

        // COMMIT FIRST - see the note above. A clean list makes this a no-op.
        Get pbDirty to bOk
        If (bOk = True) Begin
            /* SAY WHAT IS UNSAVED (Nils 2026-08-15: "That doesn't seem right. The df-cli must
               already have been installing the previous GitHub repo, so what needs to be saved?").
               The old text read as if it were about the package - it is not. pbDirty means a
               list edit made in THIS dialog since the last save: a Remove or Change... waiting
               for OK. df-cli rewrites the dependency array on disk, so an unsaved edit would be
               lost under it; that is why the save has to come first. Name that, not the package. */
            Get FTYesNoBox ("You have unsaved changes in this list - a library removed or changed since the last save, waiting for OK." + Character(10) + Character(10) + "Adding a package hands the workspace file to df-cli, which rewrites its dependency list on disk. Anything not saved before that would be lost under it." + Character(10) + Character(10) + "Save your list changes now and continue with the package?") "Unsaved list changes" MB_DEFBUTTON1 to i
            If (i <> MBR_Yes) ;
                Procedure_Return
            Get SaveEntries to bOk
            If (bOk = False) ;
                Procedure_Return
        End

        /* THE ONE FIELD, as the Studio does it (Nils 2026-08-14). A package is a path, a
           publisher/package#version, or a git url - df-cli takes all three through the same
           argument, so asking for a search term first would be asking the wrong question of anyone
           who already has the string. Browsing DataFlex's package list is its own action now; see
           DoSearchPackages. */
        Get PromptForPackage sSws to sPick
        If (sPick = "") ;
            Procedure_Return
        Send InstallChosenPackage sPick
    End_Procedure

    /* Install what has been chosen, however it was chosen - typed into the Add dialog or picked
       from DataFlex's package list. Shared so both doors report the same way and re-read the same way. */
    Procedure InstallChosenPackage String sPick
        String[] asOutput
        String sSws sMsg sSaid sHint sLockPkg sLockKey sLockRef sLockLeaf
        Integer iRetval
        Boolean bOk bAdded
        Handle hoInv

        Get psSwsFile to sSws
        If (sSws = "" or Trim(sPick) = "") ;
            Procedure_Return
        Get DfCliPackageInstall sSws sPick (&asOutput) (&bAdded) to bOk
        If (bOk = False) Begin
            /* df-cli's LAST lines, where it says why (Nils 2026-10-29, LegacyApp5_20). This quoted the
               FIRST fifteen, and thirteen "Config file ... using defaults." notes filled them - the
               reason, an incompatible vWin32fh ref, was below the cut and the notes read as the
               error. DfCliOutputNote quotes the tail and leaves those notes out. */
            Get DfCliOutputNote (&asOutput) 15 to sSaid
            /* A LEFTOVER IN THE LOCK FILE IS OFFERED AS THE FIX (Nils 2026-10-29). LegacyApp5_20's
               .sws.lock still pinned vWin32fh at a commit nothing in the workspace asked for any
               more, so every package that asked for vWin32fh ^1.0.0 was refused. When StaleLockPin
               finds exactly that, the box says so and offers to drop the entry and install again -
               default No, and the retry puts the lock back if df-cli still says no. */
            Get Create (RefClass(cLibraryInventoryApp)) to hoInv
            Get StaleLockPin of hoInv sSws asOutput (&sLockPkg) (&sLockKey) to sLockRef
            Send Destroy of hoInv
            If (sLockRef <> "") Begin
                Move (Right(sSws, (Length(sSws) - RightPos("\", sSws))) + ".lock") to sLockLeaf
                Move ("The package was NOT installed: df-cli was asked for two different versions of " + sLockPkg + "." + Character(10) + Character(10) + "df-cli said:" + sSaid) to sMsg
                Move (sMsg + Character(10) + Character(10) + "The workspace's lock file, " + sLockLeaf + ", still pins " + sLockPkg + " at " + sLockRef + ", and nothing in this workspace's dependencies asks for that version any more - it is left over from an earlier version of a dependency.") to sMsg
                Move (sMsg + Character(10) + Character(10) + "Remove that entry from the lock file and install again? df-cli then picks the version itself. If the install still fails, the lock file is put back exactly as it was.") to sMsg
                Get FTYesNoBox sMsg "A leftover in the lock file" MB_DEFBUTTON2 to iRetval
                // DEFERRED: the retry can end in another message box, which cannot pop from this
                // one's return path (see _ConvertFirstFor).
                If (iRetval = MBR_Yes) Begin
                    Set psRetryLockKey to sLockKey
                    Send ArmAction of (oMLDeferTimer(Self)) CI_MLDeferRetryInstall sPick
                End
            End
            Else Begin
                Move ("The package was NOT installed and this workspace is unchanged." + Character(10) + Character(10) + "df-cli said:" + sSaid) to sMsg
                Get DfCliIncompatibleRefHint (&asOutput) to sHint
                If (sHint <> "") ;
                    Move (sMsg + Character(10) + Character(10) + sHint) to sMsg
                Send FTStopBox sMsg "Package not installed"
            End
        End
        /* ALREADY THERE IS SAID, NOT SWALLOWED (Nils 2026-08-17: re-adding vwin32fh "was silence
           and nothing happened"; and pasting an example the workspace already had looked like a
           dead OK button). df-cli is idempotent - it exits 0 and changes nothing for a package the
           workspace already lists - so a success that added no entry is "nothing to do", and the
           user who just typed a string deserves to hear that rather than watch a list not change.
           A real add stays quiet: its new row appearing in the list is the confirmation. */
        Else If (bAdded = False) Begin
            // The LAST lines: df-cli's verdict ("already installed and valid ... Added to workspace")
            // is at the end; the first lines are the config-file notes and "Installing...".
            Get DfCliOutputNote (&asOutput) 7 to sSaid
            Move ("This package is already in the workspace's dependency list, so nothing was added:" + Character(10) + Character(10) + "   " + Trim(sPick) + Character(10) + Character(10) + "df-cli said:" + sSaid) to sMsg
            Send FTInfoBox sMsg "Package already installed"
        End

        /* RE-READ, whichever way it went. df-cli owns the file now; anything we still held is a
           guess about what it did. Reloading is also what makes the new row appear with its cache
           state and its own chain already resolved. */
        Send ReloadEntriesFromFile
        Send RefreshBody
    End_Procedure

    /* THE INSTALL AGAIN, without the leftover lock entry InstallChosenPackage found (Nils
       2026-10-29). Armed on oMLDeferTimer, so it runs on a clean stack. The lock's bytes are kept
       before the entry is removed; if df-cli still says no they go back exactly, and the workspace
       is as it was before the question. A real add stays quiet, as in InstallChosenPackage: the new
       row is the confirmation. */
    Procedure DoRetryInstallWithoutLockPin String sPick
        String[] asOutput
        UChar[] ucaLock
        String sSws sKey sLock sSaid sHint sMsg
        Boolean bOk bAdded bPutBack
        Handle hoInv

        Get psSwsFile to sSws
        Get psRetryLockKey to sKey
        Set psRetryLockKey to ""
        If ((sSws = "") or (sKey = "") or (Trim(sPick) = "")) ;
            Procedure_Return
        Move (sSws + ".lock") to sLock
        Get Create (RefClass(cLibraryInventoryApp)) to hoInv
        Get RemoveLockEntry of hoInv sLock sKey (&ucaLock) to bOk
        If (bOk = False) Begin
            Send Destroy of hoInv
            Send FTStopBox ("The lock file could not be changed, so the install was not tried again:" + Character(10) + Character(10) + "   " + sLock + Character(10) + Character(10) + "It may be read-only or open in another program. This workspace is unchanged.") "Package not installed"
            Procedure_Return
        End
        Get DfCliPackageInstall sSws sPick (&asOutput) (&bAdded) to bOk
        If (bOk = False) Begin
            Get RestoreFileBytes of hoInv sLock ucaLock to bPutBack
            If (bPutBack = True) ;
                Move "The package was still NOT installed. The lock file has been put back as it was, so this workspace is unchanged." to sMsg
            Else ;
                Move ("The package was still NOT installed, and the lock file could NOT be put back: " + sLock + " no longer has the entry that was removed.") to sMsg
            Get DfCliOutputNote (&asOutput) 15 to sSaid
            Move (sMsg + Character(10) + Character(10) + "df-cli said:" + sSaid) to sMsg
            Get DfCliIncompatibleRefHint (&asOutput) to sHint
            If (sHint <> "") ;
                Move (sMsg + Character(10) + Character(10) + sHint) to sMsg
            Send FTStopBox sMsg "Package not installed"
        End
        Send Destroy of hoInv
        Send ReloadEntriesFromFile
        Send RefreshBody
    End_Procedure

    /* Remove a NESTED entry - one a LIBRARY declares - from the .sws that declares it.

       Nils's case: vwin32fh and VDFQuery exist both as local libraries inside WebAppSrc and now as
       GitHub packages on the workspace. The local ones have to go, and they are listed in
       WebAppSrc's own .sws, not in this workspace.

       WRITTEN AT ONCE, and to ANOTHER FILE - the two things that make this different from removing
       a workspace entry, which only edits a list until OK. The confirmation says both, and names
       the file: that .sws may be shared, so this changes the library for every workspace using it.
       Defaults to No.

       sParam is "<declaring .sws>|<raw entry>". The path cannot contain "|" and neither can a
       [Libraries] value, so one split is safe. */
    Procedure DoRemoveNested String sParam
        String[] asEntries asOut
        String sSws sEntry sLeaf
        Integer i iMax iPos iRetval
        Boolean bOk
        Handle hoInv

        Move (Pos("|", sParam)) to iPos
        If (iPos < 2) ;
            Procedure_Return
        Move (Left(sParam, iPos - 1)) to sSws
        Move (Mid(sParam, (Length(sParam) - iPos), (iPos + 1))) to sEntry
        If (sSws = "" or Trim(sEntry) = "") ;
            Procedure_Return
        If (vFilePathExists(sSws) = 0) Begin
            Send FTStopBox ("The library that declares this entry is no longer at:" + Character(10) + Character(10) + "   " + sSws) "Library file not found"
            Procedure_Return
        End
        Move (RightPos("\", sSws)) to iPos
        If (iPos > 0) ;
            Move (Right(sSws, (Length(sSws) - iPos))) to sLeaf
        Else ;
            Move sSws to sLeaf

        Get FTYesNoBox ("Remove" * sEntry + " from " + sLeaf + "?" + Character(10) + Character(10) + "This entry is declared by that LIBRARY, not by this workspace - so it is written immediately, and Cancel here will not put it back." + Character(10) + Character(10) + "Every workspace that uses " + sLeaf + " will stop getting this library through it.") "Remove entry from the library" MB_DEFBUTTON2 to iRetval
        If (iRetval <> MBR_Yes) ;
            Procedure_Return

        Get Create (RefClass(cLibraryInventoryApp)) to hoInv
        Get SwsLibraryEntries of hoInv sSws to asEntries
        Move (SizeOfArray(asEntries) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            If (Lowercase(Trim(asEntries[i])) <> Lowercase(Trim(sEntry))) ;
                Move asEntries[i] to asOut[-1]
        Loop
        // NOTHING MATCHED is not success. The entry came from this list a moment ago, so a miss
        // means the file changed underneath - say so rather than writing it back unchanged.
        If (SizeOfArray(asOut) = SizeOfArray(asEntries)) Begin
            Send Destroy of hoInv
            Send FTStopBox ("That entry is no longer listed in " + sLeaf + ". Nothing was changed - the file may have been edited since this list was built.") "Entry not found"
            Send ReloadEntriesFromFile
            Send RefreshBody
            Procedure_Return
        End
        Get WriteSwsLibraryEntries of hoInv sSws (&asOut) to bOk
        Send Destroy of hoInv
        If (bOk = False) ;
            Send FTStopBox ("The entry could NOT be removed from " + sLeaf + ". The file may be read-only - a library taken from the package cache is.") "Not written"
        // RE-READ either way: the chain below this workspace has changed shape.
        Send ReloadEntriesFromFile
        Send RefreshBody
    End_Procedure

    /* Remove the entry a CARD names. The card carries its own index into the working list, so
       this simply points piSelected at it and reuses DoRemove - the selection is now a highlight
       rather than the mechanism, and no button can act on a row the user did not press. */
    /* CHANGE - swap the entry a card names for another .sws file, in the LIST only (Nils
       2026-08-13: "each and every one of the local libraries should be able to be deleted or
       updated for another .sws file"; parked behind the Remove trace then, delivered 2026-08-15).

       Mirrors DoAdd - same file dialog, same validations, same relative rewrite - with two
       differences. The browse STARTS in the folder the current file sits in, because the everyday
       change is a version move and the new file is a sibling of the old. And the already-listed
       sweep SKIPS the row being replaced: the current file is not a duplicate of itself, and
       picking it again is answered with "nothing changed" rather than an error.

       Nothing is written until OK, exactly like Add and Remove. */
    Procedure DoChangeIndex String sIndex
        String[] asEntries
        String sChosen sSws sHome sEntry sExisting sResolved sStartIn
        Integer i iMax iAt
        Boolean bAccepted
        Handle hoInv

        Move (Integer(sIndex)) to iAt
        Get pasEntries to asEntries
        Move (SizeOfArray(asEntries) - 1) to iMax
        If (iAt < 0 or iAt > iMax) Begin
            Send FTStopBox "That row no longer matches the list. Nothing was changed - close and reopen this dialog." "Row out of step"
            Procedure_Return
        End
        Get psSwsFile to sSws
        If (sSws = "") ;
            Procedure_Return
        Get _FolderOf sSws to sHome

        // Resolve where the CURRENT file sits, so the browse opens there. A short-lived inventory
        // object, destroyed before the dialog shows - the modal can sit open for minutes.
        Get Create (RefClass(cLibraryInventoryApp)) to hoInv
        Get ResolveLibraryPath of hoInv asEntries[iAt] sHome to sStartIn
        Send Destroy of hoInv
        Get _FolderOf sStartIn to sStartIn

        If (sStartIn = "") ;
            Move sHome to sStartIn
        Get _BrowseForLibrarySws sStartIn to sChosen
        If (sChosen = "") ;
            Procedure_Return

        Get Create (RefClass(cLibraryInventoryApp)) to hoInv
        Get NormalizePath of hoInv sChosen to sResolved
        Get NormalizePath of hoInv sSws to sExisting
        If (Lowercase(sResolved) = Lowercase(sExisting)) Begin
            Send Destroy of hoInv
            Send FTStopBox "That is this workspace's own .sws file. A workspace cannot be a library of itself." "Cannot use this workspace"
            Procedure_Return
        End

        // The SAME file this entry already names is not a change - and not an error either.
        Get ResolveLibraryPath of hoInv asEntries[iAt] sHome to sExisting
        Get NormalizePath of hoInv sExisting to sExisting
        If (Lowercase(sExisting) = Lowercase(sResolved)) Begin
            Send Destroy of hoInv
            Send FTStopBox "That is the file this entry already points at, so there is nothing to change." "No change"
            Procedure_Return
        End

        // ALREADY LISTED - resolved-path compare like DoAdd, but the row being replaced is SKIPPED.
        For i from 0 to iMax
            If (i <> iAt) Begin
                Get ResolveLibraryPath of hoInv asEntries[i] sHome to sExisting
                Get NormalizePath of hoInv sExisting to sExisting
                If (Lowercase(sExisting) = Lowercase(sResolved)) Begin
                    Send Destroy of hoInv
                    // Select + point, same as DoAdd: sorted by name means it may be off-screen.
                    Set piSelected to i
                    Send RefreshBody
                    Send FTStopBox ("That library is already in the list, as entry " + String(i + 1) + " of " + String(iMax + 1) + ":" + Character(10) + Character(10) + asEntries[i] + Character(10) + Character(10) + "It is highlighted in the list. Changing this row to the same file would list it twice.") "Already listed"
                    Procedure_Return
                End
            End
        Loop

        // The dev / demo / sample trap - see _AcceptLibrarySwsRole.
        Get _AcceptLibrarySwsRole hoInv sChosen "Use" to bAccepted
        If (bAccepted = False) Begin
            Send Destroy of hoInv
            Procedure_Return
        End

        // Written RELATIVE where that resolves - see DoAdd.
        Get RelativeLibraryEntry of hoInv sChosen sHome to sEntry
        Send Destroy of hoInv

        Move sEntry to asEntries[iAt]
        Set pasEntries to asEntries
        Set piSelected to iAt
        Set pbDirty to True
        Send RefreshBody
    End_Procedure

    Procedure DoRemoveIndex String sIndex
        String[] asEntries
        String sEntry
        Integer i iMax iRetval

        Move (Integer(sIndex)) to i
        Get pasEntries to asEntries
        Move (SizeOfArray(asEntries) - 1) to iMax
        If (i < 0 or i > iMax) Begin
            Send FTStopBox "That row no longer matches the list. Nothing was removed - close and reopen this dialog." "Row out of step"
            Procedure_Return
        End
        Move asEntries[i] to sEntry

        /* NAMES WHAT IT WILL REMOVE, and that is not politeness (Nils 2026-08-14).

           A card carries an INDEX into the working list, assigned while the cards were built in
           tree order - and the list is then SORTED for display. If those two ever disagree, the
           button removes a neighbour, silently and plausibly: two entries for the same library
           differing only in version look almost identical on screen. Nils hit exactly that -
           pressed Remove on the 19.1 row and lost the 26.0 one.

           Showing the entry BEFORE acting makes a mismatch impossible to miss: the dialog either
           names the row that was clicked, or it does not, and the user sees which. That is worth
           a keystroke on any destructive action, and here it is also the diagnosis. */
        Get FTYesNoBox ("Remove this entry from the list?" + Character(10) + Character(10) + "   " + sEntry + Character(10) + Character(10) + "Nothing is written until OK, and Cancel discards every change.") "Remove library entry" MB_DEFBUTTON1 to iRetval
        If (iRetval <> MBR_Yes) ;
            Procedure_Return
        Set piSelected to i
        Send DoRemove
    End_Procedure

    /* Uninstall a package by the name df-cli knows it by - the card carries it, so no selection
       index is involved and it cannot act on the wrong row.

       ASKED FIRST, and it says the name: this uninstalls NOW, and Cancel will not put it back.
       Saves the working list first for the same reason the install does - df-cli rewrites the
       dependency array, so anything held in memory would be written back over its change. */

    // DF 26+ ONLY - every line below goes through df-cli. Guarded with its buttons and dispatch (Nils 2026-08-19).
    /* ASK WHAT IS NEWER, AND CHANGE NOTHING.

       Two sources, because the two kinds of dependency answer to different things:

         system  -> DfCliPackageSearch, which df-cli scopes BY THE WORKSPACE'S DataFlex version.
                     `package details` also reports a version but is UNSCOPED - measured, it
                     offered 1.1.0 for a theme whose compatible newest was 1.0.4 - so an Update
                     badge driven by details would point the user at an incompatible release.
         git      -> by what the pin is: a commit against GitRemoteHead, the branch head; a
                     version tag against GitRemoteTagsEx, the newest tag in its major version.

       NEVER `package update`. Its help says "Checks for an update" and it takes no --yes, which
       reads as safe; measured 2026-08-20 it AUTO-INSTALLS whenever the pins differ from what is
       installed, and left a probe workspace BROKEN at exit 15. Doing that to a customer's
       workspace from a button labelled "check" is the one outcome this feature must not have.

       search EXITS 15 on a workspace whose pins have drifted - safe, but it returns no rows.
       That is reported as "could not check", never as an empty result, because an empty result
       would read as "nothing to update", which is the opposite of the truth. */
    /*  ARM THE AUTOMATIC CHECK - only where the answer is already known to be interesting.

        Nils 2026-09-10: "instead of the need to press the Update Check button, it would be better
        if that check could be done automatically, but only if it was decided when the workspace
        was opened, that an update was needed." That last clause is the whole design. Checking on
        every opening would put a network call behind a dialog people open to read a list, and
        would do it on workspaces whose answer was "nothing to update" ten seconds earlier.

        Four refusals:
          no workspace          - nothing to check.
          already checked       - the button was pressed, or this already ran; the chips are live.
          scan says not behind  - includes "could not answer" and "nothing remote to ask", because
                                  piUpdBehind is 0 in all three and none of them is a reason to go
                                  to the network again.
          a different workspace - UpdateScanDone is keyed on the .sws, so the open-scan verdict is
                                  only used for the workspace it was actually about.

        The preference needs no test here: with it off the open-scan never ran, so UpdateScanDone
        is False and the third refusal catches it. */
    Procedure ArmAutoUpdateCheck
        String sSws
        Boolean bDone

        Get pbAutoUpdChkDone to bDone
        If (bDone = True) ;
            Procedure_Return
        Get pbUpdateChecked to bDone
        If (bDone = True) ;
            Procedure_Return
        Get psSwsFile to sSws
        If (Trim(sSws) = "") ;
            Procedure_Return
        If ((UpdateScanDone(ghoLibraryReport, sSws)) = False) ;
            Procedure_Return
        If ((piUpdBehind(ghoLibraryReport)) < 1) ;
            Procedure_Return

        Set pbAutoUpdChkDone to True
        Send ArmAction of (oMLDeferTimer(Self)) CI_MLDeferAutoUpdChk
    End_Procedure

    // The button: same check, and it SAYS what it found. See DoUpdateCheckEx.
    Procedure DoUpdateCheck
        Send DoUpdateCheckEx False
    End_Procedure

    /* THE ONE RETIRE PATH - the [Retire folders] button and the question at close both come here,
       so there is one confirm, one rename and one account of what happened.

       RENAMED, NEVER DELETED (Nils 2026-08-30, having swapped DUF from a library to a package: "it
       is not acceptable to have two copies with the same name. One has to be either renamed or
       deleted"). A rename is undone by renaming back, and it is enough - the compiler resolves a Use
       by file name, so a folder called <name>.OLD cannot be reached. These trees are NOT copies of
       what replaced them: one real Libraries\DUF held 86 source files present in no form in its
       package, and a list edit must not destroy them. RetireLibraryFolder numbers past a name an
       earlier retire already owns, and refuses to overwrite.

       ONE DECISION, TWO EFFECTS. The scope ticks are dropped WITH the rename, never on their own - a
       tick is the user's curation, and clearing one because the tool believes a folder is stale is
       the intent-measuring design rejected on 2026-08-31. Decline, and nothing changes at all.

       sHeadline says why THESE folders are offered; the rest of the confirm is the same for both
       callers. Everything is escaped - HelpParagraph takes markup, and "R&D" is an ordinary folder. */
    Procedure _RetireLibraryFolders String[] asFolder String sHeadline
        Handle ho hoDD
        String[] asSel
        String sMsg sList sWhy sNew sFailed
        Boolean bOk
        Integer i iMax iDone iRows iGone

        Move (SizeOfArray(asFolder) - 1) to iMax
        If (iMax < 0) ;
            Procedure_Return
        Move "" to sList
        For i from 0 to iMax
            Move (sList + rfHtmlEscape(asFolder[i]) + CS_Br) to sList
        Loop
        Get FTHtmlMessage "Retire the unused library folders" ;
            (HelpParagraph(rfHtmlEscape(sHeadline)) ;
             + HelpParagraph(sList) ;
             + HelpParagraph("Rename them to " + (BoldText("<name>.OLD")) + ", so the compiler can no longer find them? " ;
                + "Nothing is deleted: renaming one back restores it, and a folder may hold source " ;
                + "that whatever replaced it does not.") ;
             + HelpParagraph("Folder selections pointing into a renamed folder are dropped with it. " ;
                + "Decline and nothing changes at all.")) ;
            "Retire them" "Not now" to bOk
        If (bOk = False) ;
            Procedure_Return

        Get Create (RefClass(cShadowedPackageFiles)) to ho
        If (ho = 0) ;
            Procedure_Return
        Get phoFolderSelHeaDD of ghoApplication to hoDD
        Move 0 to iDone
        Move 0 to iRows
        Move "" to sFailed
        For i from 0 to iMax
            Get RetireLibraryFolder of ho asFolder[i] (&sNew) to sWhy
            If (sWhy = "") Begin
                Increment iDone
                If (hoDD <> 0) Begin
                    Get RemoveSelectedFoldersUnder of hoDD asFolder[i] to iGone
                    Move (iRows + iGone) to iRows
                End
            End
            Else ;
                Move (sFailed + Character(10) + "   " + asFolder[i] + " - " + sWhy) to sFailed
        Loop
        Send Destroy of ho

        // The live scope cache is keyed by home and was just shortened underneath us.
        If (hoDD <> 0) Begin
            Get SelectedFolderPathsByHome of hoDD to asSel
            Set pasSelectedFolders of ghoApplication to asSel
        End
        // The tile's memo watches the .sws, which a rename does not touch.
        Send InvalidateLibraryCounts of ghoLibraryReport

        Move (String(iDone) + " folder(s) renamed to <name>.OLD.") to sMsg
        If (iRows > 0) ;
            Move (sMsg + Character(10) + String(iRows) + " folder selection(s) pointing into them were dropped from the refactoring scope.") to sMsg
        If (sFailed <> "") ;
            Move (sMsg + Character(10) + Character(10) + "These could NOT be renamed:" + sFailed ;
                + Character(10) + Character(10) + "A file in them is probably open - in the Studio, or in an editor.") to sMsg
        Send FTInfoBox sMsg "Retire folders"
    End_Procedure

    /* RETIRE THE LIBRARY FOLDERS A PACKAGE REPLACED - the bar's [Retire folders] button.

       Nils 2026-09-10 chose a button over an offer-on-open: nothing here happens because a dialog was
       opened. The tile carries the standing signal ("2 replaced library folders on disk"), and since
       2026-09-11 the question at close (OfferRetireAfterClose) catches the folders a showing of this
       dialog stops using. The button is what is left for: a workspace that arrived already in this
       state, or a question that was declined. */
    Procedure DoRetireStaleLibraryFolders
        String[] asFolder
        Integer iCount

        Send RescanStaleLibraryFolders of ghoLibraryReport
        Get piLibStale        of ghoLibraryReport to iCount
        Get pasLibStaleFolder of ghoLibraryReport to asFolder
        If (iCount = 0) Begin
            Send FTInfoBox "No replaced library folders are left in this workspace." "Retire folders"
            Send RefreshBody
            Procedure_Return
        End
        Send _RetireLibraryFolders asFolder ("A package has replaced each of these " + String(iCount) ;
            + " folder(s), and nothing in the workspace declares them any more - but they are still on" ;
            + " disk, so a file in one can be edited, or compiled, instead of the package's own.")
        Send RefreshBody
    End_Procedure

    /* THE QUESTION AT CLOSE (Nils 2026-09-11, having nearly missed the [Retire folders] button among
       the others: offer the retirement "when the Libraries dialog is closed and libraries have been
       removed").

       FILE AGAINST FILE. What the .sws declared when this showing opened (pasOpenEntries, read in
       Popup) is compared with what it declares now. The offer this replaces read its baseline at OK,
       on the argument that a snapshot taken at open "would describe a state two writes old" - but
       for finding a REMOVAL those two writes are exactly the point. Add Package saves the list
       before df-cli runs, and so does the conversion to the JSON format, so the natural order -
       remove the library, then add its package - had already written the removal by the time OK
       was pressed, and the old offer found nothing to ask about.

       ANY CLOSE. Sent by MaintainWorkspaceLibraries once the modal has returned, so OK, Cancel,
       Alt+C and the title bar all arrive here - a package added, updated or uninstalled has written
       the file whichever button ends the showing.

       ASKED ONLY WHEN THIS SHOWING STOPPED USING A FOLDER that is still on disk and that nothing
       further down the chain declares - never on an unchanged list, so closing never nags. The one
       question then carries every folder there is to retire: those, and any a package has replaced
       (the Libraries tile's own detector), so nothing is left behind for the button.

       ONLY FOR THE LOADED WORKSPACE - see _IsLoadedWorkspace. */
    Procedure OfferRetireAfterClose
        Handle hoShadow hoInv
        String[] asOpen asNow asGone asDeclared asFolders asLow asStale
        String sSws sHome sHead
        Integer i iMax iRemoved
        Boolean bLoaded

        Get _IsLoadedWorkspace to bLoaded
        If (bLoaded = False) ;
            Procedure_Return
        Get psSwsFile to sSws
        Get psHomeTarget of ghoApplication to sHome
        Get pasOpenEntries to asOpen
        If (Trim(sHome) = "" or SizeOfArray(asOpen) = 0) ;
            Procedure_Return

        Get Create (RefClass(cLibraryInventoryApp)) to hoInv
        If (hoInv = 0) ;
            Procedure_Return
        Get SwsLibraryEntries of hoInv sSws to asNow
        Send Destroy of hoInv
        Get Create (RefClass(cShadowedPackageFiles)) to hoShadow
        If (hoShadow = 0) ;
            Procedure_Return
        Get LibraryFoldersNoLongerListed of hoShadow sHome asOpen asNow to asGone
        // The resolver is only asked when there is something to ask about - an unchanged list, the
        // usual close, costs one read of the .sws and nothing more.
        If (SizeOfArray(asGone) > 0) ;
            Get GetWorkspaceLibrarySourceFolders of ghoApplication sSws to asDeclared
        Move (SizeOfArray(asGone) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            /* STILL ON DISK, AND NOTHING FURTHER DOWN THE CHAIN NEEDS IT. One library's .sws can
               declare another, so a folder the workspace no longer names itself may still be one the
               build reads; IsOrphanedLibraryFolder asks the resolver, which follows local
               dependencies all the way down. */
            If (vFolderExists(asGone[i]) <> 0) Begin
                If (IsOrphanedLibraryFolder(hoShadow, asGone[i], asDeclared) = True) Begin
                    Move (Lowercase(asGone[i])) to asLow[-1]
                    Move asGone[i]              to asFolders[-1]
                End
            End
        Loop
        Send Destroy of hoShadow
        Move (SizeOfArray(asFolders)) to iRemoved
        If (iRemoved = 0) ;
            Procedure_Return

        // Everything a package has replaced joins the same question - nothing left for the button.
        Send RescanStaleLibraryFolders of ghoLibraryReport
        Get pasLibStaleFolder of ghoLibraryReport to asStale
        Move (SizeOfArray(asStale) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            If (SearchArray(Lowercase(asStale[i]), asLow) = -1) Begin
                Move (Lowercase(asStale[i])) to asLow[-1]
                Move asStale[i]              to asFolders[-1]
            End
        Loop

        Move ("The library list no longer uses " + String(iRemoved) + " folder(s) that are still on disk, and nothing else in the workspace declares them.") to sHead
        If (SizeOfArray(asFolders) > iRemoved) ;
            Move (sHead + " " + String(SizeOfArray(asFolders) - iRemoved) + " more, which a package has replaced, are listed with them.") to sHead
        Move (sHead + " Left in place, a file in one can still be edited, or compiled, instead of whatever replaced it.") to sHead
        Send _RetireLibraryFolders asFolders sHead
    End_Procedure

    /*  bQuiet is for the automatic run. It lights the row chips and says nothing: the user opened
        this dialog to look at libraries, and three of the four things the button reports would be
        a box in front of the list they asked for. The tile already told them something was behind
        - that is why this ran at all - so the chips going live IS the message. */
    Procedure DoUpdateCheckEx Boolean bQuiet
        String[] asKey asLatest
        String sSws sMsg
        Integer iFound iErr iAnswered

        Get psSwsFile to sSws
        If (sSws = "") ;
            Procedure_Return
        If (DfCliPath() = "") Begin
            If (bQuiet = False) ;
                Send FTStopBox "No df-cli.exe was found beside the selected compiler, and it is what reports package versions." "df-cli not available"
            Procedure_Return
        End

        /* ONE implementation, shared with the Libraries tile since 2026-09-10 (WorkspaceUpdateScan in
           cDfCliPackages.pkg). The two loops that used to stand here - registry rows against the
           scoped search, git rows against the branch head - are its body verbatim. A tile that said
           "up to date" while these chips said otherwise is the failure a second copy produces.

           The BUTTON keeps the long bound: a user who pressed it is waiting on purpose. */
        Get WorkspaceUpdateScan sSws CI_GitLsRemoteTimeoutSecs (&asKey) (&asLatest) (&iErr) (&iAnswered) to iFound
        Set pasUpdKey       to asKey
        Set pasUpdLatest    to asLatest
        Set pbUpdateChecked to True
        // The tile asks the same question; give it this answer rather than let it scan again.
        Send SetUpdateResult of ghoLibraryReport sSws iFound (iAnswered > 0)
        Send RefreshBody

        // iErr is non-zero ONLY when a registry search actually ran and failed - the scan skips it
        // entirely on a workspace with no registry packages, so this no longer fires on a git-only
        // workspace the way the old "no hits" test could.
        If (iErr <> 0 and bQuiet = True) ;
            Procedure_Return
        If (iErr <> 0) Begin
            Move ("df-cli could not read this workspace's packages (exit" * String(iErr) + ")." + Character(10) + Character(10)) to sMsg
            Move (sMsg + "Registry packages were NOT checked, which is not the same as nothing to update. Git packages were still checked against their branch heads.") to sMsg
            Send FTStopBox sMsg "Could not check system packages"
            Procedure_Return
        End
        /* THREE outcomes, because "nothing is behind" and "nothing could tell us" are the same
           iFound and must not be the same sentence - the same rule the tile line follows. A
           workspace of nothing but path libraries lands here too, and it has not been checked;
           it has no remote to check. */
        If (bQuiet = True) ;
            Procedure_Return
        If (iAnswered = 0) ;
            Send FTInfoBox "Nothing here could be checked: this workspace declares no package or git dependency, or no remote could be reached." "Update Check"
        Else If (iFound = 0) ;
            Send FTInfoBox (String(iAnswered) * "dependencies checked - all are at the newest version this workspace can take.") "Update Check"
        Else ;
            Send FTInfoBox (String(iFound) * "package(s) have something newer. The Update button on those rows is now live.") "Update Check"
    End_Procedure

    /* Upgrade ONE named package, after asking. Same order as the uninstall: our own edits are
       committed FIRST, because df-cli rewrites the dependency array and anything still held in
       memory would be written back over its change.

       ONE package, never a blanket upgrade: pins are interdependent (measured - "Incompatible
       version for DataFlex-dev/Web UI: 1.0.51 does not satisfy 1.0.52"), so `upgrade` with no
       package named would move things the user never looked at. */
    /* ONE FIELD AT A TIME out of a chip id. "*" separates them because it cannot occur in a Windows
       path, a git URL or a version string - while "#" DOES occur inside a system-package entry
       ("DataFlex-dev/Conversions#1.0.0"), which is what an earlier cut of this split on. */
    Function _NextField String ByRef sRest Returns String
        String sOne
        Integer iAt

        Move (Pos("*", sRest)) to iAt
        If (iAt = 0) Begin
            Move sRest to sOne
            Move ""    to sRest
        End
        Else Begin
            Move (Left(sRest, iAt - 1)) to sOne
            Move (Right(sRest, (Length(sRest) - iAt))) to sRest
        End
        Function_Return (Trim(sOne))
    End_Function

    // One labelled line of the fact block. The key column is fixed and quiet, the value bright:
    // Nils on the first cut - "It is not clear what is shown" - and it was a wall of unlabelled prose.
    Function _FactRow String sKey String sValueHtml Returns String
        If (Trim(sValueHtml) = "") ;
            Function_Return ""
        Function_Return ('<tr><td style="padding:3px 14px 3px 0;color:var(--dfr-ink-cool, #8f8d84);vertical-align:top;white-space:nowrap;">' ;
            + (rfHtmlEscape(sKey)) + '</td><td style="padding:3px 0;color:var(--dfr-ink-bright, #e6e6e6);vertical-align:top;">' ;
            + sValueHtml + '</td></tr>')
    End_Function

    /* THE LICENCE, WHICH IS THE WHOLE REASON THIS DIALOG EXISTS (Nils 2026-08-21: "where is the
       license text shown? That is of great interest when adding a package").

       "NOASSERTION" is SPDX for "the publisher declined to state one" - it is not a licence, and
       printing the token verbatim would read like one to anyone who has not met SPDX. It is real and
       common: DataFlex-dev/Conversions carries it, while Security - CNG carries BSD-3-Clause and Web
       UI FlexTron carries DocumentRef-DataFlex, an SPDX pointer to a licence document rather than an
       identifier. So a known id is shown as itself, and the two empty cases are spelled out with what
       to do about them.

       The SPDX list is printed as TEXT, not as a link. This body renders inside the message box's own
       web view, and a navigation there would take the dialog with it. */
    Function _LicenceHtml String sLic Returns String
        String s

        Move (Trim(sLic)) to s
        If (s <> "" and Uppercase(s) <> "NOASSERTION") ;
            Function_Return (rfHtmlEscape(s))

        /* THE STYLES STAY INLINE, and not for the badge's reason. This body travels into the
           MESSAGE BOX's page (RDCFlexTron's FTMessageBox.dg, through FTHtmlMessage), which knows
           the library's sheets only. A class for it would have to live there, which would put a
           Libraries concern in a sheet shared by every message box. */
        Function_Return ((SpanOpenStyle("", "color:var(--dfr-code-ink, #e0b34d);")) ;
            + 'Not declared' + CS_SpanEnd ;
            + (DivOpenStyle("", "margin-top:3px;color:var(--dfr-ink-cool, #8f8d84);")) ;
            + 'The publisher has not stated a licence' ;
            + (If(Uppercase(s) = "NOASSERTION", ' (SPDX: NOASSERTION)', '')) ;
            + ('. Ask them for the terms before shipping this in a product.' + CS_Br) ;
            + ('SPDX licence list: https://spdx.org/licenses/' + CS_DivEnd))
    End_Function

    /* The supported range, in words, and ALWAYS shown here.

       The card suppresses a range that says nothing (Nils: "Not much of a help, is it?") - a package
       in a DF26 workspace obviously supports DF26. The popup is the opposite case: it is the place
       someone opens BEFORE adding a package, where "26.0 only" is the fact that decides it. */
    Function _DfRangeText String sMin String sMax Returns String
        Move (Trim(sMin)) to sMin
        Move (Trim(sMax)) to sMax

        If (sMin = "" and sMax = "") ;
            Function_Return "not stated"
        If (sMin <> "" and sMax <> "") Begin
            If (sMin = sMax) ;
                Function_Return (sMin + " only")
            Function_Return (sMin + " to " + sMax)
        End
        If (sMin <> "") ;
            Function_Return (sMin + " and later")
        Function_Return ("up to " + sMax)
    End_Function

    /* WHAT THE STUDIO'S DESCRIPTION TAB SHOWS, and why it cannot live on the card.

       Nils compared the two (2026-08-21). Our card said "Conversion library to convert values from
       one format to the other."; the Studio said six lines about Celcius to Fahrenheit, Ounces to
       Grams and Decimal to Hexadecimal. Both are real - they are DIFFERENT FIELDS. The one-liner is
       "description" inside the package object; the long one is the TOP-LEVEL "description", and it
       exists only on the package service:

           https://packages.dataflex.dev/api/v1/packages/<publisher>/<name>
           { "package": { "description": <one-liner>, "license": .., "dfMinVersion": .. },
             "description": <the long text>,
             "versions": [ { "version": .., "changelog": .., "dependencies": [ {name, publisher,
                             version}, .. ] } ] }

       So it costs a network round trip per package - which is why this is a click and not a column.
       A fetch per row would put the network between the user and a list that has to open at once.

       A GIT package is not on that service at all, so its equivalent is the repository README,
       fetched raw AT THE PINNED COMMIT - not the branch head, so what is read is what is installed -
       and its facts come from the manifest in its own checkout instead.

       Shown through ShowMessage, which takes raw body HTML: no new dialog, the app's own theme, and
       the panel already scrolls a long body. */
    Procedure DoShowPackageDetails String sParam
        String[] asDoc
        String sKind sSrc sVer sManifest sUrl sOwner sRepo sName sPub sTitle sFacts sBody
        Boolean bGit bVoid
        Handle hoInv

        Move (Trim(sParam)) to sParam
        If (sParam = "") ;
            Procedure_Return

        // "<K>*<f1>*<f2>*<f3>" - K is G for a git package, S for a system one.
        Get _NextField (&sParam) to sKind
        Get _NextField (&sParam) to sSrc
        Get _NextField (&sParam) to sVer
        Get _NextField (&sParam) to sManifest
        Move (Uppercase(sKind) = "G") to bGit

        Get Create (RefClass(cLibraryInventoryApp)) to hoInv
        Move "" to sUrl
        Move "" to sFacts
        If (bGit = True) Begin
            Get GitOwnerFromSource of hoInv sSrc to sOwner
            Get GitRepoFromSource  of hoInv sSrc to sRepo
            Move sRepo  to sName
            Move sOwner to sPub
            If (sOwner <> "" and sRepo <> "" and sVer <> "") ;
                Move ("https://raw.githubusercontent.com/" + sOwner + "/" + sRepo + "/" + sVer + "/README.md") to sUrl
            Get _FactsFromManifest hoInv sManifest sPub sSrc to sFacts
            Get ShortDependencyVersion of hoInv sVer to sVer
        End
        Else Begin
            // The entry still carries "<publisher>/<name>#<version>" - it was never split.
            Get PackageName       of hoInv sSrc to sName
            Get PackageRepository of hoInv sSrc to sPub
            Get PackageVersion    of hoInv sSrc to sVer
            If (sPub <> "" and sName <> "") ;
                Move ("https://packages.dataflex.dev/api/v1/packages/" + (Replaces(" ", sPub, "%20")) + "/" + (Replaces(" ", sName, "%20"))) to sUrl
        End
        Send Destroy of hoInv

        Move (sName + (If(sVer = "", "", " " + sVer))) to sTitle

        If (sUrl = "") Begin
            Move (sFacts + (HelpParagraph("There is no address to fetch a description from for this one."))) to sBody
            Get FTHtmlMessage sTitle sBody "Close" "" to bVoid
            Procedure_Return
        End

        Move (rfHttpGetText(sUrl)) to asDoc
        If (SizeOfArray(asDoc) = 0) Begin
            /* SAY WHICH ADDRESS FAILED. "Could not fetch" with no url sends the reader nowhere; with
               it they can paste it into a browser and see for themselves whether the package, the
               network or the name is what is wrong. The facts still show - they came from disk, and
               a licence is worth reading even when the prose did not arrive. */
            Move (sFacts + ((HelpParagraph("Nothing came back from:")) + '<pre>') + (rfHtmlEscape(sUrl)) + '</pre>') to sBody
            Move (sBody + (HelpParagraph("The package may publish no description, or the machine may be offline."))) to sBody
            Get FTHtmlMessage sTitle sBody "Close" "" to bVoid
            Procedure_Return
        End

        If (bGit = True) ;
            Get _DetailsReadmeHtml sFacts sOwner sRepo sVer (&asDoc) to sBody
        Else ;
            Get _DetailsApiHtml sPub sVer (&asDoc) to sBody
        Get FTHtmlMessage sTitle sBody "Close" "" to bVoid
    End_Procedure

    /* A git package's facts, from the manifest in its own checkout.

       Deliberately NOT _PkgMetaExtras, which lives on oWebHost and builds the card's chips. That one
       SUPPRESSES a range that constrains nothing, which is right for a row and wrong here; and it
       cannot be reached from dialog scope anyway - a private helper on a child object raises error 98
       at runtime, which is how the Details button crashed the first time. */
    Function _FactsFromManifest Handle hoInv String sManifest String sPub String sSrc Returns String
        tSwsDependency[] aReq
        String sOut sLic sMin sMax sReq sOne
        Integer i iMax

        Move "" to sOut
        Move (sOut + (_FactRow(Self, "Publisher", (rfHtmlEscape(sPub))))) to sOut
        Move (sOut + (_FactRow(Self, "Repository", (rfHtmlEscape(sSrc))))) to sOut

        If (Trim(sManifest) <> "") Begin
            Get SwsManifestValue of hoInv sManifest "license" to sLic
            Get SwsManifestValue of hoInv sManifest "df-min"  to sMin
            Get SwsManifestValue of hoInv sManifest "df-max"  to sMax

            Get SwsJsonObjectDependencies of hoInv sManifest to aReq
            Move "" to sReq
            Move (SizeOfArray(aReq) - 1) to iMax
            For i from 0 to iMax
                Get GitRepoFromSource of hoInv (Trim(aReq[i].sSource)) to sOne
                If (Trim(sOne) = "") ;
                    Move (Trim(aReq[i].sSws)) to sOne
                If (Trim(sOne) <> "") Begin
                    If (sReq <> "") ;
                        Move (sReq + ", ") to sReq
                    Move (sReq + (rfHtmlEscape(Trim(sOne)))) to sReq
                End
            Loop
        End

        Move (sOut + (_FactRow(Self, "Licence", (_LicenceHtml(Self, sLic))))) to sOut
        Move (sOut + (_FactRow(Self, "DataFlex", (rfHtmlEscape(_DfRangeText(Self, sMin, sMax)))))) to sOut
        Move (sOut + (_FactRow(Self, "Requires", (If(sReq = "", "nothing", sReq))))) to sOut
        Function_Return ('<table style="border-collapse:collapse;margin:0 0 12px 0;">' + sOut + '</table>')
    End_Function

    /* A git README as body HTML: PLAIN TEXT in a <pre>.

       Rendering markdown would mean a parser this program has no other use for, and the raw text
       reads well enough - headings and lists survive as themselves. Bounded, because a README can run
       to hundreds of lines and this is a message box, not a document viewer. */
    Function _DetailsReadmeHtml String sFacts String sOwner String sRepo String sVer String[] ByRef asDoc Returns String
        String sBody
        Integer i iMax

        Move (sFacts + (CS_ParaOpen + (BoldText("Read me")))) to sBody
        If (Trim(sVer) <> "") ;
            Move (sBody + (' ' + (SpanOpen("ml-dim")) + 'as at ') + (rfHtmlEscape(sVer)) + CS_SpanEnd) to sBody
        Move (sBody + (CS_ParaEnd + '<pre>')) to sBody

        Move (SizeOfArray(asDoc) - 1) to iMax
        If (iMax > CI_DetailsMaxLines) ;
            Move CI_DetailsMaxLines to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            Move (sBody + (rfHtmlEscape(asDoc[i])) + Character(10)) to sBody
        Loop
        Move (sBody + '</pre>') to sBody
        If (SizeOfArray(asDoc) > (CI_DetailsMaxLines + 1)) ;
            Move (sBody + (HelpParagraph('Only the first ' + String(CI_DetailsMaxLines + 1) + ' lines are shown.'))) to sBody
        Function_Return sBody
    End_Function

    /* The package service's answer as body HTML: the fact block, then the long description.

       The version-specific fields are read from the "versions" entry that MATCHES WHAT IS INSTALLED,
       not from versions[0] - a package with four published versions would otherwise show the newest
       one's licence and changelog beside the older one the workspace actually has. The package object
       supplies the fallback when no entry matches. */
    Function _DetailsApiHtml String sPub String sInstalledVer String[] ByRef asDoc Returns String
        String sAll sOut sLong sLic sMin sMax sChg sReq sVer sOne sDepVer
        UChar[] ucJson
        Integer i iMax iVerMax iDepMax iDep
        Boolean bOk
        Handle hoJson hoPkg hoVers hoV hoDeps hoDep

        Move "" to sAll
        Move (SizeOfArray(asDoc) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            Move (sAll + asDoc[i]) to sAll
        Loop

        Get Create (RefClass(cJsonObject)) to hoJson
        If (hoJson = 0) ;
            Function_Return (HelpParagraph("Could not create a JSON reader."))

        Move (rfStringToUtf8Bytes(sAll)) to ucJson
        Get ParseUtf8 of hoJson ucJson to bOk
        If (bOk = False) Begin
            Send Destroy of hoJson
            Function_Return (HelpParagraph("The package service answered, but not with JSON this program could read."))
        End
        Set pbRequireAllMembers of hoJson to False
        Get MemberValueEnh of hoJson "description" to sLong

        Get MemberEnh of hoJson "package" to hoPkg
        If (hoPkg <> 0) Begin
            Set pbRequireAllMembers of hoPkg to False
            Get MemberValueEnh of hoPkg "license"      to sLic
            Get MemberValueEnh of hoPkg "dfMinVersion" to sMin
            Get MemberValueEnh of hoPkg "dfMaxVersion" to sMax
        End

        Move "" to sReq
        Get MemberEnh of hoJson "versions" to hoVers
        If (hoVers <> 0) Begin
            Set pbRequireAllMembers of hoVers to False
            Get MemberCount of hoVers to iVerMax
            Decrement iVerMax
            For i from 0 to iVerMax
                Get MemberEnh of hoVers i to hoV
                If (hoV <> 0) Begin
                    Set pbRequireAllMembers of hoV to False
                    Get MemberValueEnh of hoV "version" to sVer
                    If (Trim(sVer) = Trim(sInstalledVer)) Begin
                        Get MemberValueEnh of hoV "changelog"    to sChg
                        Get MemberValueEnh of hoV "license"      to sOne
                        If (Trim(sOne) <> "") ;
                            Move sOne to sLic
                        Get MemberValueEnh of hoV "dfMinVersion" to sOne
                        If (Trim(sOne) <> "") ;
                            Move sOne to sMin
                        Get MemberValueEnh of hoV "dfMaxVersion" to sOne
                        If (Trim(sOne) <> "") ;
                            Move sOne to sMax

                        /* dependencies[] holds OBJECTS - {name, publisher, version, source, ..} -
                           and the version is a RANGE ("~1.0.2", "<2.0.0"), not a pin. It is shown
                           verbatim: rewriting a range as a number would state something the package
                           did not. Checked against the live service before writing this, rather than
                           assumed - Conversions publishes an empty list, so the shape came from
                           Security - CNG and MCP Server. */
                        Get MemberEnh of hoV "dependencies" to hoDeps
                        If (hoDeps <> 0) Begin
                            Set pbRequireAllMembers of hoDeps to False
                            Get MemberCount of hoDeps to iDepMax
                            Decrement iDepMax
                            For iDep from 0 to iDepMax
                                Get MemberEnh of hoDeps iDep to hoDep
                                If (hoDep <> 0) Begin
                                    Set pbRequireAllMembers of hoDep to False
                                    Get MemberValueEnh of hoDep "name"    to sOne
                                    Get MemberValueEnh of hoDep "version" to sDepVer
                                    If (Trim(sOne) <> "") Begin
                                        If (sReq <> "") ;
                                            Move (sReq + ", ") to sReq
                                        Move (sReq + (rfHtmlEscape(Trim(sOne)))) to sReq
                                        If (Trim(sDepVer) <> "") ;
                                            Move (sReq + (' ' + (SpanOpen("ml-dim"))) + (rfHtmlEscape(Trim(sDepVer))) + CS_SpanEnd) to sReq
                                    End
                                End
                            Loop
                        End
                    End
                End
            Loop
        End
        Send Destroy of hoJson

        Move "" to sOut
        Move (sOut + (_FactRow(Self, "Publisher", (rfHtmlEscape(sPub))))) to sOut
        Move (sOut + (_FactRow(Self, "Licence",  (_LicenceHtml(Self, sLic))))) to sOut
        Move (sOut + (_FactRow(Self, "DataFlex", (rfHtmlEscape(_DfRangeText(Self, sMin, sMax)))))) to sOut
        Move (sOut + (_FactRow(Self, "Requires", (If(sReq = "", "nothing", sReq))))) to sOut
        Move (sOut + (_FactRow(Self, "This version", (rfHtmlEscape(Trim(sChg)))))) to sOut
        Move ('<table style="border-collapse:collapse;margin:0 0 12px 0;">' + sOut + '</table>') to sOut

        If (Trim(sLong) = "") ;
            Function_Return (sOut + (HelpParagraph("This package publishes no long description.")))

        Move (Replaces((Character(13) + Character(10)), sLong, Character(10))) to sLong
        Move (Replaces(Character(10), (rfHtmlEscape(sLong)), CS_Br)) to sLong
        Function_Return (sOut + (HelpParagraph((BoldText("Description")))) + (HelpParagraph(sLong)))
    End_Function

    /*  THE VERSION A ROW DECLARES ABOUT ITSELF, read from the manifest in its DfPkg checkout.

        A git dependency records a COMMIT, so the .sws cannot say what version moved - only which
        40 characters did. The manifest in the checkout can, when the repository bothered to
        declare one, and that is the number a person recognises: "20.0.0 to 20.1.0" rather than
        "7581c60 to 5088217".

        Called BEFORE and AFTER the upgrade. Both readings come off disk rather than from what was
        asked for, because the after-reading is the only evidence of what df-cli actually installed.
        Returns "" when the repository declares no version, which every caller treats as "then say
        nothing about versions" - never as 0 or unknown-and-therefore-changed. */
    Function _ManifestVersionOf String sSws String sRowKey Returns String
        tSwsDependency[] aObjDeps
        String sHome sMan sKey sVer
        Integer i iMax
        Handle hoInv

        Move "" to sVer
        If (Trim(sSws) = "" or Trim(sRowKey) = "") ;
            Function_Return ""
        Get _FolderOf sSws to sHome
        Get Create (RefClass(cLibraryInventoryApp)) to hoInv
        Get SwsJsonObjectDependencies of hoInv sSws to aObjDeps
        Move (SizeOfArray(aObjDeps) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            Get SwsDependencyKey aObjDeps[i] to sKey
            If (Uppercase(Trim(sKey)) = Uppercase(Trim(sRowKey))) Begin
                Get ObjectDependencyManifest of hoInv sHome aObjDeps[i] to sMan
                If (sMan <> "") ;
                    Get SwsManifestValue of hoInv sMan "version" to sVer
            End
        Loop
        Send Destroy of hoInv
        Function_Return (Trim(sVer))
    End_Function

    /*  WHAT MOVED, in the terms the row actually has (Nils 2026-09-10).

        A registry row knows its versions and has no commit worth showing. A git row always has
        commits and SOMETIMES has versions - only when the repository declares one in its manifest.
        So the two lines are independent and either may be absent; what is never done is inventing
        a version for a row that has none, or printing 40 characters where 12 identify the commit
        as well as git itself does.

        A version line is only printed when the two differ. A git package can move commit without
        changing its declared version - that is the normal case for a repository that never bumps
        it - and "Version: 20.0.0 -> 20.0.0" would read as a failed upgrade rather than a real one. */
    Function _UpgradeReportText String sPkgName Boolean bIsGit String sOldVer String sNewVer ;
                                String sOldPin String sNewPin Returns String
        String sMsg sName
        Integer iPos

        // A registry entry carries its version in the name; the box wants the package, not the pin.
        Move (Trim(sPkgName)) to sName
        Move (Pos((Character(35)), sName)) to iPos
        If (iPos > 1) ;
            Move (Left(sName, (iPos - 1))) to sName

        Move ("Package update succeeded." + Character(10) + Character(10)) to sMsg
        Move (sMsg + sName + Character(10)) to sMsg
        If (sOldVer <> "" and sNewVer <> "" and sOldVer <> sNewVer) ;
            Move (sMsg + Character(10) + "Version went from" * sOldVer * "to" * sNewVer) to sMsg
        /* A TAG pin (2026-09-25) is a version already, so it is said as one - "Commit went from
           1.0.0" would be false - and only when the manifests did not just say the same thing. */
        If (bIsGit = True and sOldPin <> "" and sNewPin <> "") Begin
            If (IsGitCommitPin(sOldPin) = True) ;
                Move (sMsg + Character(10) + "Commit went from" * (Left(sOldPin, 12)) * "to" * (Left(sNewPin, 12))) to sMsg
            Else If ((sOldVer <> sOldPin) or (sNewVer <> sNewPin)) ;
                Move (sMsg + Character(10) + "Pin went from" * sOldPin * "to" * sNewPin) to sMsg
        End
        If (bIsGit = True and (sOldVer = "" or sNewVer = "") and (IsGitCommitPin(sOldPin) = True)) ;
            Move (sMsg + Character(10) + Character(10) + "This repository declares no package version of its own, so the commit is all there is to compare.") to sMsg
        Function_Return sMsg
    End_Function


    Procedure DoUpgradeNamedPackage String sPkgName
        tSwsDependency[] aObjDeps
        String[] asOutput asKey asLatest asRestoreOut
        String sSws sMsg sLatest sOldPin sRowKey sOldVer sNewVer sSource sDepSws sSaid sHint
        Integer i iMax iRetval iLeft
        Boolean bOk bIsGit bPinBack bPinStuck
        Handle hoInv

        Get psSwsFile to sSws
        Move (Trim(sPkgName)) to sPkgName
        If (sSws = "" or sPkgName = "") ;
            Procedure_Return

        /* WHAT are we moving this row TO, and is it a git pin or a system-package version?

           A GIT ROW IS ONE OF THE WORKSPACE'S OBJECT DEPENDENCIES - asked of the file, not read off
           the target (2026-09-25). This used to test for a 40-character target, which was the same
           thing while every git pin was a commit. A git row pinned to a version TAG moves to a tag,
           and read as a registry package it would have gone to `package upgrade`, which takes the
           newest release there is - measured: a "1.0.0" pin straight to 2.0.0, past what the
           workspace's libraries accept.

           df-cli's `package upgrade` CANNOT move a git dependency pinned to a commit: asked to, it
           answers "Library <name> is up to date." and exits 0, because the pin IS the requested
           version and there is no range to resolve (measured 2026-08-21 - the button appeared to
           work, disabled itself, and changed nothing). A git row therefore has its pin REWRITTEN
           here, commit or tag alike, and df-cli is then asked to reconcile the checkout to it. */
        Get pasUpdKey    to asKey
        Get pasUpdLatest to asLatest
        Move "" to sLatest
        Move (SizeOfArray(asKey) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            If (Uppercase(Trim(asKey[i])) = Uppercase(sPkgName)) Begin
                Move (Trim(asLatest[i])) to sLatest
                Move (Trim(asKey[i]))    to sRowKey
            End
        Loop
        Move False to bIsGit
        If (sRowKey <> "") Begin
            Get Create (RefClass(cLibraryInventoryApp)) to hoInv
            Get SwsJsonObjectDependencies of hoInv sSws to aObjDeps
            Send Destroy of hoInv
            Move (SizeOfArray(aObjDeps) - 1) to iMax
            For i from 0 to iMax
                If (Uppercase(Trim(SwsDependencyKey(aObjDeps[i]))) = Uppercase(sRowKey)) ;
                    Move True to bIsGit
            Loop
        End
        If (DfCliPath() = "") Begin
            Send FTStopBox "No df-cli.exe was found beside the selected compiler, and it is what upgrades a package." "df-cli not available"
            Procedure_Return
        End

        Get FTYesNoBox ("Upgrade" * sPkgName + " to its newest version?" + Character(10) + Character(10) + "df-cli rewrites the dependency and installs the new version now. Cancel will not put the old one back - re-adding it by version would.") "Upgrade package" MB_DEFBUTTON2 to iRetval
        If (iRetval <> MBR_Yes) ;
            Procedure_Return

        Get pbDirty to bOk
        If (bOk = True) Begin
            Get SaveEntries to bOk
            If (bOk = False) ;
                Procedure_Return
        End

        /* WHAT THIS ROW IS NOW, captured before anything moves - there is no reading it afterwards.
           A git row is asked of its manifest; a registry row carries its version in the entry. */
        If (bIsGit = True) ;
            Get _ManifestVersionOf sSws sRowKey to sOldVer
        Else Begin
            Get Create (RefClass(cLibraryInventoryApp)) to hoInv
            Get PackageVersion of hoInv sPkgName to sOldVer
            Send Destroy of hoInv
        End

        Move False to bPinStuck
        If (bIsGit = True) Begin
            /* GIT: rewrite the pin, then have df-cli make the checkout agree with it. The old pin
               is read from the file rather than remembered, so a workspace edited elsewhere since
               the check cannot be repointed from a stale value - and the entry is named by its
               SOURCE and .sws, so a tag two packages share ("1.0.0") moves only this one. */
            Get Create (RefClass(cLibraryInventoryApp)) to hoInv
            Get SwsJsonObjectDependencies of hoInv sSws to aObjDeps
            Move "" to sOldPin
            Move (SizeOfArray(aObjDeps) - 1) to iMax
            For i from 0 to iMax
                Get _PackageKeyOf of (oWebHost(Self)) aObjDeps[i] to sMsg
                If (Uppercase(Trim(sMsg)) = Uppercase(sRowKey)) Begin
                    Move (Trim(aObjDeps[i].sVersion)) to sOldPin
                    Move (Trim(aObjDeps[i].sSource))  to sSource
                    Move (Trim(aObjDeps[i].sSws))     to sDepSws
                End
            Loop
            Move False to bOk
            If (sOldPin <> "") ;
                Get SetSwsDependencyVersion of hoInv sSws sSource sDepSws sOldPin sLatest to bOk
            Send Destroy of hoInv
            If (bOk = False) Begin
                Send FTStopBox ("The pin for " + sPkgName + " could not be rewritten, so nothing was changed." + Character(10) + Character(10) + "The dependency's current pin was not found in the workspace file - it may have been edited since the last Update Check. Run Update Check again.") "Package not upgraded"
                Send ReloadEntriesFromFile
                Send RefreshBody
                Procedure_Return
            End
            // The pins are now what the user asked for; this makes the installed set match them.
            Get DfCliPackageReconcile sSws sPkgName (&asOutput) to bOk
            /* A FAILED RECONCILE PUTS THE OLD PIN BACK (2026-09-25). The box below says this
               workspace is unchanged, and until now that was not true: the file kept the new pin
               df-cli had just refused. On a package that libraries ask for by range that is a
               BROKEN workspace (measured: a tag pin their range refused -> "Incompatible ref",
               exit 15), so the old pin goes back and df-cli is asked once more to make the checkout
               match it - measured to bring that same workspace back to OK. */
            If (bOk = False) Begin
                Get Create (RefClass(cLibraryInventoryApp)) to hoInv
                Get SetSwsDependencyVersion of hoInv sSws sSource sDepSws sLatest sOldPin to bPinBack
                Send Destroy of hoInv
                If (bPinBack = True) ;
                    Get DfCliPackageReconcile sSws sPkgName (&asRestoreOut) to bPinBack
                Else ;
                    Move True to bPinStuck
            End
        End
        Else ;
            Get DfCliPackageUpgrade sSws sPkgName (&asOutput) to bOk
        If (bOk = False) Begin
            If (bPinStuck = True) ;
                Move ("The package was NOT upgraded, and the workspace file could not be put back: it still pins" * sLatest * "where it pinned" * sOldPin + ". Change it back before you build." + Character(10) + Character(10) + "df-cli said:") to sMsg
            Else ;
                Move ("The package was NOT upgraded and this workspace is unchanged." + Character(10) + Character(10) + "df-cli said:") to sMsg
            Get DfCliOutputNote (&asOutput) 15 to sSaid
            Move (sMsg + sSaid) to sMsg
            Get DfCliIncompatibleRefHint (&asOutput) to sHint
            If (sHint <> "") ;
                Move (sMsg + Character(10) + Character(10) + sHint) to sMsg
            Send FTStopBox sMsg "Package not upgraded"
        End
        Else Begin
            /* WHAT LANDED, read back rather than assumed. For a git row the .sws now names the new
               commit and DfPkg holds its checkout, so the manifest there is the new version; for a
               registry row df-cli installed the version the check named. */
            If (bIsGit = True) ;
                Get _ManifestVersionOf sSws sRowKey to sNewVer
            Else ;
                Move sLatest to sNewVer
        End
        /* RE-READ whichever way it went - df-cli owns the file now. The check result is dropped
           with it: the dependency array just moved, so every "newer" answer is stale and the
           chips must go back to "Run Update Check first" rather than offer a second upgrade. */
        Send ReloadEntriesFromFile
        Send RefreshBody
        /* RESTATE THE TILE COUNT, which the reload above has just cleared.

           This path knows what a general invalidation cannot: exactly one row moved, and it was
           one of the rows the check had counted. So the remainder is the count it found minus
           one - no second scan, no network. Upgrading the only behind package leaves 0, and the
           tile turns from "1 package needs an update" to "Packages up to date"; upgrading one of
           three leaves 2, which is the true answer and not a guess.

           asKey is the check result read at the top of this procedure, so it survives the reload
           regardless of what that clears. Floored at 0: a chip cannot be clicked without a check
           behind it, but a count that went negative would render as an update that does not exist.

           bAnswered stays True - something did answer, and 0 behind is a real answer here. */
        If (bOk = True) Begin
            Move (SizeOfArray(asKey) - 1) to iLeft
            If (iLeft < 0) ;
                Move 0 to iLeft
            Send SetUpdateResult of ghoLibraryReport sSws iLeft True
        End
        // Said AFTER the repaint, so the box stands in front of a list that already agrees with it.
        If (bOk = True) ;
            Send FTInfoBox (_UpgradeReportText(Self, sPkgName, bIsGit, sOldVer, sNewVer, sOldPin, sLatest)) "Package updated"
    End_Procedure

    Procedure DoUninstallNamedPackage String sPkgName
        String[] asOutput
        String sSws sMsg sSaid
        Integer iRetval
        Boolean bOk

        Get psSwsFile to sSws
        Move (Trim(sPkgName)) to sPkgName
        If (sSws = "" or sPkgName = "") ;
            Procedure_Return
        If (DfCliPath() = "") Begin
            Send FTStopBox "No df-cli.exe was found beside the selected compiler, and it is what uninstalls a package." "df-cli not available"
            Procedure_Return
        End
        Get FTYesNoBox ("Uninstall" * sPkgName + " from this workspace?" + Character(10) + Character(10) + "df-cli removes the dependency and the package itself. This happens at once - Cancel will not put it back.") "Uninstall package" MB_DEFBUTTON2 to iRetval
        If (iRetval <> MBR_Yes) ;
            Procedure_Return

        Get pbDirty to bOk
        If (bOk = True) Begin
            Get SaveEntries to bOk
            If (bOk = False) ;
                Procedure_Return
        End

        Get DfCliPackageUninstall sSws sPkgName (&asOutput) to bOk
        If (bOk = False) Begin
            Move ("The package was NOT uninstalled and this workspace is unchanged." + Character(10) + Character(10) + "df-cli said:") to sMsg
            Get DfCliOutputNote (&asOutput) 15 to sSaid
            Move (sMsg + sSaid) to sMsg
            Send FTStopBox sMsg "Package not uninstalled"
        End
        // RE-READ whichever way it went - df-cli owns the file now.
        Send ReloadEntriesFromFile
        Send RefreshBody
    End_Procedure

    // DF 26+ ONLY - every line below goes through df-cli. Guarded with its buttons and dispatch (Nils 2026-08-19).
    /* BROWSE DATAFLEX'S PACKAGES - the half the Studio's Add dialog does not have, kept because it does
       something the one field cannot: answer "what is there?". Same gates as Add Package, and the
       same install path once something is picked. */
    Procedure DoSearchPackages
        tPackageHit[] aHits aShow
        String[] asMine
        String sSws sTerm sHtml sHead sPick sKey sMineKey sPickName
        Integer i iMax iFormat iAt iMineMax iHidden iPos
        Boolean bMine
        Handle hoInv hoInvP

        Get psSwsFile to sSws
        If (sSws = "") ;
            Procedure_Return
        Get Create (RefClass(cLibraryInventoryApp)) to hoInv
        Get SwsFormatOf of hoInv sSws to iFormat
        Send Destroy of hoInv
        // Convert first, then browse - the same door as Add Package... (Nils 2026-09-03).
        If (iFormat <> eSwsJson) Begin
            Send _ConvertFirstFor CI_MLDeferSearchPkg
            Procedure_Return
        End
        If (DfCliPath() = "") Begin
            Send FTStopBox "No df-cli.exe was found beside the selected compiler, and it is what searches DataFlex's packages." "df-cli not available"
            Procedure_Return
        End

        /* NO SEARCH PROMPT ANY MORE (Nils 2026-08-15: "directly show the list of existing
           packages ... I think that would be more logical"). The package list a workspace can draw on
           is a couple of dozen packages, not thousands - a term prompt in front of a list that
           size was a door in front of a door. Blank asks df-cli for everything compatible. */
        Move "" to sTerm
        Get DfCliPackageSearch sSws sTerm to aHits
        /* ONLY WHAT IS NOT ALREADY HERE (Nils 2026-08-15: "The list should only show unused
           packages"). A hit whose publisher/package matches a dependency this dialog is already
           editing is not something to install - offering it re-installs at best. Compared against
           the WORKING list (pasEntries), so a package added a moment ago drops out before OK.
           Matched on the string form with the #version stripped; a git OBJECT dependency has a
           source url rather than a package name, so it cannot be matched and is not tried.
           The count of hidden hits is SAID below - a silently shorter list reads as the whole
           DataFlex's package list (the no-silent-shortening rule this dialog already follows). */
        Get pasEntries to asMine
        Move (SizeOfArray(asMine) - 1) to iMineMax
        Move (SizeOfArray(aHits) - 1) to iMax
        Move 0 to iHidden
        For i from 0 to iMax
            Move False to bMine
            Move (Trim(aHits[i].sName)) to sKey
            For iAt from 0 to iMineMax
                Move (Trim(asMine[iAt])) to sMineKey
                Move (Pos("#", sMineKey)) to iPos
                If (iPos > 1) ;
                    Move (Left(sMineKey, iPos - 1)) to sMineKey
                If (Lowercase(Trim(sMineKey)) = Lowercase(sKey)) ;
                    Move True to bMine
            Loop
            If (bMine = True) ;
                Increment iHidden
            Else ;
                Move aHits[i] to aShow[-1]
        Loop
        Move aShow to aHits
        Move (SizeOfArray(aHits) - 1) to iMax
        If (iMax < 0) Begin
            If (iHidden > 0) Begin
                Send FTInfoBox ("Every DataFlex Package Manager package available to this workspace - " + String(iHidden) + " - is already installed in it. There is nothing new to add.") "Nothing to add"
                Procedure_Return
            End
            Send FTInfoBox ("No packages matched" * (If(Trim(sTerm) = "", "this workspace", '"' + sTerm + '"')) + "." + Character(10) + Character(10) + "DataFlex Package Manager packages are searched for ones compatible with the DataFlex version this workspace declares, so a workspace on an older version will find nothing.") "No packages found"
            Procedure_Return
        End


        /* THE PICK LIST, as body links rather than a control: ConfirmDialogFT's named body action
           (cfmact:) already ends the dialog and hands back which one was clicked, which is exactly
           the shape needed here - and it is the mechanism built for the Libraries report's own door
           out. The chosen package rides in the name as "<repo>/<Name>#<version>", which is both the
           install argument and the dependency entry df-cli will write. */
        /* THE NOTES ARE PINNED, NOT SCROLLED (Nils 2026-08-15, round three: "should be its own
           text control above the list ... the text is not visible when scrolling the list - and
           it should be"). psHeaderHtml renders in the dialog's fixed head band, so the what-
           happens note and the hidden-count line stay put while the list scrolls beneath them;
           the click-to-install instruction rides the chip band beside Close (psFooterNote). */
        // Main ink, not muted (Nils: the head texts were "hard to read").
        Move ('<p style="color:var(--dfr-ink, #eceae1);font-size:12.5px;">df-cli downloads and installs the package, then records it in this workspace. DFRefactor does not fetch anything itself.' + CS_ParaEnd) to sHead
        If (iHidden > 0) ;
            Move (sHead + '<p style="color:var(--dfr-ink, #eceae1);font-size:12.5px;">' + String(iHidden) + ' package' + (If(iHidden = 1, " that is", "s that are")) + ' already in this workspace ' + (If(iHidden = 1, "is", "are")) + (' not shown.' + CS_ParaEnd)) to sHead
        /* CARDS, NOT ROWS (Nils 2026-08-22: "comparing with the Browse packages dialog, all items
           instead looks like a long list"). The Libraries dialog gives every entry its own bordered
           tile - .ml-card / .ml-pk - and this list, showing the same KIND of thing, was a bare
           <table>. The two now share a shape as well as a vocabulary; .bpcard is .ml-pk's box. */
        Move "" to sHtml
        /* ITS OWN inventory object: the hoInv created at the top of this procedure was destroyed
           immediately after the format check, ~85 lines above. Reaching for it here would be error
           98 at RUNTIME and compile perfectly - the same shape as the helper-destroyed-before-render
           bug this codebase has already paid for once. */
        Get Create (RefClass(cLibraryInventoryApp)) to hoInvP
        For i from 0 to iMax
            /* THE TITLE IS THE PACKAGE, NOT "publisher/package" (Nils 2026-08-21). Every row in
               this list came from the same package service, so the prefix repeated on all of them and
               bought nothing; the publisher belongs on the second line, where the installed cards
               already put it. PackageName strips both the "<publisher>/" head and any "#version"
               tail, and falls back to the whole string so a row is never nameless.

               The second line mimics an installed card: the person glyph then the publisher, the
               layers glyph then the version - the same two marks, in the same order, so the two
               lists read as one vocabulary. Glyphs as TEXT, not assets, for the reason the card
               comments give: an <img> cannot take the CSS colour. */
            /* THE TITLE IS THE PACKAGE, NOT "publisher/package" (Nils 2026-08-21). Every row came
               from the same publisher list, so the prefix repeated on all of them and bought nothing.
               PackageName strips the "<publisher>/" head and any "#version" tail, falling back to
               the whole string so a row is never nameless.

               ORDER: name, description, then publisher + version - description ABOVE the meta line,
               which is the Libraries card's own order. The meta line carries the same two marks in
               the same order as a card: person then publisher, layers then version.

               THE ROW NO LONGER INSTALLS ON CLICK. An Install button in the last column does it -
               the column that used to repeat the version, which the meta line now says. A list where
               clicking anywhere installs something is one mis-click from an install nobody asked
               for; a button is aimed. */
            Get PackageName of hoInvP aHits[i].sName to sPickName
            If (Trim(sPickName) = "") ;
                Move (Trim(aHits[i].sName)) to sPickName
            Move (sHtml + ((DivOpen("bpcard")) + (DivOpen("bpct")))) to sHtml
            Move (sHtml + (DivOpen("bpn")) + sPickName + CS_DivEnd) to sHtml
            If (Trim(aHits[i].sDescription) <> "") ;
                Move (sHtml + (DivOpen("bpd")) + aHits[i].sDescription + CS_DivEnd) to sHtml
            Move (sHtml + (DivOpen("bpm"))) to sHtml
            Move (sHtml + (((SpanOpenTip("", "Publisher")) + (SpanOpen("bpgl")) + CS_PublisherGlyph) + CS_SpanEnd) + aHits[i].sPublisher + CS_SpanEnd) to sHtml
            Move (sHtml + (((SpanOpenTip("", "Version")) + (SpanOpen("bpgl")) + CS_VersionGlyph) + CS_SpanEnd) + aHits[i].sVersion + CS_SpanEnd) to sHtml
            Move (sHtml + (CS_DivEnd + CS_DivEnd)) to sHtml
            Move (sHtml + (ButtonHtml("span", "act", ('pick|' + aHits[i].sName + '#' + aHits[i].sVersion), "", "Install", ('Install ' + sPickName + ' ' + aHits[i].sVersion + ' into this workspace.'), True)) + CS_DivEnd) to sHtml
        Loop
        Send Destroy of hoInvP
        /* ITS OWN DIALOG (2026-08-16). This list used to ride the shared confirm dialog through
           psHeaderHtml / psFooterNote / body-action links - the last caller keeping that dialog
           two-shaped. BrowsePackagesFT is a picker and nothing else: header, click-rows, Close;
           ShowPicker hands back the clicked key ("" for Close / Esc). */
        /* NO FOOTER NOTE (Nils 2026-08-21). It read "Click a package to install it into this
           workspace" - which stopped being true when the rows got their own Install button, and
           the button says what it does anyway. ShowPicker keeps the parameter; it simply has
           nothing to say here, and the band omits the note when it is blank. */
        Get ShowPicker of ghoBrowsePackages "Browse packages" sHead sHtml "" to sPick
        If (sPick = "") ;
            Procedure_Return
        Send InstallChosenPackage sPick
    End_Procedure

    /* UNINSTALL a package through df-cli - the mirror of DoAddPackage, and committed for the same
       reason: df-cli rewrites the dependency array on disk, so our copy has to be saved first and
       re-read after or OK would put the package back.

       ASKED FIRST, unlike removing a library. A library row leaves the list and nothing happens
       until OK, so Cancel undoes it; this deletes an installed package from the workspace the
       moment it is confirmed. The confirmation says which, and defaults to No.

       A REFUSAL IS INFORMATION. df-cli declines when another package depends on this one, and its
       own words name the dependent - so they are shown rather than replaced with a summary. No
       --force: see DfCliPackageUninstall. */
    Procedure DoUninstallPackage String sEntry
        String[] asOutput
        String sSws sName sMsg sSaid
        Integer iRetval
        Boolean bOk
        Handle hoInv

        Get psSwsFile to sSws
        If (sSws = "" or sEntry = "") ;
            Procedure_Return
        If (DfCliPath() = "") Begin
            Send FTStopBox ("This entry is a DataFlex package, and removing one means uninstalling it - which df-cli does. No df-cli.exe was found beside the selected compiler." + Character(10) + Character(10) + "Taking the entry out of the list by hand would leave the package installed and its dependencies in place, so this stops instead. Choose a DataFlex 26 compiler in Target version..., or uninstall it from the Studio.") "df-cli not available"
            Procedure_Return
        End

        Get Create (RefClass(cLibraryInventoryApp)) to hoInv
        Get PackageName of hoInv sEntry to sName
        Send Destroy of hoInv
        If (sName = "") ;
            Move sEntry to sName

        Get FTYesNoBox ("Uninstall the package " + sName + " from this workspace?" + Character(10) + Character(10) + "df-cli removes it and its entry from the workspace file. Unlike removing a library, this happens NOW - Cancel will not put it back. Any other changes you have made to the list are saved first." + Character(10) + Character(10) + "Uninstall it?") "Uninstall package" MB_DEFBUTTON2 to iRetval
        If (iRetval <> MBR_Yes) ;
            Procedure_Return

        // Commit our own edits before handing the file over - same order as DoAddPackage.
        Get SaveEntries to bOk
        If (bOk = False) ;
            Procedure_Return

        Get DfCliPackageUninstall sSws sEntry (&asOutput) to bOk
        If (bOk = False) Begin
            Move ("The package was NOT uninstalled and this workspace is unchanged." + Character(10) + Character(10) + "df-cli said:") to sMsg
            Get DfCliOutputNote (&asOutput) 15 to sSaid
            Move (sMsg + sSaid) to sMsg
            Move (sMsg + Character(10) + Character(10) + "If another package depends on this one, uninstall that first - the check is deliberate, and DFRefactor does not override it.") to sMsg
            Send FTStopBox sMsg "Package not uninstalled"
        End

        Send ReloadEntriesFromFile
        Send RefreshBody
    End_Procedure

    // Discard the working list and take the file's, with nothing pending. Used after df-cli has
    // written the dependency list itself.
    Procedure ReloadEntriesFromFile
        String[] asEntries
        String sSws
        Handle hoInv

        /* THE UPDATE-CHECK ANSWER DIES WITH THE OLD LIST. Every path that re-reads the file -
           install, uninstall, upgrade, add, change - lands here, so invalidating once here
           covers all eight callers; doing it at one call site would leave the other seven
           showing "Update" chips computed against a dependency array that no longer exists.

           THE TILE HOLDS THE SAME ANSWER and was not being told (Nils 2026-09-10: after an
           upgrade it still said "1 package needs an update"). The chips going dead was never
           enough - the count on the Libraries tile came from the workspace-open scan and had no
           other way to learn the array had moved. Cleared here for the same eight callers and
           the same reason; an upgrade restates it below, because it alone knows what changed. */
        Set pbUpdateChecked to False
        Send InvalidateUpdateResult of ghoLibraryReport

        Get psSwsFile to sSws
        If (sSws = "") ;
            Procedure_Return
        Get Create (RefClass(cLibraryInventoryApp)) to hoInv
        Get SwsLibraryEntries of hoInv sSws to asEntries
        Send Destroy of hoInv
        Set pasEntries to asEntries
        Set piSelected to -1
        Set pbDirty to False
    End_Procedure

    /* FIND USAGES - which of this workspace's own source files Use each library.

       ON DEMAND, never on open: it reads every source file in scope, which is Deep-Scan-sized work.
       The answer is what turns "no 26.0 build - needs a newer copy" into a decision, because 3 files
       and 300 are different projects.

       SCANNED AGAINST THE FOUND LIBRARIES ONLY. Usage is matched by a library's package names, read
       off its folder - an entry pointing at nothing has no folder to read, so it has no usage count
       either, and pretending otherwise would report zero dependants for a library the workspace may
       lean on heavily.

       THE WORKING LIST, not the file: a library the user has just added is scanned, and one they
       have just removed is not - which is the whole point of asking here rather than in a report.

       Cancelled leaves the previous answer standing (a half-finished count is worse than none). The
       results live on ghoLibraryReport, which owns the scan and the cell renderer; this dialog only
       asks for them and repaints. */
    Procedure DoFindUsages
        tLibraryInfo[] aInfo
        tLibraryUsage[] aUse
        String[] asEntries asSeen
        String sSws sHome sTarget
        Boolean bCancelled
        Handle hoInv

        Get psSwsFile to sSws
        Get pasEntries to asEntries
        If (sSws = "" or SizeOfArray(asEntries) = 0) ;
            Procedure_Return
        Get _FolderOf sSws to sHome
        Get psTargetDfVersion of ghoApplication to sTarget

        // The WHOLE tree, so the indented rows get a usage count too. "How much of my code cares"
        // is a fair question about a library a library brought in - arguably a sharper one, since
        // that is the dependency the user did not choose.
        Get Create (RefClass(cLibraryInventoryApp)) to hoInv
        Get LibraryEntriesTree of hoInv (&asEntries) sHome sTarget 0 CI_MLMaxChainDepth (&asSeen) to aInfo
        Send Destroy of hoInv

        Get ScanLibraryUsages of ghoLibraryReport (&aInfo) (&bCancelled) to aUse
        If (bCancelled = True) ;
            Procedure_Return
        Set pUsages      of ghoLibraryReport to aUse
        Set pbHaveUsages of ghoLibraryReport to True
        Send RefreshBody
    End_Procedure

    /* OK - back up, then rewrite the [Libraries] section and nothing else.

       A CLEAN LIST CLOSES WITHOUT WRITING. Rewriting a file the user did not change would back it
       up, restamp it, and leave a diff on a workspace file for no reason - and in the migration flow
       that would happen every single time the dialog is shown and dismissed.

       A FAILED WRITE IS THE ONLY THING SPOKEN ALOUD. Success needs no dialog (the list on screen is
       the result), but a refusal must never look like a save - the realistic cause is a JSON-format
       .sws, which the writer declines by design rather than corrupting, and the user has to be told
       that their edit did not land. */
    /* Writes the working list to the .sws and reports whether it landed. Extracted from DoOk so
       that adding a package - which hands the file to df-cli, and must not leave our edits behind
       to be overwritten - can commit first through exactly the same path. A clean list is a no-op
       and answers True: there is nothing to write and nothing went wrong. */
    Function SaveEntries Returns Boolean
        String[] asEntries
        String sSws sMsg
        Boolean bOk bDirty
        Handle hoInv
        Integer iFormat

        Get psSwsFile to sSws
        Get pbDirty to bDirty
        If (sSws = "" or bDirty = False) ;
            Function_Return True

        Get pasEntries to asEntries
        // NO BACKUP OF THE .sws IS TAKEN, deliberately (2026-08-26). This used to call SaveBackupFile
        // first, and the copy it left in DFRefactor_Backup was actively dangerous: the Revert dialog
        // lists everything under that folder, "select all" therefore ticked the .sws too, and Revert
        // MOVES a backup back rather than copying it. So one routine revert - the ordinary way to
        // rewind a round of refactoring - would silently restore the whole library set, putting
        // removed libraries and packages back as source to compile. The .sws is workspace
        // configuration, not refactored source, and does not belong in a folder that is restored
        // wholesale. The cost is accepted: a library change made here has no in-tool undo.
        Get Create (RefClass(cLibraryInventoryApp)) to hoInv
        Get SwsFormatOf of hoInv sSws to iFormat
        Get WriteSwsLibraryEntries of hoInv sSws asEntries to bOk
        Send Destroy of hoInv

        If (bOk = False) Begin
            Move ("The library list was NOT saved - " + sSws + " is unchanged." + Character(10) + Character(10)) to sMsg
            /* NAME THE LIKELY CAUSE per format, and NOTHING THIS TOOL CHOSE TO DO (2026-08-14).

               This used to explain that the write had been DECLINED because a dependency was a git
               or cloud package - which was accurate, and useless: it told the user their edit could
               never be saved, in a dialog whose entire purpose is saving it. The JSON writer now
               carries object dependencies through untouched, so that is no longer a cause and the
               text no longer claims it. What is left is genuine I/O failure, and the two formats
               fail for the same reasons - the JSON note only adds where to look. */
            Move (sMsg + "The file could not be written. It may be open in the Studio, read-only, or on a drive that is no longer reachable.") to sMsg
            If (iFormat = eSwsJson) ;
                Move (sMsg + Character(10) + Character(10) + "A workspace taken from the package cache is installed READ-ONLY by df-cli, which is the usual reason on a DataFlex 26 workspace.") to sMsg
            Send FTStopBox sMsg "Libraries not saved"
            Function_Return False
        End
        Set pbDirty to False
        Function_Return True
    End_Function

    Procedure DoOk
        Boolean bOk

        Get SaveEntries to bOk
        If (bOk = False) ;
            Procedure_Return
        // The folders this showing stopped using are offered once the dialog has closed, whichever
        // way it closed - see OfferRetireAfterClose, sent by MaintainWorkspaceLibraries.
        Set pbConfirmed to True
        Send Close_Panel
    End_Procedure


    Procedure DoCancel
        Set pbConfirmed to False
        Send Close_Panel
    End_Procedure

    /* FT: the dialog's own deferred work, on a native tick off the WebView2 pump - the repaint a Popup arms (the
       control is WARM on a re-open, where a bare Set is inert), the conversion offer after the close, and the
       convert-then-resume chain, which re-arms itself from a message box's return path (see _ConvertFirstFor).
       The page's clicks run in oMLPage's OnHtmlClick, after the web click has returned (the library's click
       relay), since 2026-10-07. */
    Object oMLDeferTimer is a cFTDeferTimer
        Procedure OnDeferredAction Integer iAction String sParam
            If (iAction = CI_MLDeferAutoUpdChk) ;
                Delegate Send DoUpdateCheckEx True
            // Every package action goes through df-cli, which is DF 26+ - see the Add Package button.
            If (iAction = CI_MLDeferSearchPkg) ;
                Delegate Send DoSearchPackages
            If (iAction = CI_MLDeferConvertOffer) ;
                Delegate Send DoOfferConvertAfterClose sParam
            If (iAction = CI_MLDeferAddPkg) ;
                Delegate Send DoAddPackage
            If (iAction = CI_MLDeferConvertResume) ;
                Delegate Send DoConvertThenResume
            If (iAction = CI_MLDeferUnresolvedOffer) ;
                Delegate Send DoOfferUnresolvedFix
            If (iAction = CI_MLDeferRetryInstall) ;
                Delegate Send DoRetryInstallWithoutLockPin sParam
            If (iAction = CI_MLDeferRefresh) Begin
                Delegate Send RefreshBody
                // AFTER the paint, never before: the check can take seconds and the user should be
                // looking at the list while it runs, not at nothing.
                Delegate Send ArmAutoUpdateCheck
            End
        End_Procedure
    End_Object

    /* Every Popup starts from the FILE, not from whatever the last showing left behind. The dialog
       object outlives one migration; a stale list would offer the previous workspace's libraries for
       writing into this one's .sws. */
    Procedure Popup
        String[] asEntries
        tLibraryUsage[] aNoUse
        String sSws
        Handle hoInv

        Set pbConfirmed to False
        Set pbDirty to False
        Set piSelected to -1
        Set pbAutoUpdChkDone to False
        /* USAGE COUNTS DO NOT SURVIVE A CLOSE. They describe source that a refactoring run may have
           rewritten since, and a stale count reads exactly like a fresh one - so every opening asks
           again rather than showing an answer it can no longer stand behind. (The same rule the
           report followed; it is the reason these live on ghoLibraryReport as per-showing state
           rather than as a cache.) */
        Set pUsages      of ghoLibraryReport to aNoUse
        Set pbHaveUsages of ghoLibraryReport to False
        Get psSwsFile to sSws
        If (sSws <> "") Begin
            Get Create (RefClass(cLibraryInventoryApp)) to hoInv
            Get SwsLibraryEntries of hoInv sSws to asEntries
            Send Destroy of hoInv
        End
        Set pasEntries to asEntries
        Set pasOpenEntries to asEntries
        // Arm the repaint rather than Set psHtml: a re-open finds the control WARM, where a bare Set
        // is inert. The timer fires inside the modal pump, once the panel is up.
        Send ArmAction of (oMLDeferTimer(Self)) CI_MLDeferRefresh
        Forward Send Popup
    End_Procedure

    On_Key Key_Alt+Key_A Send DoAdd
    On_Key Key_Alt+Key_R Send DoRemove
    On_Key Key_Alt+Key_C Send DoCancel
End_Object

/*  MAIN ACCESS METHOD - the one way to open Library Maintenance on a workspace file.

    Takes the .sws EXPLICITLY rather than reading psSWSFile, because its first caller edits a
    workspace that is not open: RunWorkspaceCopy shows this for the copy it has just written, before
    offering to load it. Pass "" to mean "the loaded workspace" and it resolves that itself.

    Returns True when OK was pressed - which is not the same as "the file changed": an unedited list
    saves nothing and still returns True. Callers that need to report a change should compare the
    entries themselves.

        Get MaintainWorkspaceLibraries sNewSws to bOk

    Global, so both the copy flow (a different .dg) and the command-bar client can reach it. */
Function MaintainWorkspaceLibraries Global String sSwsFile Returns Boolean
    Handle ho
    Boolean bOk

    Move (Trim(sSwsFile)) to sSwsFile
    If (sSwsFile = "") ;
        Get psSWSFile of ghoApplication to sSwsFile
    If (sSwsFile = "" or vFilePathExists(sSwsFile) = 0) Begin
        Send FTStopBox "There is no workspace file to maintain libraries for. Open a workspace first." "No workspace"
        Function_Return False
    End

    /* BOTH .sws FORMATS ARE READ HERE (2026-08-12). The up-front JSON refusal that stood here is
       gone: cLibraryInventoryApp answers SwsLibraryEntries from the "dependencies" array of a JSON
       workspace and from [Libraries] of an INI one, and the choice is made PER FILE - which is what
       a mixed workspace needs, and mixed is the normal case. The DF26 Studio converts the workspace
       it migrates and leaves the library .sws files alone, so a JSON workspace routinely declares
       libraries that are still INI, and each is read the way it is written.

       What can still refuse is the WRITE, and it says so at the point of saving rather than
       forbidding the whole screen - looking at the list was never the dangerous half. */
    /* THROUGH THE CLIENT AREA, which the dialog is a child of (2026-11-19). oMaintainLibraries_dg(Self) found it only
       for a caller inside the client area - the dashboards, the copy flow; from oMain (Workspace > Libraries in the
       menu bar, and Alt+B) it found nothing, and the next line was error 98 "Invalid message SET_PSSWSFILE". */
    Move (oMaintainLibraries_dg(Client_Id(ghoCommandBars))) to ho
    Set psSwsFile of ho to sSwsFile
    Send Popup of ho
    /* THE MODAL HAS CLOSED - by OK, Cancel, Alt+C or the title bar, all of which return here. The
       library folders this showing stopped using are offered now, once, whichever way it closed
       (Nils 2026-09-11): a package added, updated or uninstalled has written the file whatever
       button ended the showing. See OfferRetireAfterClose. */
    Send OfferRetireAfterClose of ho
    // ...and the conversion, while the file is still INI - a tick later, see DoOfferConvertAfterClose.
    Send ArmAction of (oMLDeferTimer(ho)) CI_MLDeferConvertOffer sSwsFile
    Get pbConfirmed of ho to bOk
    Function_Return bOk
End_Function
